(window.UPANISHAD_DATA=window.UPANISHAD_DATA||{})["mundaka"]={"slug":"mundaka","name":"Muṇḍaka","deva":"मुण्डक","note":"","intro":["Three muṇḍakas · six khaṇḍas · 64 mantras — Atharva-Veda, Śaunaka-śākhā<br>Devanāgarī · IAST · Pada-by-Pada Gloss · Literal · Paraphrase · Five Commentaries","Sanskrit text courtesy of sanskritdocuments.org (Śaunaka recension, the one Śaṅkara comments upon). English glosses cross-checked against Śaṅkara's bhāṣya (Gambhirananda 1958), Hume (1921), Radhakrishnan (1953), and Olivelle (1998). Grammar abbreviations follow Whitney / Macdonell."],"sections":[{"t":"Introduction","u":[{"ref":"1. About the Muṇḍaka Upaniṣad","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["The Muṇḍaka Upaniṣad belongs to the Atharva-Veda. Within the corpus of principal Upaniṣads (the daśopaniṣad commented upon by Śaṅkara), it is grouped with two other Atharvan Upaniṣads — the Praśna and the Māṇḍūkya — that share its peace-invocation (\"oṃ bhadraṃ karṇebhiḥ\"). Of these three, the Muṇḍaka is the most poetic: nearly the whole text is in the elevated tṛṣṭubh metre, with the exception of the prose colophons and a handful of anuṣṭubh ślokas inserted as summary-verses.","Its standing within Vedānta is disproportionate to its length. Of all the principal Upaniṣads it contains the densest concentration of mantras that the later tradition would treat as standalone aphorisms: parā cāparā ca (the two knowledges, 1.1.4), tad-vijñānārthaṃ sa gurum evābhigacchet samit-pāṇiḥ (the samit-pāṇi rule, 1.2.12), praṇavo dhanuḥ śaro hi ātmā (the bow-arrow meditation, 2.2.4), dvā suparṇā sayujā sakhāyā (the two birds on one tree, 3.1.1), satyam eva jayate (\"truth alone triumphs,\" 3.1.6 — the motto of the Republic of India), nāyam ātmā pravacanena labhyo (the ātman not gained by exposition, 3.2.3), and yathā nadyaḥ syandamānāḥ samudre (rivers flowing to the ocean, 3.2.8).","The frame is a teaching transmitted through five generations. Brahmā the Self-born taught brahmavidyā to his eldest son Atharvan; Atharvan taught Aṅgir; Aṅgir taught Bhāradvāja Satyavāha; Satyavāha taught Aṅgiras; and at the point the Upaniṣad opens, the great householder Śaunaka approaches Aṅgiras with the foundational question — kasmin nu bhagavo vijñāte sarvam idaṃ vijñātaṃ bhavati? (\"O blessed one, by knowing what does all this become known?\") Aṅgiras's answer is the entire Upaniṣad.","The doctrinal core is the parā–aparā distinction. Aparā vidyā is everything that can be said: the four Vedas, the six aṅgas (Śikṣā, Kalpa, Vyākaraṇa, Nirukta, Chandas, Jyotiṣa). Parā vidyā is the knowing by which That-which-is-indestructible (tad akṣaram) is grasped — invisible, ungraspable, family-less, casteless, sight-less, hearing-less, hand-less, foot-less, eternal, all-pervading, omnipresent, the womb of beings. The rest of the Upaniṣad expounds how to move from aparā to parā: not by abandoning ritual but by transcending it, not by argument but by jñāna-prasāda — the grace of knowing that follows a purified consciousness."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"2. Why \"Muṇḍaka\"? The shaven-head vow","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["The name muṇḍaka is unique in the Upaniṣad corpus. It derives from muṇḍ (\"to shave\") and refers — according to Śaṅkara — to the vow called muṇḍana or śiro-vrata that was prescribed for Atharvan brahmacārins: the head was completely shaven and a fire was carried on the head as part of the initiatic discipline (cf. 3.2.10, śiro-vrataṃ vidhi-vad yais tu cīrṇam, \"by those who have duly performed the head-vow\"). The text restricts itself to those who have performed this vow — only such a student is competent to receive parā vidyā.","There is a secondary etymological reading, attested in Śaṅkara's introduction and developed at length by Aurobindo: muṇḍaka means \"that which shaves off,\" i.e., the doctrine which shaves the seeker of ignorance (avidyā). On this reading the title is not historical (the head-vow) but allegorical (the cutting-away of nāma-rūpa). Both readings are compatible: the head-vow is the external sign, the cutting-of-ignorance is its inner meaning.","Each of the three principal sections is called a muṇḍaka — \"shaving\" — and the text refers to itself as the muṇḍakopaniṣad. The three muṇḍakas are themselves three successive removals: the first muṇḍaka shaves the false equation of ritual with liberation; the second shaves the lower self-image (puruṣa as embodied agent) and replaces it with the cosmic Puruṣa; the third shaves the residual sense of doership and reveals the akṣara-Brahman directly."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"3. Recensions, manuscript variations, numbering","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["The Muṇḍaka exists in two recensions: the Śaunakīya (the dominant tradition, the one Śaṅkara comments upon) and the Paippalāda (preserved only fragmentarily; the principal full witness is the Orissa palm-leaf manuscript published by Bhattacharyya, 1997). Śaṅkara knows only the Śaunaka recension. The Paippalāda recension differs principally in three places: (a) the order of mantras 1.2.7–10 is inverted in some Paippalāda witnesses; (b) the famous dvā suparṇā mantra (3.1.1) reads sayujau sakhāyau in dual rather than sayujā sakhāyā — a metrically equivalent variant also found in Ṛgveda 1.164.20; (c) the final benedictory verse (3.2.11) is shorter in Paippalāda, omitting the namaḥ paramaṛṣibhyaḥ repetition.","The total mantra count is 64 in the standard division: 1.1 has 9 mantras, 1.2 has 13, 2.1 has 10, 2.2 has 11, 3.1 has 10, 3.2 has 11. (A minor variation: some editions split mantra 2.2.7 into 2.2.7a + 2.2.7b — the prose half ya sarvajñaḥ sarvavid and the anuṣṭubh half mano-mayaḥ prāṇa-śarīra-netā — giving a total of 65 mantras. The split is editorial; both halves are unbroken in Śaṅkara's bhāṣya and most printed editions, and we treat them as one mantra here, following the Adyar/Gambhirananda numbering.)","<table><tr><td>Locus</td><td>Variation</td><td>Stake</td></tr><tr><td>1.1.1 śānti-pāṭha</td><td>Atharva-Veda peace mantra, shared with Praśna &amp; Māṇḍūkya. Identical wording in all three.</td><td>Confirms Atharvan affiliation; no doctrinal variant.</td></tr><tr><td>1.1.4 paropara</td><td>Some MSS read parā vāparā ca; Śaṅkara reads parā caivāparā ca.</td><td>Trivial — both mean the same.</td></tr><tr><td>1.2.7 plavāḥ</td><td>aṣṭādaśoktam avaraṃ yeṣu karma vs. aṣṭādaśoktam avaraṃ yeṣu karma. Some Paippalāda MSS read navadaśa (\"nineteen\"); Śaunaka uniformly aṣṭādaśa (\"eighteen\").</td><td>The eighteen = the eighteen ritual offices of a śrauta-sacrifice (16 ṛtviks + the yajamāna + his wife). The Paippalāda variant counts 19.</td></tr><tr><td>2.2.4 praṇavo</td><td>All recensions agree on praṇavo dhanuḥ śaro hy ātmā. Aurobindo prefers dhanur for euphony with the next pāda.</td><td>No doctrinal stake.</td></tr><tr><td>3.1.1 sayujā</td><td>Śaunaka: sayujā sakhāyā (dual, but pseudo-singular form for metre); Paippalāda &amp; ṚV 1.164.20: sayujau sakhāyau (regular dual).</td><td>Identical sense; both attest the antiquity of the verse.</td></tr><tr><td>3.1.3 puṇya-pāpe</td><td>All recensions agree puṇya-pāpe vidhūya, but Śvetāśvatara 4.7 (which quotes this mantra) reads pāpa-puṇye.</td><td>Trivial word-order. Confirms ŚU's borrowing from MuU.</td></tr><tr><td>3.1.6 satyam eva</td><td>All recensions agree. The famous motto.</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>3.2.3 nāyam ātmā</td><td>Identical to Kaṭha 1.2.23 except for the closing phrase: MuU reads tasyaiṣa ātmā vivṛṇute tanūṃ svām (\"to him this ātman reveals its own body\"); Kaṭha reads tasyaiṣa ātmā vivṛṇute tanūṃ svām (identical). Either MuU borrows from Kaṭha or both quote a now-lost common source.</td><td>Cross-Upaniṣadic attestation. Most likely common source.</td></tr><tr><td>3.2.11 colophon</td><td>Śaunaka ends with namaḥ paramaṛṣibhyaḥ namaḥ paramaṛṣibhyaḥ (doubled); Paippalāda omits the second.</td><td>Liturgical doubling; no doctrinal stake.</td></tr></table>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"4. The five commentaries summarised","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["The five-commentary stack used in this volume mirrors the Īśā / Kena / Praśna volumes, with one substitution: Aurobindo replaces Sharvananda as the fifth voice. Aurobindo wrote a complete, verse-by-verse Muṇḍaka commentary (one of only three principal Upaniṣads he treated in full, the others being the Īśā and the Kena), and his treatment is the deepest of any 20th-century reading. Sharvananda has not written on the Muṇḍaka.","Swami Gambhirananda (Advaita Ashrama, Eight Upaniṣads with the Commentary of Śaṅkarācārya, vol. 2, 1958) — Carrier of Śaṅkara's Muṇḍaka-bhāṣya. Strictly bhāṣya-following Advaita with extensive ārṣa-pramāṇa citation. Reproduces every Śaṅkaran word-by-word gloss and every yuktiśāstra cross-reference. Cited herein as \"Śaṅkara\" when summarising the bhāṣya, \"Gambhirananda\" when summarising his English notes.","Swami Sivananda (Divine Life Society, The Principal Upanishads, 1937; supplemented by his The Mundakopanishad lecture-series, Rishikesh) — Practical-sādhanā Advaita. Reads the Muṇḍaka as a sādhanā-text: the parā/aparā distinction is presented as a discipline of perception; the praṇava-dhanus passage is given as a complete meditation method; the head-vow is reinterpreted as the daily detachment of the mind from sense-objects.","Swami Krishnananda (Divine Life Society, The Mundaka Upanishad lectures, 1980s, published posthumously) — Philosophical-Advaita. Krishnananda's strength is to triangulate the Muṇḍaka against the Bṛhad-Āraṇyaka 3.8 (Gārgī's akṣara), Chāndogya 6 (tat-tvam-asi), and the Māṇḍūkya-Kārikā. He reads the akṣara of Muṇḍaka and the akṣara of Gārgī as identical, and treats the dvā suparṇā mantra as the Upaniṣadic anticipation of the Advaita ābhāsa-vāda.","Paul Deussen (Sechzig Upaniṣad's des Veda, 1897; Eng. tr. Bedekar/Palsule, Motilal 1980) — Indological-historical. Treats the Muṇḍaka as a transitional text between the Brāhmaṇa-period karma-mīmāṃsā and the later Upaniṣadic jñāna-mīmāṃsā. Famous for arguing that mantras 1.2.7–10 are the strongest anti-ritualist polemic anywhere in the Veda. Provides German parallel translations of every cross-cited verse (ṚV, AV, ŚU, KaU, BG).","Sri Aurobindo (The Upanishads I — Īśā, Kena, Muṇḍaka, with commentaries; CWSA vol. 17, originally 1916–20 in Ārya) — Symbolist-Vedānta. The most expansive 20th-century reading. Aurobindo treats every image (the spider, the spark, the two birds, the bow-and-arrow, the rivers) as a precisely intended symbol, not metaphor. His commentary is the only one to argue that aparā vidyā is itself a vidyā — a knowledge, not non-knowledge — and that the move from aparā to parā is not abandonment but inclusion.","Order of commentary digests below: Śaṅkara (via Gambhirananda) — Sivananda — Krishnananda — Deussen — Aurobindo."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Peace Invocation (Atharva-Veda Śānti-pāṭha)","title":"","sa":["ॐ भद्रं कर्णेभिः शृणुयाम देवा भद्रं पश्येमाक्षभिर्यजत्राः ।","स्थिरैरङ्गैस्तुष्टुवाꣳसस्तनूभिर्व्यशेम देवहितं यदायुः ॥","स्वस्ति न इन्द्रो वृद्धश्रवाः स्वस्ति नः पूषा विश्ववेदाः ।","स्वस्ति नस्तार्क्ष्यो अरिष्टनेमिः स्वस्ति नो बृहस्पतिर्दधातु ॥","॥ ॐ शान्तिः शान्तिः शान्तिः ॥"],"iast":["oṃ bhadraṃ karṇebhiḥ śṛṇuyāma devā bhadraṃ paśyemākṣabhir yajatrāḥ |","sthirair aṅgais tuṣṭuvāṃsas tanūbhir vyaśema devahitaṃ yad āyuḥ ||","svasti na indro vṛddha-śravāḥ svasti naḥ pūṣā viśva-vedāḥ |","svasti nas tārkṣyo ariṣṭa-nemiḥ svasti no bṛhaspatir dadhātu ||","|| oṃ śāntiḥ śāntiḥ śāntiḥ ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"Oṃ. May we, O gods, hear what is auspicious with our ears; may we, O worshipful ones, see what is auspicious with our eyes. With steady limbs and bodies, praising you, may we attain the full life-span set for us by the gods.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["May Indra of widespread fame grant us welfare; may Pūṣā the all-knowing grant us welfare; may Tārkṣya of unbroken wheel-rim grant us welfare; may Bṛhaspati grant us welfare.","Oṃ. Peace, peace, peace.\"","This is the standard Atharva-Veda śānti-mantra, shared by the Muṇḍaka, Praśna, and Māṇḍūkya. Its structure is outward-then-protective: the first ṛc petitions for auspicious sense-perception (the prerequisite for receiving the teaching), the second invokes four protective deities (Indra, Pūṣā, Tārkṣya, Bṛhaspati) named for their epithets (vṛddha-śravā = \"of widespread fame,\" viśva-vedā = \"all-knowing,\" ariṣṭa-nemi = \"of unbroken wheel-rim,\" bṛhaspati = \"lord of mantra\"). Śaṅkara notes that the four protectors map to the four Vedas: Indra to Ṛc, Pūṣā to Sāma, Tārkṣya to Yajus, Bṛhaspati to Atharva — which is why this is specifically the Atharvan invocation."]}]},{"t":"","u":[{"ref":"The Three Muṇḍakas","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["Each mantra below is given as: Devanāgarī → IAST → pada-by-pada gloss → literal → paraphrase → five-commentary digest. Grammar abbreviations: m./f./n. (masc./fem./neut.); nom./acc./instr./dat./abl./gen./loc./voc.; sg./du./pl.; pres./impf./pf./pot./impv./ger./inf./caus.; pp. = past participle; bv. = bahuvrīhi; tp. = tatpuruṣa; dv. = dvandva; kd. = karmadhāraya. The verse-numbering is the standard Adyar/Śaṅkara count."],"kind":"note"}]},{"t":"Muṇḍaka 1, Khaṇḍa 1 — Aṅgiras' teaching: parā and aparā vidyā (9 mantras)","u":[{"ref":"Muṇḍaka 1, Khaṇḍa 1","title":"Aṅgiras' teaching: parā and aparā vidyā (9 mantras)","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["The Upaniṣad opens with a five-generation paraṃparā (Brahmā → Atharvan → Aṅgir → Bhāradvāja Satyavāha → Aṅgiras), then a question from the householder Śaunaka, then the central doctrinal distinction (parā/aparā), then a cosmogony in which the akṣara emits the cosmos as a spider emits its thread."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Mantra 1.1.1","title":"Brahmā, first of gods, teaches Atharvan brahma-vidyā","sa":["ॐ ब्रह्मा देवानां प्रथमः संबभूव विश्वस्य कर्ता भुवनस्य गोप्ता ।","स ब्रह्मविद्यां सर्वविद्याप्रतिष्ठामथर्वाय ज्येष्ठपुत्राय प्राह ॥ १॥"],"iast":["oṃ brahmā devānāṃ prathamaḥ saṃbabhūva viśvasya kartā bhuvanasya goptā |","sa brahma-vidyāṃ sarva-vidyā-pratiṣṭhām atharvāya jyeṣṭha-putrāya prāha || 1 ||"],"words":[["oṃ","indecl. (praṇava)","Oṃ"],["brahmā","m. nom. sg.","Brahmā (the four-faced creator)"],["devānām","m. gen. pl.","of the gods"],["prathamaḥ","m. nom. sg. adj.","first, foremost"],["saṃbabhūva","3 sg. pf. of sam-bhū","came into being together"],["viśvasya","n. gen. sg.","of the universe"],["kartā","m. nom. sg.","maker"],["bhuvanasya","n. gen. sg.","of the world"],["goptā","m. nom. sg.","protector"],["saḥ","m. nom. sg.","he"],["brahma-vidyām","f. acc. sg. tp.","the knowledge of Brahman"],["sarva-vidyā-pratiṣṭhām","f. acc. sg. tp.","the foundation of all knowledges"],["atharvāya","m. dat. sg.","to Atharvan"],["jyeṣṭha-putrāya","m. dat. sg. kd.","(to) the eldest son"],["prāha","3 sg. pf. of pra-ah","spoke, taught"]],"lit":"\"Oṃ. Brahmā arose as the first of the gods, maker of the universe, protector of the world. He taught the knowledge of Brahman — the foundation of all knowledges — to his eldest son Atharvan.\"","para":"The Upaniṣad opens by establishing the antiquity and the authority of its teaching. Brahmā (Hiraṇyagarbha, the four-faced creator) is the first of the devas to come into being; he is the maker (kartā) of the cosmos and its protector (goptā). To Atharvan, his firstborn, he transmits not just any knowledge but brahma-vidyā — the knowledge that is the foundation (pratiṣṭhā) of all the other knowledges. The Upaniṣad is thus claimed to be the senior knowledge, the one on which every other rests.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> The compound sarva-vidyā-pratiṣṭhā is the key claim. Every other vidyā — the Vedas, the aṅgas, the upāṅgas, even the secular knowledges of agriculture and warfare — rests on brahma-vidyā in the sense that without the discrimination Brahman/non-Brahman they have no liberative force. The pratiṣṭhā here is not chronological priority (older knowledge) but ontological priority (more-foundational knowledge). Note that Brahmā is described as kartā and goptā — both creator and preserver — establishing him as the īśvara-level deity, not yet identified with the para-Brahman of mantra 4."],["Sivananda","Brahmā here is Hiraṇyagarbha, the cosmic Mind — not the para-Brahman. The lineage Brahmā → Atharvan → … establishes a guru-paraṃparā. The practical lesson: brahma-vidyā cannot be self-acquired. It must descend through a living chain. Even Brahmā, the cosmic Mind, transmits it onward and does not retain it."],["Krishnananda","The opening mantra is unusual in that it names Brahmā as a son's father — the cosmogony is presented as genealogy. Krishnananda reads this as the Atharvan signature: Atharvan-period thought treats cosmogony and family-descent as homologous. The same pattern recurs in 2.1.1 where the akṣara emits beings as fire emits sparks, and in 3.2.6 where the rivers reach the ocean by flowing."],["Deussen","Reads the verse historically. The five-name paraṃparā that follows (Brahmā / Atharvan / Aṅgir / Bhāradvāja Satyavāha / Aṅgiras) places the Muṇḍaka within the Atharvan school of brahmavidyā. Note that Atharvan is here Brahmā's son — a different genealogical position from the Ṛgvedic Atharvan (where Atharvan is an ancient priest, not the firstborn of Brahmā). The shift reflects the Atharvan school's claim to seniority among the four Vedic traditions."],["Aurobindo","The opening is not narrative — it is dīkṣā. Brahmā as \"first of the gods\" is the cosmic Intelligence that knows itself; brahmavidyā transmitted to the firstborn Atharvan is the descent of self-knowledge from the universal into the individual. Aurobindo treats sarva-vidyā-pratiṣṭhā as the foundational claim of all Vedānta: every knowledge is partial; only the knowledge-of-That is foundation, because That is the knower in every knowledge."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 1.1.2","title":"The lineage continues: Aṅgir → Bhāradvāja → Aṅgiras","sa":["अथर्वणे यां प्रवदेत ब्रह्माऽथर्वा तं पुरोवाचाङ्गिरे ब्रह्मविद्याम् ।","स भारद्वाजाय सत्यवाहाय प्राह भारद्वाजोऽङ्गिरसे परावराम् ॥ २॥"],"iast":["atharvaṇe yāṃ pravadeta brahmātharvā taṃ puro-vācāṅgire brahma-vidyām |","sa bhāradvājāya satyavāhāya prāha bhāradvājo'ṅgirase parāvarām || 2 ||"],"words":[["atharvaṇe","m. dat. sg.","to Atharvan"],["yām","f. acc. sg. rel.","which"],["pravadeta","3 sg. pot. of pra-vad","had spoken / taught"],["brahmā","m. nom. sg.","Brahmā"],["atharvā","m. nom. sg.","Atharvan"],["tām","f. acc. sg.","that (vidyā)"],["purā","indecl.","of old, formerly"],["uvāca","3 sg. pf. of vac","spoke"],["aṅgire","m. dat. sg.","to Aṅgir"],["brahma-vidyām","f. acc. sg. tp.","the knowledge of Brahman"],["saḥ","m. nom. sg.","he (Aṅgir)"],["bhāradvājāya","m. dat. sg.","to (the) Bhāradvāja"],["satyavāhāya","m. dat. sg.","(named) Satyavāha"],["prāha","3 sg. pf. of pra-ah","spoke"],["bhāradvājaḥ","m. nom. sg.","Bhāradvāja"],["aṅgirase","m. dat. sg.","to Aṅgiras"],["parāvarām","f. acc. sg. dv.","the higher-and-lower (knowledge)"]],"lit":"\"That knowledge which Brahmā had taught to Atharvan — that (knowledge of) Brahman — Atharvan in turn taught of old to Aṅgir. He (Aṅgir) taught it to Bhāradvāja Satyavāha; Bhāradvāja (taught it) to Aṅgiras, the higher-and-lower (knowledge).\"","para":"The Upaniṣad now fills in the lineage: Brahmā taught Atharvan, Atharvan taught Aṅgir, Aṅgir taught Bhāradvāja Satyavāha, Bhāradvāja Satyavāha taught Aṅgiras. Five generations are named. At Aṅgiras the knowledge is significantly described as parāvarā — the \"higher-and-lower\" — anticipating the parā/aparā distinction of mantra 4. The compound parāvara is doctrinal foreshadowing: it tells the listener that the vidyā about to be transmitted has two tiers.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> The compound parāvarām is a dvandva (\"the higher-and-lower\"), and its placement here is no accident. Śaṅkara reads it as the bīja-mantra of the whole Upaniṣad — what Aṅgiras receives is not just the parā but the parā and the aparā together. He notes that the very fact that the transmission contains both knowledges shows that even an Atharvan-school brahmavidyā does not abandon the aparā but incorporates it."],["Sivananda","Each link in the chain is a name — and each name is significant. Atharvan is the visionary of the Atharva-Veda; Aṅgir is the ancestor of the Aṅgirases (the Atharvan rṣi family); Bhāradvāja Satyavāha means \"the truth-bearer Bhāradvāja\"; Aṅgiras the recipient is the rṣi to whom Śaunaka now comes. The chain is unbroken father-to-son or teacher-to-student, and the practical lesson is that brahmavidyā moves person-to-person only."],["Krishnananda","parāvarām is a hapax in this position — nowhere else in the principal Upaniṣads does it foreshadow a doctrinal distinction in this way. Krishnananda reads its insertion at the end of the verse as deliberate: the whole rest of the Upaniṣad is anticipated in this one compound."],["Deussen","Notes that the lineage compresses what in the Atharvan śākhā tradition is presented as a much longer chain. The five-name version of the Muṇḍaka is the shortest of any school. Deussen takes this as evidence that the Muṇḍaka is a relatively late text — earlier Atharvan brahmavidyā genealogies (preserved in the Gopatha-Brāhmaṇa) include up to twelve teachers."],["Aurobindo","The lineage is symbolic as well as historical. Brahmā = the universal Mind; Atharvan = the priest of fire (the inner agni); Aṅgir = the messenger between heaven and earth; Bhāradvāja Satyavāha = the carrier of satya; Aṅgiras the receiver = the consummated rṣi. The transmission is the descent of self-knowledge through five gradations of consciousness — from cosmic Mind to embodied wisdom."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 1.1.3","title":"Śaunaka's question: by knowing what does all become known?","sa":["शौनको ह वै महाशालोऽङ्गिरसं विधिवदुपसन्नः पप्रच्छ ।","कस्मिन्नु भगवो विज्ञाते सर्वमिदं विज्ञातं भवतीति ॥ ३॥"],"iast":["śaunako ha vai mahāśālo'ṅgirasaṃ vidhi-vad upasannaḥ papraccha |","kasmin nu bhagavo vijñāte sarvam idaṃ vijñātaṃ bhavatīti || 3 ||"],"words":[["śaunakaḥ","m. nom. sg.","Śaunaka"],["ha vai","indecl. (emphatic)","indeed, verily"],["mahā-śālaḥ","m. nom. sg. bv.","the great householder (lit. \"of the great hall\")"],["aṅgirasam","m. acc. sg.","to Aṅgiras"],["vidhi-vat","indecl. adv.","in the prescribed manner"],["upasannaḥ","m. nom. sg. pp.","having approached"],["papraccha","3 sg. pf. of pracch","asked"],["kasmin","n. loc. sg. interr.","in what (when known)"],["nu","indecl.","indeed (mild interrog. force)"],["bhagavaḥ","m. voc. sg.","O blessed one"],["vijñāte","n. loc. sg. pp.","having been known"],["sarvam","n. nom. sg.","everything"],["idam","n. nom. sg.","this"],["vijñātam","n. nom. sg. pp.","known"],["bhavati","3 sg. pres. of bhū","becomes"],["iti","indecl.","thus (closing the quotation)"]],"lit":"\"Śaunaka, verily a great householder, having approached Aṅgiras in the prescribed manner, asked: 'O Blessed One, by what being known does all this become known?'\"","para":"The frame-narrative breaks: Śaunaka, a great householder (mahāśāla), approaches Aṅgiras with the proper formalities of a pupil and asks the foundational question of the Upaniṣad — Kasmin nu bhagavo vijñāte sarvam idaṃ vijñātaṃ bhavati? — \"By knowing what, does all this become known?\" The question presupposes that there is some single thing the knowing of which is the knowing of everything. The rest of the Upaniṣad is the answer.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> The question is the same as Chāndogya 6.1.3 (kena tu khalu bhagavo sa ādeśo bhavatīti — \"by what teaching does that become so?\" — followed by Uddālaka's discussion of the clay/clay-pot relation). Śaunaka's framing is more abstract: he does not ask kena (\"by what means\") but kasmin (\"in what, when it is known\"). The locative absolute construction (vijñāte) is precise: when X is known, then automatically Y is known. The relation is one of identity-knowledge, not inference. Śaṅkara emphasises that the answer cannot be the karma-kāṇḍa, because karma produces effects but no single-cognition-yields-all-cognitions effect."],["Sivananda","Śaunaka is identified as a mahāśāla — \"great-housed,\" i.e., a wealthy householder, the head of a Vedic clan-establishment with many disciples. Yet despite his wealth and standing, he approaches Aṅgiras vidhivat — \"according to rule\" — as an ordinary student. The lesson is that brahmavidyā recognises no caste or social rank in the moment of asking. The mahāśāla bows like the brahmacārī."],["Krishnananda","The locative vijñāte is the most important grammatical feature of the verse. It is a sat-saptamī (locative absolute) — \"when X is known.\" Krishnananda contrasts this with the instrumental construction that would have been natural in karma-mīmāṃsā (\"by what means\"). The Upaniṣad will answer not in terms of means but in terms of substrate: when the substrate is known, all that depends on it is known."],["Deussen","Compares the Muṇḍaka's question to the parallel openings of Chāndogya 6.1, Bṛhad-Āraṇyaka 2.4.5 (Maitreyī asks Yājñavalkya), and Praśna 1 (the six approach Pippalāda). All four texts use the same structural template: a question of cosmic scope is asked of a guru; the answer is the body of the Upaniṣad. Deussen treats this as the genre-marker of Upaniṣadic literature."],["Aurobindo","Śaunaka is described as mahāśāla but his approach is brahmacāric — the contrast is the verse's first lesson. The mahāśāla, the master of an establishment, asks not how to enlarge it but what knowing will reveal it as illusory in the very moment of revealing what is real. Aurobindo emphasises that the question is not about ontology but about cognition: not \"what is the source of all?\" but \"what single cognition, when had, is the having-of-all-cognitions?\" — a phenomenological framing, not a metaphysical one."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 1.1.4","title":"Aṅgiras' answer: parā and aparā","sa":["तस्मै स होवाच ।","द्वे विद्ये वेदितव्ये इति ह स्म यद्ब्रह्मविदो वदन्ति परा चैवापरा च ॥ ४॥"],"iast":["tasmai sa hovāca |","dve vidye veditavye iti ha sma yad brahma-vido vadanti parā caivāparā ca || 4 ||"],"words":[["tasmai","m. dat. sg.","to him"],["saḥ","m. nom. sg.","he (Aṅgiras)"],["ha uvāca","indecl. + 3 sg. pf. of vac","indeed said"],["dve","f. nom. du.","two"],["vidye","f. nom. du.","knowledges"],["veditavye","f. nom. du. gerundive","to be known"],["iti","indecl.","thus"],["ha sma","indecl. (narrative)","(it is said)"],["yat","n. acc. sg. rel.","what"],["brahma-vidaḥ","m. nom. pl.","the knowers of Brahman"],["vadanti","3 pl. pres. of vad","say"],["parā","f. nom. sg.","the higher"],["ca eva","indecl.","and indeed"],["aparā","f. nom. sg.","the lower"],["ca","indecl.","and"]],"lit":"\"To him he said: 'Two knowledges are to be known — so the knowers of Brahman say — the higher (parā) and the lower (aparā).'\"","para":"Aṅgiras's reply is the doctrinal core of the Muṇḍaka and one of the most-quoted half-verses in all Vedānta. There are two knowledges that the knowers of Brahman acknowledge as worth knowing: the parā (higher) and the aparā (lower). The distinction is not between true and false knowledge — both are veditavye, \"to be known\" — but between two registers of knowing. The next mantra will define each.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> The dual form veditavye is critical: both vidyās are to be known. Aparā is not to be discarded — it is to be known. Śaṅkara reads this as the textual basis for the Advaita position that the karma-kāṇḍa retains validity at its own level; only its claim to be the supreme means of liberation is to be transcended, not its content. The phrase brahma-vido vadanti (\"the knowers of Brahman say\") indicates that this is not Aṅgiras's innovation but a settled view of the school of those-who-know."],["Sivananda","The two-knowledge framework is the parent of the entire later Vedānta apavāda methodology. Aparā vidyā corresponds to vyavahārika satya; parā vidyā to pāramārthika satya. Sivananda's daily-practice gloss: study the Vedas and śāstras (aparā) by day; meditate on the akṣara (parā) at twilight; let neither displace the other until parā ripens into spontaneous abiding."],["Krishnananda","The dvandva parā caivāparā ca is not separative but unitive. Both knowledges name the same Real from different vantages: aparā names it as the world (the cosmic structure described by the Vedas); parā names it as the akṣara, the substrate of that structure. To say \"two knowledges\" is to say \"two aspects of one knowledge.\" The verb veditavye (\"to be known\") is dual because the aspects are two, not because the things known are two."],["Deussen","This is, for Deussen, the most important verse in the Upaniṣad and one of the most important in the Veda. The parā/aparā distinction historically licenses the entire later Vedāntic project: a single tradition can authoritatively contain both ritualism (aparā) and gnosis (parā) by treating them as registers, not rivals. Deussen notes that no other Upaniṣad makes this distinction so cleanly; the Bṛhad-Āraṇyaka's vidyā/avidyā contrast (4.4.10) is sharper but excludes ritual; the Muṇḍaka's parā/aparā contrast is more capacious."],["Aurobindo","The genius of the Muṇḍaka is to call the lower knowledge a knowledge. Other Upaniṣads call ritualism avidyā or karma-kāṇḍa; the Muṇḍaka grants it the title vidyā. The doctrinal consequence is enormous: the move from aparā to parā is not abandonment of one knowledge for another, but the supercession of one knowledge by a fuller one which contains it. Aurobindo's whole later doctrine of supramental transformation rests on this verse: lower planes are not denied but included."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 1.1.5","title":"The aparā: four Vedas and six aṅgas; the parā: the akṣara","sa":["तत्रापरा ऋग्वेदो यजुर्वेदः सामवेदोऽथर्ववेदः शिक्षा कल्पो व्याकरणं निरुक्तं छन्दो ज्योतिषमिति ।","अथ परा यया तदक्षरमधिगम्यते ॥ ५॥"],"iast":["tatrāparā ṛgvedo yajurvedaḥ sāmavedo'tharvavedaḥ śikṣā kalpo vyākaraṇaṃ niruktaṃ chando jyotiṣam iti |","atha parā yayā tad akṣaram adhigamyate || 5 ||"],"words":[["tatra","indecl.","of those (two)"],["aparā","f. nom. sg.","the lower"],["ṛgvedaḥ","m. nom. sg.","the Ṛgveda"],["yajurvedaḥ","m. nom. sg.","the Yajurveda"],["sāmavedaḥ","m. nom. sg.","the Sāmaveda"],["atharvavedaḥ","m. nom. sg.","the Atharvaveda"],["śikṣā","f. nom. sg.","phonetics"],["kalpaḥ","m. nom. sg.","ritual-procedure"],["vyākaraṇam","n. nom. sg.","grammar"],["niruktam","n. nom. sg.","etymology"],["chandaḥ","n. nom. sg.","metrics"],["jyotiṣam","n. nom. sg.","astronomy/astrology"],["iti","indecl.","thus (closing the list)"],["atha","indecl.","now"],["parā","f. nom. sg.","the higher"],["yayā","f. instr. sg. rel.","by which"],["tat","n. acc. sg.","that"],["akṣaram","n. acc. sg.","indestructible (one)"],["adhigamyate","3 sg. pres. pass. of adhi-gam","is reached / attained"]],"lit":"\"Of these, the lower is: the Ṛgveda, the Yajurveda, the Sāmaveda, the Atharvaveda; Śikṣā, Kalpa, Vyākaraṇa, Nirukta, Chandas, Jyotiṣa. Now the higher is that by which the Indestructible is reached.\"","para":"The contents of the two vidyās are now spelt out. Aparā vidyā = the four Vedas plus the six Vedāṅgas (Phonetics, Ritual, Grammar, Etymology, Metrics, Astronomy) — i.e., the entire Vedic and Vedic-auxiliary corpus, the whole of received scriptural learning. Parā vidyā is defined only relationally: it is yayā tad akṣaram adhigamyate, \"that by which the Indestructible is attained.\" Note the asymmetry: aparā is enumerable; parā is identifiable only by its result.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> The radical claim of this verse is that the four Vedas themselves are aparā vidyā. Even the karma-kāṇḍa-rich Vedic corpus, even the Upaniṣad-bearing brāhmaṇas, even — by some readings — the verbal text of this very Upaniṣad, are aparā when treated as recited text rather than as access-route to the akṣara. The aparā is the śabda; the parā is the artha. Śaṅkara cites Bṛhad-Āraṇyaka 2.4.10 — \"the Vedas exhaled out of the Self\" — to make the parallel: the Vedas come from the akṣara and are aparā precisely because they are emanations, not the source."],["Sivananda","A daily-practice gloss: the same text can be aparā or parā depending on how you read it. Reciting the Gīta to fulfill a pārāyaṇa-vow is aparā; reading it to understand Kṛṣṇa's nature is also aparā; reading it as the very voice of the akṣara addressing your specific predicament is parā. The shift is not from one body of text to another; it is from text-as-information to text-as-summons."],["Krishnananda","The asymmetric definition (aparā enumerated, parā only described by its goal) is itself a teaching. Parā vidyā cannot be enumerated because it is not a quantum of information. It is the cognitive movement by which the akṣara is reached — and that movement is the same whether occasioned by the Upaniṣad, by the Vedānta-sūtras, by the words of a guru, or by silent meditation. The enumerative form fits aparā; only the relational form fits parā."],["Deussen","The list of six Vedāṅgas is in canonical order (śikṣā, kalpa, vyākaraṇa, nirukta, chandas, jyotiṣa) — the order they take in every later school. Deussen notes that this is one of the earliest occurrences of the full six-fold list; in earlier Brāhmaṇa-period sources only some are named. The Muṇḍaka thus inherits a settled six-fold canon and is therefore post-Brāhmaṇa."],["Aurobindo","That the four Vedas are called aparā is the verse's hammer-blow. Vedic ritual, Vedic recitation, Vedic learning — all aparā. But the same Vedic mantras are also the access-route to parā when treated symbolically rather than literally. Aurobindo cites his own Secret of the Veda: every Vedic mantra has both an outer (ritual) and an inner (psychological) meaning; the outer is aparā, the inner is parā. The Muṇḍaka legitimises this hermeneutic."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 1.1.6","title":"The akṣara: invisible, ungraspable, casteless, eternal","sa":["यत्तदद्रेश्यमग्राह्यमगोत्रमवर्ण-मचक्षुःश्रोत्रं तदपाणिपादम् ।","नित्यं विभुं सर्वगतं सुसूक्ष्मं तदव्ययं यद्भूतयोनिं परिपश्यन्ति धीराः ॥ ६॥"],"iast":["yat tad adreśyam agrāhyam agotram avarṇam acakṣuḥ-śrotraṃ tad apāṇi-pādam |","nityaṃ vibhuṃ sarva-gataṃ susūkṣmaṃ tad avyayaṃ yad bhūta-yoniṃ paripaśyanti dhīrāḥ || 6 ||"],"words":[["yat","n. nom. sg. rel.","that which"],["tat","n. nom. sg.","(is) that"],["adreśyam","n. nom. sg. adj.","not-to-be-seen, invisible"],["agrāhyam","n. nom. sg. adj.","ungraspable"],["agotram","n. nom. sg. adj. bv.","without lineage, family-less"],["avarṇam","n. nom. sg. adj. bv.","without colour/caste"],["acakṣuḥ-śrotram","n. nom. sg. adj. bv.","without eye or ear"],["tat","n. nom. sg.","that"],["apāṇi-pādam","n. nom. sg. adj. bv.","without hand or foot"],["nityam","n. nom. sg. adj.","eternal"],["vibhum","n. nom. sg. adj.","all-pervading"],["sarva-gatam","n. nom. sg. adj. tp.","going-everywhere"],["su-sūkṣmam","n. nom. sg. adj.","extremely subtle"],["tat","n. nom. sg.","that"],["avyayam","n. nom. sg. adj.","imperishable"],["yat","n. nom. sg. rel.","which"],["bhūta-yonim","f. acc. sg. tp.","as the womb of beings"],["paripaśyanti","3 pl. pres. of pari-paś","see-around, perceive (fully)"],["dhīrāḥ","m. nom. pl.","the wise"]],"lit":"\"That which is invisible, ungraspable, without family, without colour, without eye or ear, without hand or foot — eternal, all-pervading, omnipresent, extremely subtle, imperishable — that the wise behold as the womb of beings.\"","para":"The first description of the akṣara, by negation (neti-neti) and then by positive predication. Negation: a-dreśyam, a-grāhyam, a-gotram, a-varṇam, a-cakṣuḥ-śrotram, a-pāṇi-pādam. Positive: nitya, vibhu, sarva-gata, su-sūkṣma, a-vyaya. And the climactic identification: bhūta-yoni, the womb of beings. The negations strip the akṣara of every form of finitude (no sensory marker, no genealogical or social marker, no instrumental marker); the positives affirm what remains (eternity, ubiquity, fineness, imperishability). The dhīras — the wise — see this not by closed-eye inspection but by paripaśyanti, \"seeing-all-around,\" a 360° perception.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> The string of six negations is not a denial of existence but a denial of qualification. The akṣara is not invisible because it is hidden; it is invisible because it has no visible attribute. Similarly with agotra (\"casteless\") and avarṇa (\"colourless\"): the akṣara does not belong to a brāhmaṇa-kṣatriya-vaiśya-śūdra schema and does not have a fair/dark/red/white complexion — it has no category of body and therefore none of body's predicates. The positive list (nitya, vibhu, sarva-gata, su-sūkṣma, avyaya) is then the upalakṣaṇa — the indicator-quality — of that which has no qualities of the negated kinds."],["Sivananda","Gives this as the canonical mantra for nirguṇa-meditation. Sit, breathe slowly, recite the six negations one by one (\"not visible, not graspable, not lineaged, not coloured, not sense-organed, not limbed\"), then the five positives (\"eternal, pervading, everywhere, finest, imperishable\"), and rest. Repeat morning and evening. Sivananda calls this the upādhi-niṣedha exercise."],["Krishnananda","The agotra and avarṇa terms are politically radical: they declare the akṣara to be without caste. This is, for Krishnananda, the Upaniṣad's most underrated phrase. The Veda — supposedly the source of the four-varṇa schema — here declares its own ultimate referent to be casteless. The aparā is varṇic; the parā is avarṇa."],["Deussen","The string of negations follows a standard Upaniṣadic apophatic pattern (cf. Bṛhad-Āraṇyaka 3.8.8 — sthūlam aśūlam aṇv ahrasvaṃ, Gārgī's akṣara-passage). Deussen translates apāṇi-pādam not as \"hand-less, foot-less\" but as \"limb-less in general\" — taking the dvandva as an upalakṣaṇa for all body-parts. The whole list is then: senseless, organless, limbless, casteless — i.e., bodyless and socially-categoryless."],["Aurobindo","The most significant phrase is bhūta-yoni — the womb of beings. The akṣara is not transcendent in the sense of distant; it is transcendent in the sense of source. Every being has just-now emerged from it and is in some sense still inside it. The negations are not denials of being but denials of finite-mode-of-being. Compare Aurobindo's later term in The Life Divine: \"Saccidānanda is not above the world; it is within the world as its sustaining ground.\""]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 1.1.7","title":"Cosmogony: spider, plants, hair — the akṣara emits the cosmos","sa":["यथोर्णनाभिः सृजते गृह्णते च यथा पृथिव्यामोषधयः संभवन्ति ।","यथा सतः पुरुषात्केशलोमानि तथाऽक्षरात्संभवतीह विश्वम् ॥ ७॥"],"iast":["yathorṇa-nābhiḥ sṛjate gṛhṇate ca yathā pṛthivyām oṣadhayaḥ saṃbhavanti |","yathā sataḥ puruṣāt keśa-lomāni tathākṣarāt saṃbhavatīha viśvam || 7 ||"],"words":[["yathā","indecl.","as, just as"],["ūrṇa-nābhiḥ","m. nom. sg. bv.","spider (lit. \"wool-naveled\")"],["sṛjate","3 sg. pres. mid. of sṛj","emits"],["gṛhṇate","3 sg. pres. mid. of grah","draws back, withdraws"],["ca","indecl.","and"],["yathā","indecl.","just as"],["pṛthivyām","f. loc. sg.","on the earth"],["oṣadhayaḥ","f. nom. pl.","plants, herbs"],["saṃbhavanti","3 pl. pres. of sam-bhū","come into being"],["yathā","indecl.","just as"],["sataḥ","m. abl. sg. pres. part. of as","from (the) being / living"],["puruṣāt","m. abl. sg.","from a man"],["keśa-lomāni","n. nom. pl. dv.","hairs (of head and body)"],["tathā","indecl.","so"],["akṣarāt","n. abl. sg.","from the Indestructible"],["saṃbhavati","3 sg. pres. of sam-bhū","comes into being"],["iha","indecl.","here"],["viśvam","n. nom. sg.","the universe"]],"lit":"\"As the spider emits and draws back (its thread), as plants come forth from the earth, as hairs come forth from a living man — so from the Indestructible the universe comes forth here.\"","para":"Three similes, mounting in intimacy: (1) the spider that issues forth its web from its own body and at will draws it back in; (2) plants that arise spontaneously from the earth — no external sower needed; (3) hairs that grow from a living person without any deliberate act on their part. Each illustrates a different aspect of cosmogony: the spider models emanation-and-reabsorption (sṛjate / gṛhṇate); the plants model spontaneous unforced arising; the hair models emergence without the source's deliberate effort. The cosmos (viśva) emerges from the akṣara in just these three ways simultaneously — self-emitted, spontaneous, effortless.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> The three similes establish the abhinna-nimitta-upādāna doctrine — the akṣara is both the efficient cause (nimitta) and the material cause (upādāna) of the universe. The spider is its own material (the silk comes from inside it) and its own efficient agent (no other spider weaves for it). Earth is its own material for plants (the herbs are made of earthly substance) and the agent of their production (no other agent sows them). The puruṣa is the material of his hair (the hair is bodily matter) and the agent of its growth (no other person grows it for him). All three similes therefore deny external agency and external material; the akṣara is sva-yoni and sva-upādāna — its own womb, its own substance. This is the locus classicus for abhinna-nimitta-upādāna Brahman in Advaita."],["Sivananda","The spider simile is the more famous, but the hair simile is the more practical. Hairs grow whether you intend them or not; the cosmos arises whether the akṣara intends it or not. The implication for the seeker is that one's own thoughts arise in the same way — spontaneously from the substrate — and need no agent. Sivananda treats this verse as a propaedeutic for vāsanā-kṣaya: see the thoughts as hair-on-the-head, neither willed into being nor disturbed by their continual emergence."],["Krishnananda","The Sanskrit cosmogony before the Muṇḍaka had been theistic: Prajāpati creates, Brahmā emits, Viśvakarman builds. The Muṇḍaka's three similes are radically a-theistic in the technical sense — they replace deliberate creation with emanation. The akṣara is not a creator; it is a source. Krishnananda treats this as the doctrinal hinge between Ṛgvedic cosmogony (Hiraṇyagarbha, Puruṣa-sūkta) and Vedāntic emanation (the same in Chāndogya 6 with the clay-pot, the same in Taittirīya 2 with the food-sheath)."],["Deussen","The spider simile (ūrṇa-nābhi) recurs in Bṛhad-Āraṇyaka 2.1.20 (the same image used by Ajātaśatru) and Śvetāśvatara 6.10. The plant simile is unique to Muṇḍaka. The hair simile is unique to Muṇḍaka in this form, though Bṛhad-Āraṇyaka 1.4.7 uses a related image (\"as a razor in its case\"). Deussen treats the spider-simile as the most ancient and the hair-simile as the most precise. The hair-simile uniquely captures effortlessness; the other two do not."],["Aurobindo","The three similes are not three illustrations of the same fact but a graded ascent into the nature of emanation. The spider weaves: emanation is purposive (the web has a function). The plants grow: emanation is teleological (the plant has its own end). The hair sprouts: emanation is unteleological and unpurposive — there is no end-state the hair is reaching toward. By the third simile, the akṣara's emission of the cosmos is shown to have no purpose, no end, no function — only spontaneous overflow of its own nature. Aurobindo calls this līlā: the play that is its own justification."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 1.1.8","title":"Brahman matures by tapas: the cosmic order spelt out","sa":["तपसा चीयते ब्रह्म ततोऽन्नमभिजायते ।","अन्नात्प्राणो मनः सत्यं लोकाः कर्मसु चामृतम् ॥ ८॥"],"iast":["tapasā cīyate brahma tato'nnam abhijāyate |","annāt prāṇo manaḥ satyaṃ lokāḥ karmasu cāmṛtam || 8 ||"],"words":[["tapasā","n. instr. sg.","by tapas (heat / discipline / brooding)"],["cīyate","3 sg. pres. pass. of ci","is gathered / heaped / matured"],["brahma","n. nom. sg.","Brahman"],["tataḥ","indecl.","from that"],["annam","n. nom. sg.","food / matter"],["abhijāyate","3 sg. pres. of abhi-jan","comes forth"],["annāt","n. abl. sg.","from food"],["prāṇaḥ","m. nom. sg.","breath, life-force"],["manaḥ","n. nom. sg.","mind"],["satyam","n. nom. sg.","the real / cosmic order"],["lokāḥ","m. nom. pl.","worlds"],["karmasu","n. loc. pl.","in (the realm of) actions"],["ca","indecl.","and"],["amṛtam","n. nom. sg.","the immortal (recompense)"]],"lit":"\"By tapas Brahman is matured (\"piled-up\"); from that food comes forth; from food, breath, mind, the cosmic order, the worlds, and (in the realm of) actions, the immortal (fruit).\"","para":"The cosmogonic sequence: tapas → Brahman ripens → anna (food/matter) → prāṇa (breath/life) → manas (mind) → satya (the truth-order) → lokāḥ (worlds) → karma → amṛta (the deathless fruit of action). The verse compresses an entire ontology into a single ślokic line. Tapas (originally \"heat,\" then \"creative concentration,\" then \"ascesis\") is the catalyst by which Brahman concentrates into materiality; from materiality emerge the successive layers of life, mind, cosmic order, the worlds — and within the world of actions, the immortal element (the eternal merit accruing to karma).","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> The verb cīyate (\"is piled up, is matured\") is Atharvan idiom: Brahman is not made but ripened, as fruit ripens under the sun's tapas. This is not the creatio ex nihilo of later theistic Vedānta; it is a maturation-cosmogony. Note the sequence Brahman → anna → prāṇa → manas → satya → lokāḥ — exactly the Taittirīya 2 pañca-kośa minus the ānanda-maya. Śaṅkara cross-cites Taittirīya 2.6.1 (so'kāmayata bahu syāṃ prajāyeyeti — \"He desired: may I become many, may I be born\"). The Muṇḍaka's tapasā cīyate is the Atharvan form of Taittirīya's so'kāmayata."],["Sivananda","The practical lesson of this verse is that tapas is creative, not punitive. The Western misreading of tapas as \"asceticism\" — i.e., deprivation — misses its etymology. Tapas = warmth, concentration, gathering of inner heat. Brahman \"matures by tapas\" because tapas is the principle by which any being matures into a creator. Sivananda's daily-tapas: rise pre-dawn, kindle inner heat by prāṇāyāma, then attempt one creative act before the day's distractions begin."],["Krishnananda","The verse spells out a complete pañcakośa-like cosmology in eight syllables. Krishnananda diagrams it: tapas (the catalyst) → Brahman (the ripening source) → anna (the densest emanation) → prāṇa (the next subtler) → manas → satya → loka → karma-phala (amṛta). The reverse order would be the meditator's path of return: from karma back through loka, satya, manas, prāṇa, anna, to Brahman, with tapas as the constant agent of all transitions."],["Deussen","tapas here cannot mean \"asceticism\" in the post-Buddhist sense. The Ṛgvedic and Atharvan tapas is creative-cosmic heat — the same heat with which Prajāpati incubates the cosmic egg in ṚV 10.121 and AV 11.4. Brahman is here the cosmic egg in process of ripening; tapas is the warmth in which it ripens. Deussen takes this as a survival of Brāhmaṇa-period cosmogonic vocabulary inside a Vedāntic frame."],["Aurobindo","tapas is consciousness-force (cit-śakti) acting on itself. Brahman ripens by its own self-application; nothing external acts on it. anna is not food in the modern sense but matter-as-substrate — the densest of the seven planes Aurobindo will later enumerate. From matter emerges life (prāṇa), from life mind (manas), from mind truth-consciousness (satya), from truth the differentiated worlds (lokāḥ), and within those worlds the actions whose fruit is the immortality that returns the seeker to Brahman. The cosmogony is also the soteriology read backward."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 1.1.9","title":"The omniscient one whose tapas is knowledge","sa":["यः सर्वज्ञः सर्वविद्यस्य ज्ञानमयं तपः ।","तस्मादेतद्ब्रह्म नाम रूपमन्नं च जायते ॥ ९॥"],"iast":["yaḥ sarva-jñaḥ sarva-vid yasya jñāna-mayaṃ tapaḥ |","tasmād etad brahma nāma rūpam annaṃ ca jāyate || 9 ||"],"words":[["yaḥ","m. nom. sg. rel.","He who"],["sarva-jñaḥ","m. nom. sg. tp.","all-knowing"],["sarva-vit","m. nom. sg. tp.","all-knower (omniscient in detail)"],["yasya","m. gen. sg. rel.","whose"],["jñāna-mayam","n. nom. sg. adj. tp.","consisting of knowledge"],["tapaḥ","n. nom. sg.","tapas"],["tasmāt","m. abl. sg.","from him"],["etat","n. nom. sg.","this"],["brahma","n. nom. sg.","Brahman (here = Hiraṇyagarbha)"],["nāma","n. nom. sg.","name"],["rūpam","n. nom. sg.","form"],["annam","n. nom. sg.","food / matter"],["ca","indecl.","and"],["jāyate","3 sg. pres. of jan","is born"]],"lit":"\"He who is all-knowing, all-knower, whose tapas consists of knowledge — from him are born this Brahman (= Hiraṇyagarbha), name, form, and matter.\"","para":"The closing mantra of khaṇḍa 1.1 names the source. The akṣara of mantras 5–8 is now characterised by two qualities — sarvajña (knower of the general) and sarvavid (knower of the particular) — and its tapas is glossed as jñāna-mayam, \"consisting of knowledge.\" From this knowledge-tapas come four products: brahman (here = Hiraṇyagarbha, the cosmic mind), nāma (name), rūpa (form), and anna (matter/food). The doctrinal point: tapas is not deliberate self-mortification but the natural self-illumination of the omniscient — and from that self-illumination the entire nāma-rūpa cosmos and its substrate matter emerge.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> The distinction sarva-jña vs. sarva-vit is technical. sarva-jña = sāmānya-jñātṛ (knower of the universal / abstract); sarva-vid = viśeṣa-jñātṛ (knower of the particular / concrete). The akṣara knows both the general form and every concrete instance. Śaṅkara further distinguishes the two brahmans of the verse: the brahman of the relative clause (yaḥ sarva-jñaḥ — the para-Brahman) and the brahman of the main clause (etat brahma — here Hiraṇyagarbha, the cosmic mind). The first is the akṣara; the second is its first emanation. From the first the second comes forth."],["Sivananda","jñāna-mayaṃ tapaḥ — \"tapas whose substance is knowledge\" — is the key phrase. Ordinary tapas burns the body; the akṣara's tapas illuminates the cosmos. The lesson for the seeker: convert your tapas from mortification (kāya-kleśa) to knowing (jñāna). The same disciplinary energy that withers the flesh, redirected, irradiates the mind."],["Krishnananda","The mantra completes the cosmogony of 1.1.7–8 by naming the products: brahman (= Hiraṇyagarbha), name, form, matter. The pair nāma-rūpa is here introduced for the first time in the Muṇḍaka and will recur as a key term in 3.2.8 (yathā nadyaḥ syandamānāḥ samudre 'staṃ gacchanti nāma-rūpe vihāya — \"as rivers reach the ocean, abandoning name-and-form\"). The opening cosmogony and the closing simile of liberation thus frame the whole Upaniṣad around nāma-rūpa: from the akṣara it emerges; into the akṣara it dissolves."],["Deussen","This closes the first khaṇḍa. Deussen notes the structural parallel with Bṛhad-Āraṇyaka 1.4.7 (taddhedaṃ tarhy avyākṛtam āsīt — \"this was then unmanifest\") followed by the emergence of nāma-rūpa. The Muṇḍaka compresses BĀU's longer cosmogony into a single śloka by identifying the akṣara's tapas with its knowledge."],["Aurobindo","sarva-jña / sarva-vid is the Upaniṣad's most precise definition of consciousness. Knowledge of the universal (sarva-jña) and knowledge of the particular (sarva-vid) are not two knowings but one — the akṣara knows the particular through the universal and the universal through the particular without succession. This is for Aurobindo the supramental cognition: simultaneous comprehensive-and-articulated knowing. From it Hiraṇyagarbha (the cosmic mind) is born, and from Hiraṇyagarbha name-form-matter; the cosmos is a thought of the all-knower thought into being."]],"notes":["End of khaṇḍa 1.1."]}]},{"t":"Muṇḍaka 1, Khaṇḍa 2 — The seven tongues of fire and the ferries of sacrifice (13 mantras)","u":[{"ref":"Muṇḍaka 1, Khaṇḍa 2","title":"The seven tongues of fire and the ferries of sacrifice (13 mantras)","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["Khaṇḍa 1.2 turns to the aparā — the karma-kāṇḍa — and assesses it. The first six verses describe the proper performance of the agnihotra: the fire-altar, the seven tongues of flame, the rewards of correct offerings, the deva-yāna (path of the gods) by which a correct sacrificer travels. Verses 7–10 then deliver the most uncompromising critique of ritualism anywhere in the Veda. Verses 11–13 conclude with the alternative — renounce, take samit-pāṇi to a guru, and receive brahmavidyā."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Mantra 1.2.1","title":"The mantras the seers saw: practise them, this is your path","sa":["तदेतत्सत्यं मन्त्रेषु कर्माणि कवयो यान्यपश्यंस्तानि त्रेतायां बहुधा संततानि ।","तान्याचरथ नियतं सत्यकामा एष वः पन्थाः सुकृतस्य लोके ॥ १॥"],"iast":["tad etat satyaṃ mantreṣu karmāṇi kavayo yāny apaśyaṃs tāni tretāyāṃ bahudhā saṃtatāni |","tāny ācaratha niyataṃ satya-kāmā eṣa vaḥ panthāḥ su-kṛtasya loke || 1 ||"],"words":[["tat etat","n. nom. sg.","this same"],["satyam","n. nom. sg.","is true / is the truth"],["mantreṣu","m. loc. pl.","in the mantras"],["karmāṇi","n. acc. pl.","the rites"],["kavayaḥ","m. nom. pl.","the seers"],["yāni","n. acc. pl. rel.","which"],["apaśyan","3 pl. impf. of paś","saw"],["tāni","n. nom. pl.","those"],["tretāyām","f. loc. sg.","in the Tretā(-yuga; or in the three-fire arrangement)"],["bahudhā","indecl.","in many ways"],["saṃtatāni","n. nom. pl. pp.","spread out, extended"],["tāni","n. acc. pl.","those"],["ācaratha","2 pl. impv. of ā-car","practise!"],["niyatam","indecl. adv.","constantly"],["satya-kāmāḥ","m. nom. pl. bv.","O you who desire truth"],["eṣaḥ","m. nom. sg.","this"],["vaḥ","m. gen. pl.","(is) your"],["panthāḥ","m. nom. sg.","path"],["su-kṛtasya","n. gen. sg.","of meritorious deed"],["loke","m. loc. sg.","in the world"]],"lit":"\"This is the truth: the rites that the seers saw in the mantras are extended in many ways in the Tretā (or: in the three-fire arrangement). Practise them constantly, O you who desire truth — this is your path of meritorious deed in (this) world.\"","para":"The text first acknowledges the aparā at its best. The mantras the kavi-seers saw in their vision are extended (saṃtata) in many ways in the Tretā-yuga (or: in the three-fire altar arrangement — tretā as the three-fire system, a technical reading Śaṅkara preferred). These rites are true at their level (tad etat satyam) — practise them constantly. For one who desires the proper fruits of meritorious action (sukṛtasya loke), this is the path. The mantra grants the karma-kāṇḍa full validity at its own level before the upcoming demolition.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> The ambiguity of tretāyām is deliberate. (a) Yuga reading: \"in the Tretā-age,\" when ritual was first systematised. (b) Three-fire reading: tretā = the triad of fires (gārhapatya, āhavanīya, dakṣiṇāgni), so tretāyāṃ = \"in the three-fire altar arrangement.\" Śaṅkara prefers (b) as the older meaning. Either way, the verse grants that the mantras are truth at their level (tad etat satyam) and that practising them is a legitimate path. The aparā is not falsehood; it is real-at-its-level. The qualification will come in the next several verses: the path leads only to sukṛta — meritorious fruit — and meritorious fruit is exhaustible."],["Sivananda","The opening word tad etat satyam is critical: the Upaniṣad does not dismiss ritual. It says: yes, ritual is true. But true at what level? Read the next twelve verses to find out. Sivananda's commentary at this point insists on the gracious arc of the Muṇḍaka — it praises before it criticises, in the manner of a teacher who first credits the student's diligence and then shows its limit."],["Krishnananda","The technical sense of tretā (three fires) is what makes the verse hang together. The next mantra (1.2.2) elaborates on which fire receives which offering; this would be inexplicable if tretā here meant only the historical Tretā-yuga. Krishnananda reads the verse as the canonical introduction to the agnihotra-mīmāṃsā for the karma-kāṇḍa-minded brahmacārin."],["Deussen","The cosmic/yuga sense of tretā is, for Deussen, the older one; the three-fire reading is a learned re-reading by later Brāhmaṇa-period editors. Deussen translates: \"in the Tretā-age were these manifoldly stretched out.\" His reason: the verb saṃtatāni (extended-out) better fits a temporal spreading than a spatial arrangement of three fires."],["Aurobindo","tretāyām is best translated \"in the three-form arrangement\" — but the three forms are not the three altar-fires of the brahmins; they are the three planes of consciousness (annamaya, prāṇamaya, manomaya) on which the karmic action takes effect. The Vedic mantras the kavis saw were seen in the inner threefold cosmos; their outer extension into ritual is a derivative form. Aurobindo's gloss: \"these are the mantras the seers saw, threefold-extended in the planes of being.\""]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 1.2.2","title":"When the flame flickers, then make the offerings","sa":["यदा लेलायते ह्यर्चिः समिद्धे हव्यवाहने ।","तदाऽऽज्यभागावन्तरेणाऽऽहुतीः प्रतिपादयेत् ॥ २॥"],"iast":["yadā lelāyate hy arciḥ samiddhe havya-vāhane |","tadā''jya-bhāgāv antareṇā''hutīḥ pratipādayet || 2 ||"],"words":[["yadā","indecl.","when"],["lelāyate","3 sg. pres. intens. of lā","flickers, plays"],["hi","indecl.","indeed"],["arciḥ","f. nom. sg.","the flame"],["samiddhe","m. loc. sg. pp.","in (the fire when it is) well-kindled"],["havya-vāhane","m. loc. sg. tp.","in the offering-carrier (= fire)"],["tadā","indecl.","then"],["ājya-bhāgau","m. acc. du. tp.","(at) the two ghee-portions"],["antareṇa","indecl. prep.","between"],["āhutīḥ","f. acc. pl.","offerings"],["pratipādayet","3 sg. pot. caus. of prati-pad","should present, should offer"]],"lit":"\"When the flame plays/flickers brightly in the well-kindled fire (the offering-carrier), then, between the two ghee-portions, one should present the offerings.\"","para":"A purely ritual instruction: at the moment when the well-kindled agni-fire is flickering (lelāyate, intensive — \"is in active play\"), and between the two ājya-bhāga (the two preliminary ghee-libations), the priest should present the main āhutis (oblations). This is technical śrauta-sūtra-level prescription. Its function in the Upaniṣad: to show that the text takes the ritual seriously — it knows the exact moment of correct offering — before going on to relativise it.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> The technical terms ājya-bhāgau and āhutīḥ correspond to standard agnihotra-vidhāna: at the iṣṭi the priest makes two preliminary ghee-pourings (the ājya-bhāgas to Agni and Soma), and between them or just after them places the main offerings to the deities of the iṣṭi. The verse is doctrinally inert — it merely shows the Upaniṣad's command of ritual detail. Śaṅkara's whole commentary on this verse is two sentences."],["Sivananda","The intensive verb lelāyate is the heart of the verse — the fire is not merely burning, it is dancing, playing. Sivananda extends this allegorically: offer to your inner fire (the antar-agni of digestion, of consciousness) only when it is alive and dancing — that is, in moments of bright awareness, not in dullness. The outer rite teaches an inner timing."],["Krishnananda","The mantra is best read as a technical preface to 1.2.3 (the disqualifications of bad agnihotra). The Upaniṣad establishes the correct procedure here so that in the next verse it can list what goes wrong when the procedure is not followed."],["Deussen","One of the few verses in the Upaniṣad that is purely ritualistic with no doctrinal subtext. Deussen treats it as a quoted Brāhmaṇa-passage interpolated into the text — but acknowledges this is speculative; it could equally be original."],["Aurobindo","The flickering of the flame (lelāyate) is the symbol of inner aspiration. Just as the priest offers when the flame plays, so the inner sādhaka offers his thoughts when the inner aspiration plays. The verse's true sense is psychological even if its surface is ritual."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 1.2.3","title":"Disqualified agnihotra destroys seven worlds","sa":["यस्याग्निहोत्रमदर्शमपौर्णमास-मचातुर्मास्यमनाग्रयणमतिथिवर्जितं च ।","अहुतमवैश्वदेवमविधिना हुत-मासप्तमांस्तस्य लोकान् हिनस्ति ॥ ३॥"],"iast":["yasyāgni-hotram a-darśam a-paurṇa-māsam a-cāturmāsyam a-nāgrayaṇam atithi-varjitaṃ ca |","ahutam a-vaiśva-devam a-vidhinā hutam ā-saptamāṃs tasya lokān hinasti || 3 ||"],"words":[["yasya","m. gen. sg. rel.","whose"],["agni-hotram","n. nom. sg.","agnihotra (fire-offering)"],["a-darśam","n. nom. sg. bv.","without (the offering of) the new-moon day"],["a-paurṇa-māsam","n. nom. sg. bv.","without the full-moon (offering)"],["a-cāturmāsyam","n. nom. sg. bv.","without the four-monthly (offering)"],["a-nāgrayaṇam","n. nom. sg. bv.","without the new-crop (offering)"],["atithi-varjitam","n. nom. sg. tp.","destitute of (offering to) guests"],["ca","indecl.","and"],["ahutam","n. nom. sg.","unoffered (the daily āhuti omitted)"],["a-vaiśva-devam","n. nom. sg. bv.","without (the offering to) the Viśve-devas"],["a-vidhinā","indecl. instr.","without (proper) procedure"],["hutam","n. nom. sg.","(when) offered"],["ā-saptamān","m. acc. pl. adv.","up to the seventh (world / generation)"],["tasya","m. gen. sg.","of him"],["lokān","m. acc. pl.","the worlds"],["hinasti","3 sg. pres. of hiṃs","destroys"]],"lit":"\"He whose agnihotra is without the new-moon offering, without the full-moon, without the cāturmāsya, without the new-crop offering, deprived of the guest-offering, unoffered, without the vaiśvadeva offering, or offered without rule — (such an agnihotra) destroys up to his seven worlds (or: his seventh generation).\"","para":"A catalogue of the eight ways an agnihotra can be defective: missing the new-moon offering (darśa), missing the full-moon (paurṇa-māsa), missing the four-monthly (cāturmāsya), missing the new-harvest (āgrayaṇa), missing the guest-offering (atithi), the daily fire-offering itself omitted (ahuta), missing the vaiśvadeva, or performed contrary to rule (avidhinā hutam). Any of these defects: the householder's seven worlds (or seven generations of descendants) are destroyed.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> ā-saptamān admits two readings: (a) seven generations of descendants — the householder's lineage is ruined for seven generations; (b) seven worlds — the seven upper lokas the sacrificer would have reached are forfeited. Śaṅkara prefers (b) — the worlds, not the descendants. The lesson is not minor: a defective agnihotra is not merely worth less; it actively destroys the rewards the householder would have earned by other rites. The aparā is exacting: half-performed ritual is worse than no ritual."],["Sivananda","The eightfold list shows how demanding the karma-kāṇḍa actually is. The householder's daily duty is not just \"do agnihotra\" — it is to maintain the new-moon offering, the full-moon offering, the four-monthly seasonal offering, the new-crop offering, hospitality, the vaiśvadeva, etc., as an integrated annual cycle. To miss one is to destroy the value of all. Sivananda treats this as the verse that, by exposing the burden of the karma-kāṇḍa, prepares the listener to consider the easier path of jñāna in verses 7 onward."],["Krishnananda","The verse is the Upaniṣad's most precise statement of the karma-kāṇḍa's brittleness. Karma is a chained sequence: any link's failure undoes the chain. This brittleness is one of the karma-kāṇḍa's deepest defects (the others, named in 1.2.7, are its boat-like fragility and its time-boundedness). Krishnananda treats this verse as the technical preparation for the polemic of 1.2.7–10."],["Deussen","A standard śrauta-sūtra catalogue. The eight defects map almost exactly to those listed in the Āpastamba and Baudhāyana śrauta-sūtras. Deussen treats the verse as Brāhmaṇa-period material absorbed into the Upaniṣad's text."],["Aurobindo","The verse is a precise critique of partial sādhanā. Spiritual practice, like agnihotra, fails if it is partial — daily without seasonal, outward without inward, formal without faithful. The seven worlds destroyed are the seven planes of the inner being; the avidhinā-hutam (\"offered without rule\") is the deepest defect — a practice done without true principle is more destructive than no practice. Aurobindo glosses: \"avidhi is more dangerous than akarma.\""]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 1.2.4","title":"The seven tongues of the fire","sa":["काली कराली च मनोजवा च सुलोहिता या च सुधूम्रवर्णा ।","स्फुलिङ्गिनी विश्वरुची च देवी लेलायमाना इति सप्त जिह्वाः ॥ ४॥"],"iast":["kālī karālī ca mano-javā ca su-lohitā yā ca su-dhūmra-varṇā |","sphuliṅginī viśva-rucī ca devī lelāyamānā iti sapta jihvāḥ || 4 ||"],"words":[["kālī","f. nom. sg.","Kālī (the black one)"],["karālī","f. nom. sg.","Karālī (the terrible one)"],["ca","indecl.","and"],["mano-javā","f. nom. sg. bv.","Manojavā (\"mind-swift\")"],["su-lohitā","f. nom. sg. bv.","Sulohitā (\"deep-red\")"],["yā","f. nom. sg. rel.","she who"],["su-dhūmra-varṇā","f. nom. sg. bv.","Sudhūmravarṇā (\"smoke-coloured\")"],["sphuliṅginī","f. nom. sg.","Sphuliṅginī (\"sparking\")"],["viśva-rucī","f. nom. sg. bv.","Viśvarucī (\"all-lustrous\")"],["ca","indecl.","and"],["devī","f. nom. sg.","goddess (i.e., the flame as goddess)"],["lelāyamānāḥ","f. nom. pl. pres. part. intens.","(all) playing/dancing"],["iti","indecl.","thus (closing the list)"],["sapta","indecl. num.","seven"],["jihvāḥ","f. nom. pl.","tongues"]],"lit":"\"Kālī, Karālī, Manojavā, Sulohitā, Sudhūmravarṇā, Sphuliṅginī, Viśvarucī — these seven goddess-tongues are flickering.\"","para":"The classical list of the seven flames of the sacrificial fire — each treated as a goddess (devī) and named for her colour or character. Kālī (Black), Karālī (Terrible), Mano-javā (Mind-swift), Su-lohitā (Deep-red), Su-dhūmra-varṇā (Smoke-coloured), Sphuliṅginī (Sparking), Viśva-rucī (All-lustrous). These seven tongues are lelāyamānāḥ — the same intensive verb of 1.2.2 — actively playing. They are the means by which the sacrificer's offerings are transported to the gods.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> The seven tongues are not literally seven distinct flames but seven aspects of the one fire, named for their colour-of-light at different stages of burning. Kālī is the deepest darkness at the heart; Karālī the wild perimeter; Mano-javā the licking outer edge that moves with mind-speed; Su-lohitā the steady ember; Su-dhūmra-varṇā the smoky phase; Sphuliṅginī the spark-throwing phase; Viśva-rucī the all-illuminating climax. Śaṅkara's interest in the verse is doctrinal-technical: the seven names give the priest the vocabulary for the various visible states of his fire and thus a means of timing the offering."],["Sivananda","The list of seven goddess-flames is also a list of seven moods of the inner agni. Kālī = the deep-still meditative mood; Karālī = the fierce purifying mood; Mano-javā = the swift response of awareness; Su-lohitā = warmth of devotion; Su-dhūmra = sluggish meditation (\"smoky\"); Sphuliṅginī = bursts of insight; Viśva-rucī = full luminous awareness. The seer who sustains the inner agni traverses all seven phases."],["Krishnananda","The seven-fold count anticipates later tantric and Yoga-Upaniṣadic seven-chakra schemes — but is older and structurally different. Krishnananda emphasises that the Muṇḍaka's seven-fold is not yet anatomised (no spinal localisation); it is purely a fire-phase phenomenology. Later Yoga-Upaniṣads (Yoga-Cūḍāmaṇi, Brahma-Vidyā) inherit the seven-fold count and re-map it to the spine; the Muṇḍaka stops short of that step."],["Deussen","Lists the same seven in Atharva-Veda 19.22.6 and 19.23.6. The Muṇḍaka is here directly quoting Atharvan ritual vocabulary. The Atharvan source treats them as deities of the offering-fire; the Muṇḍaka inherits this."],["Aurobindo","The seven tongues are seven powers of consciousness-fire (cit-agni). Each performs a distinct work on the offered thought: Kālī absorbs into the unmanifest, Karālī terrifies and purifies, Mano-javā transmits at thought-speed, Su-lohitā warms with rasa, Su-dhūmra obscures (as a teaching), Sphuliṅginī releases sparks of new awareness, Viśva-rucī illuminates the whole. The sacrificer who knows all seven knows how to direct his inner offering by each."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 1.2.5","title":"The correct sacrificer is borne to Brahma-loka by the seven flames","sa":["एतेषु यश्चरते भ्राजमानेषु यथाकालं चाहुतयो ह्याददायन् ।","तं नयन्त्येताः सूर्यस्य रश्मयो यत्र देवानां पतिरेकोऽधिवासः ॥ ५॥"],"iast":["eteṣu yaś carate bhrājamāneṣu yathā-kālaṃ cāhutayo hy ādadāyan |","taṃ nayanty etāḥ sūryasya raśmayo yatra devānāṃ patir eko 'dhi-vāsaḥ || 5 ||"],"words":[["eteṣu","f. loc. pl.","in these (seven)"],["yaḥ","m. nom. sg. rel.","he who"],["carate","3 sg. pres. mid. of car","operates / acts"],["bhrājamāneṣu","f. loc. pl. pres. part. of bhrāj","(in them when) shining"],["yathā-kālam","indecl. adv.","at the proper time"],["ca","indecl.","and"],["āhutayaḥ","f. nom. pl.","(when) the offerings"],["hi","indecl.","indeed"],["ādadāyan","3 pl. pres. red. of ā-dā","take up / receive"],["tam","m. acc. sg.","him"],["nayanti","3 pl. pres. of nī","lead"],["etāḥ","f. nom. pl.","these"],["sūryasya","m. gen. sg.","of the sun"],["raśmayaḥ","m. nom. pl.","rays"],["yatra","indecl.","where"],["devānām","m. gen. pl.","of the gods"],["patiḥ","m. nom. sg.","the lord"],["ekaḥ","m. nom. sg.","the one"],["adhi-vāsaḥ","m. nom. sg.","(has his) supreme abode"]],"lit":"\"He who acts among these (seven) when they are blazing, (his) offerings taking him up at the proper time — these rays of the sun lead him to where the lord of the gods has his one supreme abode.\"","para":"The correct ritualist's recompense. He who carries out his sacrifice among the seven flames when they are bhrājamānāḥ (\"blazing\"), at the proper time (yathā-kālam), and whose offerings are received (ādadāyan, an intensive-reduplicated form) — such a one is taken up by the sun's rays themselves to the abode of the lord of gods (devānāṃ pati = Hiraṇyagarbha). This is the canonical description of the deva-yāna, the \"path of the gods,\" by which a proper sacrificer travels to Brahma-loka.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> The verse describes the bright-half path (śukla-gati) familiar from Chāndogya 5.10 and BĀU 6.2 — the sacrificer's subtle body is borne after death by the smoke of the sacrificial fire upward through ahar (day) → śukla-pakṣa (bright half-month) → uttarāyaṇa (northern course) → year → sun → moon → vidyut → brahma-loka. The Muṇḍaka here abbreviates the full sequence to its endpoint: sūryasya raśmayo nayanti — the sun's rays themselves carry him. Śaṅkara emphasises that this is the highest fruit available to the karma-kāṇḍin and that it is still not mokṣa — brahma-loka is itself temporary."],["Sivananda","The verse describes the apex of ritual achievement and is therefore important to know about even as the seeker chooses jñāna over karma: brahma-loka is the maximum ritual reward, and it is not enough. The deva-yāna is real and it is exhausting in two senses — exhausting to maintain (1.2.3 is the price) and exhaustible in its result (brahma-loka itself ends with the cosmic dissolution). Krama-mukti from brahma-loka is possible but is not the direct mokṣa of the jñānin."],["Krishnananda","This is the high-water mark of the karma-kāṇḍa within the Muṇḍaka. The Upaniṣad will, from 1.2.7 onward, undermine even this. Krishnananda's reading: the verse describes the genuinely admirable accomplishment of the proper ritualist — and then asks the listener to feel, in the next verse, the cosmic invitation that even this is not the highest."],["Deussen","Compare BĀU 6.2.15 (the bright path), Chāndogya 5.10.1, BG 8.24 (Kṛṣṇa's enumeration of the bright path). The Muṇḍaka's version is the most compressed of all four."],["Aurobindo","The sun-rays that bear the sacrificer are not physical sunlight; they are the rays of consciousness (the cetana-raśmis) which the upward-aspiring inner being follows after death. The devānām patir ekaḥ — the one lord of the gods — is Hiraṇyagarbha, the cosmic Mind. The sacrificer's destination is to dwell with the cosmic Mind — a real but not ultimate accomplishment."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 1.2.6","title":"The flames address the sacrificer: 'come, come — this is the auspicious Brahma-world built of your good works'","sa":["एह्येहीति तमाहुतयः सुवर्चसः सूर्यस्य रश्मिभिर्यजमानं वहन्ति ।","प्रियां वाचमभिवदन्त्योऽर्चयन्त्य एष वः पुण्यः सुकृतो ब्रह्मलोकः ॥ ६॥"],"iast":["ehy ehīti tam āhutayaḥ su-varcasaḥ sūryasya raśmibhir yajamānaṃ vahanti |","priyāṃ vācam abhi-vadantyo 'rcayantya eṣa vaḥ puṇyaḥ sukṛto brahma-lokaḥ || 6 ||"],"words":[["ehi ehi","2 sg. impv. (doubled) of ā-i","come, come!"],["iti","indecl.","thus (saying)"],["tam","m. acc. sg.","him"],["āhutayaḥ","f. nom. pl.","the offerings"],["su-varcasaḥ","f. nom. pl. bv.","of great lustre"],["sūryasya","m. gen. sg.","of the sun"],["raśmibhiḥ","m. instr. pl.","by the rays"],["yajamānam","m. acc. sg. pres. part. mid.","the sacrificer (lit. \"the one who sacrifices for himself\")"],["vahanti","3 pl. pres. of vah","carry"],["priyām","f. acc. sg.","dear, pleasant"],["vācam","f. acc. sg.","speech"],["abhi-vadantyaḥ","f. nom. pl. pres. part. mid.","(while) speaking"],["arcayantyaḥ","f. nom. pl. pres. part.","(while) praising"],["eṣaḥ","m. nom. sg.","this"],["vaḥ","m. gen. pl.","your"],["puṇyaḥ","m. nom. sg.","pure, holy"],["sukṛtaḥ","m. nom. sg.","made by good works"],["brahma-lokaḥ","m. nom. sg.","world of Brahmā"]],"lit":"\"'Come! Come!' — saying this, the lustrous offerings carry the sacrificer by the rays of the sun, speaking sweet words and praising: 'This is your holy Brahma-loka, made of (your) good deeds.'\"","para":"The completion of the deva-yāna picture: the offerings themselves, personified as luminous goddesses (āhutayaḥ suvarcasaḥ), call to the sacrificer — ehi! ehi! (\"come! come!\"). They bear him up by the sun's rays, speaking sweet words and praising him, and present him with the destination: \"This is your holy Brahma-world, the one your good works have made.\" The sacrificer has reached the maximum dwelling-place granted to ritual.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> The cordiality of the verse is striking: the offerings, far from being burdensome, are loving guides who praise the sacrificer and call him home. The phrase eṣa vaḥ puṇyaḥ sukṛto brahma-lokaḥ — \"this is your holy Brahma-world built of your good works\" — is the highest commendation the aparā can offer. Śaṅkara emphasises sukṛtaḥ — \"made by good deeds\": brahma-loka is constructed; it is karma's product; and as a product it must one day be unmade. The qualifier sukṛtaḥ is the verse's hidden critique — what is built must one day fall."],["Sivananda","The verse is, in Sivananda's reading, the most generous statement of the value of meritorious action in the Upaniṣad corpus. Even as the Muṇḍaka is about to turn against ritualism, it gives ritualism here its richest possible recompense. The lesson: do not denigrate the ritualist; honour him for what he has accomplished — and then point past it."],["Krishnananda","The offerings' direct speech (\"come! come!\") is the only direct speech in this khaṇḍa and one of very few in the whole Muṇḍaka. Its insertion here is dramatic: the Upaniṣad pauses, lets the ritual-path speak in its own voice, and only then begins the critique. Krishnananda treats the dramatic pause as a hermeneutic gift — the Upaniṣad will not be accused of dismissing what it has not first granted full voice."],["Deussen","Compare the post-mortem journey-description in BĀU 6.2.15–16, where the deceased is addressed by various cosmic powers along the path. The Muṇḍaka's version is more compressed and more elegant."],["Aurobindo","The praising offerings, the sweet words, the sukṛto brahma-loka — all this is the karma-path at its absolute best. And yet, in the verse that immediately follows, the Upaniṣad will call these very ferries plavāḥ adṛḍhāḥ — \"unstable rafts.\" Aurobindo highlights the deliberate contrast: the verse gives the karma-path full poetic glory before the demolition of 1.2.7. The Muṇḍaka wants the listener to feel, viscerally, that even this poetic peak is not enough."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 1.2.7","title":"These (sacrifices) are unstable ferries: those who delight in them go to old age and death again","sa":["प्लवा ह्येते अदृढा यज्ञरूपा अष्टादशोक्तमवरं येषु कर्म ।","एतच्छ्रेयो येऽभिनन्दन्ति मूढा जरामृत्युं ते पुनरेवापि यन्ति ॥ ७॥"],"iast":["plavā hy ete a-dṛḍhā yajña-rūpā aṣṭā-daśoktam avaraṃ yeṣu karma |","etac chreyo ye 'bhinandanti mūḍhā jarā-mṛtyuṃ te punar evāpi yanti || 7 ||"],"words":[["plavāḥ","m. nom. pl.","ferries, rafts"],["hi","indecl.","indeed"],["ete","m. nom. pl.","these"],["a-dṛḍhāḥ","m. nom. pl. bv.","unstable, unfirm"],["yajña-rūpāḥ","m. nom. pl. bv.","of sacrifice-form"],["aṣṭā-daśa","indecl. num.","eighteen"],["uktam","n. nom. sg. pp.","spoken-of"],["avaram","n. nom. sg.","lower"],["yeṣu","m. loc. pl. rel.","in which"],["karma","n. nom. sg.","action / rite"],["etat","n. nom. sg.","this"],["śreyaḥ","n. nom. sg.","good, the highest"],["ye","m. nom. pl. rel.","those who"],["abhinandanti","3 pl. pres. of abhi-nand","rejoice in, applaud"],["mūḍhāḥ","m. nom. pl.","deluded ones"],["jarā-mṛtyum","m. acc. sg. dv.","old age and death"],["te","m. nom. pl.","they"],["punaḥ eva","indecl.","again indeed"],["api","indecl.","also"],["yanti","3 pl. pres. of i","go"]],"lit":"\"Indeed, these (sacrifices), of the form of ritual, are unstable rafts; the lower karma — said to be eighteen-fold — is held in them. Those deluded ones who rejoice in this as the highest, again-and-again go to old age and death.\"","para":"The pivot of the whole Muṇḍaka. The same offerings that 1.2.6 described as luminous and praising are here renamed: plavāḥ adṛḍhāḥ — unstable rafts. Sacrifices are the rafts on which the karma-kāṇḍin attempts to cross the ocean of saṃsāra; but these rafts are not firm. The aṣṭādaśa karma — the \"eighteen-fold lower action,\" i.e., the eighteen ritual offices of a Vedic śrauta-sacrifice (16 ṛtviks + the yajamāna + his wife) — is housed in them. Those who praise this whole system as śreyas (the highest good) are mūḍhāḥ — deluded — and they return again-and-again to old age and death. The same Upaniṣad that praised the karma-path in 1.2.1–6 here demolishes it.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> The mantra is the doctrinal centrepiece of the khaṇḍa. The image plavāḥ adṛḍhāḥ — \"unstable rafts\" — is critical: ritualism is a raft, not a bridge. A raft can cross still water but breaks in rough seas; ritualism can cross the still water of the present life but breaks at death. The aṣṭādaśa (eighteen) is technical: the 16 ṛtvik priests of a soma-sacrifice (4 Hotṛ, 4 Adhvaryu, 4 Udgātṛ, 4 Brahmā groups) plus the yajamāna and his wife. Śaṅkara emphasises punar eva api yanti — they go again (after returning from svarga, the merit exhausted) to old age and death. The bright path of 1.2.5 is therefore real but exhaustible: brahma-loka is a temporary abode, not the deathless one."],["Sivananda","The image of the unstable raft is the Upaniṣad's most enduring image of ritualism. A raft is fine for the river; the seeker must cross the ocean. The verse's force is not against ritual as such but against ritual-as-final-resort. Sivananda's gloss: \"perform the rites; do not depend on them.\""],["Krishnananda","The four defects of the karma-kāṇḍa that the Upaniṣad implies in this verse: (a) brittleness (1.2.3: any defect destroys it); (b) temporariness (1.2.5 followed by 1.2.7: the bright path leads to brahma-loka, which is itself exhausted); (c) instability (this verse: ferries break); (d) deceptive completeness (this verse: those who think it is śreyas are mūḍhāḥ). Krishnananda calls these the catuṣ-doṣa of karma."],["Deussen","The strongest anti-ritualist polemic anywhere in the Veda. Deussen translates plavā adṛḍhāḥ as \"morsche Flösse\" (rotten rafts) — the strongest German cognate. He notes the irony of using an Atharvan Upaniṣad (the Veda most associated with ritual magic) as the locus of the sharpest critique. The aṣṭādaśa = 16 priests + yajamāna + wife is, for Deussen, conclusive evidence that the Muṇḍaka is post-Brāhmaṇa, since the 16-priest scheme is itself a Brāhmaṇa-period systematisation."],["Aurobindo","The image of the unstable raft is, for Aurobindo, the Muṇḍaka's most important contribution to the philosophy of practice. Spiritual disciplines, including the most prestigious — Vedic ritual, the highest aparā vidyā — are rafts: structures of finite skill used to traverse a finite distance. Treated as ultimate, they fail. Treated as means, they serve. The seeker's task is to use the raft, then disembark — neither despising it nor clinging to it."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 1.2.8","title":"Wandering in ignorance, calling themselves wise: blind led by blind","sa":["अविद्यायामन्तरे वर्तमानाः स्वयं धीराः पण्डितंमन्यमानाः ।","जङ्घन्यमानाः परियन्ति मूढा अन्धेनैव नीयमाना यथान्धाः ॥ ८॥"],"iast":["avidyāyām antare vartamānāḥ svayaṃ dhīrāḥ paṇḍitam-manyamānāḥ |","jaṅghanyamānāḥ pariyanti mūḍhā andhenaiva nīyamānā yathāndhāḥ || 8 ||"],"words":[["avidyāyām","f. loc. sg.","in ignorance"],["antare","n. loc. sg.","in the midst"],["vartamānāḥ","m. nom. pl. pres. part. mid.","abiding"],["svayam","indecl.","themselves"],["dhīrāḥ","m. nom. pl. adj.","wise"],["paṇḍitam-manyamānāḥ","m. nom. pl. bv.","thinking-themselves-learned"],["jaṅghanyamānāḥ","m. nom. pl. pres. part. intens. of han","being struck again-and-again"],["pariyanti","3 pl. pres. of pari-i","wander about"],["mūḍhāḥ","m. nom. pl.","the deluded"],["andhena","m. instr. sg.","by a blind man"],["eva","indecl.","indeed"],["nīyamānāḥ","m. nom. pl. pres. part. pass.","(being) led"],["yathā","indecl.","as"],["andhāḥ","m. nom. pl.","blind men"]],"lit":"\"Abiding in the midst of ignorance, considering themselves wise, thinking-themselves-learned, deluded ones wander, again-and-again struck, like blind men led by the blind.\"","para":"The image is from the standard repertory of Upaniṣadic critique — and from this Upaniṣad will be borrowed by the Kaṭha (2.1.5, where it appears in almost identical wording). The ritualists abide in avidyā but mistake themselves for the wise (svayaṃ dhīrāḥ) and the learned (paṇḍitam-manyamānāḥ). They are struck again and again (jaṅghanyamānāḥ, intensive of han — being repeatedly beaten) by the troubles of saṃsāra, yet keep going in circles, like blind men following a blind guide.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> The same image used by Kaṭha 2.5 (\"the deluded, deeming themselves wise…\"), here in its original Muṇḍaka form. The crucial phrase is paṇḍitam-manyamānāḥ — \"thinking-themselves-learned.\" The defect is not ignorance per se but self-satisfied ignorance — the ignorance that has acquired the markers of learning (Vedic recitation, ritual expertise) and so feels itself complete. Such complacency is the hardest to break, harder than raw ignorance. The śloka is the Muṇḍaka's most pointed psychological observation."],["Sivananda","The verse is the warning to scholars. Learning, treated as completion, becomes the densest form of avidyā. Sivananda's daily-check question to the seeker: \"Do I consider myself learned? Then I am the one this verse is about.\" The remedy: serve a teacher who will not flatter, in whose presence one's complacency cannot survive."],["Krishnananda","The compound paṇḍitam-manyamānāḥ is grammatically interesting — paṇḍitam is masc. acc. sg., not nom. pl. agreeing with the subject, because manyamāna takes accusative complement (\"thinking [oneself] as paṇḍita\"). The construction marks a self-misidentification, not merely a self-attribution of a quality. The technical accusative makes the verse's psychology more precise."],["Deussen","Compare Kaṭha 2.5 (avidyāyām antare vartamānāḥ — identical opening pāda; the rest of the verse is similar but not identical). Deussen takes the Muṇḍaka as the earlier source and the Kaṭha as the borrower, on the strength of contextual fit: the verse fits the Muṇḍaka's anti-ritualist polemic exactly; in the Kaṭha it is somewhat displaced."],["Aurobindo","The self-satisfied learning of the karma-kāṇḍin is the Upaniṣad's most precise psychological diagnosis. Aurobindo treats it as the besetting trap of all spiritual learning: every level of attainment generates the temptation to mistake itself for the goal. The praiseworthy ritualist of 1.2.5 risks becoming the complacent learned-fool of 1.2.8. The Upaniṣad inserts the warning at exactly the point where complacency would naturally set in."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 1.2.9","title":"Many-pathed in ignorance, calling themselves fulfilled: they fall when their world is exhausted","sa":["अविद्यायां बहुधा वर्तमाना वयं कृतार्था इत्यभिमन्यन्ति बालाः ।","यत्कर्मिणो न प्रवेदयन्ति रागा-त्तेनातुराः क्षीणलोकाश्च्यवन्ते ॥ ९॥"],"iast":["avidyāyāṃ bahudhā vartamānā vayaṃ kṛtārthā ity abhimanyanti bālāḥ |","yat karmiṇo na pravedayanti rāgāt tenāturāḥ kṣīṇa-lokāś cyavante || 9 ||"],"words":[["avidyāyām","f. loc. sg.","in ignorance"],["bahudhā","indecl.","in many ways"],["vartamānāḥ","m. nom. pl. pres. part. mid.","abiding"],["vayam","1 pl. pron.","we"],["kṛta-arthāḥ","m. nom. pl. bv.","(are) ones-whose-aim-is-accomplished"],["iti","indecl.","thus"],["abhimanyanti","3 pl. pres. of abhi-man","imagine, fancy"],["bālāḥ","m. nom. pl.","the immature, childish ones"],["yat","indecl. conj.","because"],["karmiṇaḥ","m. nom. pl.","the karmins (ritualists)"],["na","indecl.","not"],["pravedayanti","3 pl. pres. caus. of pra-vid","make known / declare (i.e., do not realise)"],["rāgāt","m. abl. sg.","from attachment"],["tena","n. instr. sg.","by that (reason)"],["āturāḥ","m. nom. pl.","stricken, distressed"],["kṣīṇa-lokāḥ","m. nom. pl. bv.","their world (= merit) exhausted"],["cyavante","3 pl. pres. mid. of cyu","fall (back from svarga)"]],"lit":"\"Abiding in ignorance in many ways, the immature imagine: 'We have accomplished our aim.' Because the karmins do not realise (the truth), out of attachment, they — stricken, their world exhausted — fall.\"","para":"A second image of the same predicament. The karmins multiply in avidyā: they take many forms (priest, householder, sacrificer) but in every form imagine themselves to have achieved (kṛtārthāḥ). They are bālāḥ — immature, childish in the technical Upaniṣadic sense. Because they do not pravedayanti (cause-to-be-known to themselves) the truth — and the cause of their not-realising is rāga, attachment to the fruit — they end up āturāḥ (stricken), their accumulated world-of-merit (loka) kṣīṇa (exhausted), and they cyavante (fall back).","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> The verb cyavante is technical: it is the post-svarga \"falling-back\" — the karmin reaches svarga or brahma-loka after death, exhausts his accumulated puṇya there, and falls back into a new birth on earth. This is the canonical karma-mīmāṃsā doctrine of the eternal return of the meritorious. The Muṇḍaka here uses it against itself: yes, the karmin reaches svarga; yes, his world is real; but his world is finite, exhausts itself, and he falls. The technical word kṣīṇa-lokāḥ (\"whose world-of-merit is exhausted\") is the Upaniṣad's most precise term for the karmin's eventual loss."],["Sivananda","The kṛtārthatā (sense of accomplishment) is the most seductive trap. The accomplished ritualist genuinely is accomplished — at the level of ritual. Sivananda warns: do not let your real accomplishment at one level prevent you from seeing the level above. The achieved kṛtārtha-tā is real; it is also limited."],["Krishnananda","rāgāt — \"out of attachment\" — names the cause of the karmin's failure to realise. It is not lack of knowledge but presence of attachment that blinds him. He could read the Upaniṣad and not see what it means because his rāga for the fruits of ritual is the gate that filters his comprehension. Krishnananda treats this as the verse establishing the precedence of detachment over knowledge."],["Deussen","The cyavante doctrine (post-svarga falling) is the inherited mīmāṃsā doctrine, presupposed but not argued for here — the Upaniṣad assumes its audience knows it. Deussen notes that this is one of the clearest evidences that the Muṇḍaka addresses an audience already inside the karma-mīmāṃsā: it does not need to prove that svarga is exhaustible; it appeals to that as a known fact."],["Aurobindo","The vayaṃ kṛtārthāḥ self-declaration is the most precise diagnosis of complacency-in-attainment. \"We have arrived\" is the sentence that closes the door to further arrival. The karmin's specific defect is that his arrival was real but not ultimate, and he treats it as ultimate because of rāga — attachment to the place at which he has arrived. Aurobindo links this to the more general spiritual law: every attainment must be ungripped to permit the next."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 1.2.10","title":"Glorifying iṣṭa-pūrta as supreme: they reach the back of heaven and then return","sa":["इष्टापूर्तं मन्यमाना वरिष्ठं नान्यच्छ्रेयो वेदयन्ते प्रमूढाः ।","नाकस्य पृष्ठे ते सुकृतेऽनुभूत्वेमं लोकं हीनतरं वा विशन्ति ॥ १०॥"],"iast":["iṣṭā-pūrtaṃ manyamānā variṣṭhaṃ nānyac chreyo vedayante pramūḍhāḥ |","nākasya pṛṣṭhe te sukṛte 'nubhūtvemaṃ lokaṃ hīna-taraṃ vā viśanti || 10 ||"],"words":[["iṣṭā-pūrtam","n. acc. sg. dv.","sacrifice-and-public-works (iṣṭa = sacrifice; pūrta = digging wells, building rest-houses, etc.)"],["manyamānāḥ","m. nom. pl. pres. part. mid.","considering"],["variṣṭham","n. acc. sg. adj. (sup. of varas)","the highest"],["na anyat","n. acc. sg.","not other"],["śreyaḥ","n. acc. sg.","good"],["vedayante","3 pl. pres. caus. of vid","make known to themselves / acknowledge"],["pramūḍhāḥ","m. nom. pl.","the utterly deluded"],["nākasya","m. gen. sg.","of heaven"],["pṛṣṭhe","n. loc. sg.","on the back"],["te","m. nom. pl.","they"],["sukṛte","m. loc. sg.","in (the place) made-by-good-works"],["anubhūtvā","ger. of anu-bhū","having experienced"],["imam","m. acc. sg.","this"],["lokam","m. acc. sg.","world"],["hīna-taram","m. acc. sg. comp.","or even lower"],["vā","indecl.","or"],["viśanti","3 pl. pres. of viś","enter"]],"lit":"\"Considering sacrifice-and-public-works as the highest, the utterly deluded acknowledge no other good. Having experienced (it) on the back of heaven, in the place made-by-good-works, they (then) enter this world, or one even lower.\"","para":"Iṣṭā-pūrta is the technical pair: iṣṭa = the agnihotra and other Vedic sacrifices; pūrta = public works (wells, gardens, rest-houses) that earn merit. The karmins who treat iṣṭā-pūrta as variṣṭha (the highest) are pra-mūḍhāḥ (utterly deluded — the strengthened form of mūḍha from 1.2.7). They reach nākasya pṛṣṭhe — \"the back of heaven\" — the highest svarga; they enjoy (anubhūtvā) the realm-of-good-deeds there; and then they enter this world again, or one even lower (hīna-taram). The technical word imaṃ lokam (\"this world\") plus hīna-taram is the Upaniṣad's most explicit statement of the post-svarga downward fall: not just back to earth, but possibly below.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> The pair iṣṭa-pūrta is the karma-mīmāṃsā's most comprehensive category for good works: iṣṭa = the Vedic homa sacrifices, pūrta = the post-Vedic public-charity acts (digging tanks, building bridges, planting orchards). The Upaniṣad acknowledges both as effective — the karmins do reach the back of heaven — and as exhaustible. The phrase hīna-taraṃ vā viśanti is critical: the post-svarga descent may land one in a lower birth than the previous, depending on what residual karma directs the next birth. Even the deva-yāna's highest reach (brahma-loka) is here re-asserted as exhausting; iṣṭa-pūrta's reach (svarga, lower than brahma-loka) is more obviously so."],["Sivananda","pūrta as a category is socially significant: it includes works of public utility — wells, rest-houses, gardens. The Upaniṣad takes great public works seriously and rates them at the highest level of karma. Sivananda's gloss is generous: build wells, plant trees, found schools — these are real puṇya. But do not consider them variṣṭha. Above them is the puṇya that does not exhaust."],["Krishnananda","This verse and 1.2.11 together form the choice-point of the khaṇḍa. 1.2.10 describes the cyclical karmic life-loop; 1.2.11 will describe the alternative — the forest, brahmacarya, the sun-door, the deathless. The verses are deliberately juxtaposed: here is the wheel, there is the door."],["Deussen","Iṣṭa-pūrta is a Brāhmaṇa-period category extensively documented in the Śatapatha Brāhmaṇa. The Muṇḍaka's use of it shows the Upaniṣad is engaging with the full karma-mīmāṃsā vocabulary, not merely the agnihotra. The verse extends the Upaniṣad's critique from the homa-priest to the broader category of the puṇya-householder."],["Aurobindo","nākasya pṛṣṭhe — \"on the back of heaven\" — is one of the most evocative phrases of the verse. The image: svarga is not a final destination but a high temporary perch; one rests there only on its back, not in it. The image of \"riding the back\" of heaven captures perfectly the impermanence of the sukṛta-loka. Aurobindo: \"to ride the back of heaven and dismount into the lower world — this is the very figure of karma's circular reach.\""]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 1.2.11","title":"Those who live tapas-śraddha in the forest: by the sun-door they go to where the deathless Puruṣa is","sa":["तपःश्रद्धे ये ह्युपवसन्त्यरण्ये शान्ता विद्वांसो भैक्ष्यचर्यां चरन्तः ।","सूर्यद्वारेण ते विरजाः प्रयान्ति यत्रामृतः स पुरुषो ह्यव्ययात्मा ॥ ११॥"],"iast":["tapaḥ-śraddhe ye hy upavasanty araṇye śāntā vidvāṃso bhaikṣya-caryāṃ carantaḥ |","sūrya-dvāreṇa te virajāḥ prayānti yatrāmṛtaḥ sa puruṣo hy avyayātmā || 11 ||"],"words":[["tapaḥ-śraddhe","f. loc. du. dv.","in tapas and faith"],["ye","m. nom. pl. rel.","those who"],["hi","indecl.","indeed"],["upavasanti","3 pl. pres. of upa-vas","dwell upon, live observing"],["araṇye","n. loc. sg.","in the forest"],["śāntāḥ","m. nom. pl. adj.","tranquil"],["vidvāṃsaḥ","m. nom. pl. pf. part. of vid","the wise"],["bhaikṣya-caryām","f. acc. sg. tp.","the way of begging"],["carantaḥ","m. nom. pl. pres. part. of car","practising"],["sūrya-dvāreṇa","m. instr. sg. tp.","by the sun-door"],["te","m. nom. pl.","they"],["virajāḥ","m. nom. pl. adj.","free of impurity (lit. \"without dust\")"],["prayānti","3 pl. pres. of pra-yā","go forth"],["yatra","indecl.","where"],["amṛtaḥ","m. nom. sg.","the deathless"],["saḥ","m. nom. sg.","that"],["puruṣaḥ","m. nom. sg.","Puruṣa"],["hi","indecl.","indeed"],["avyaya-ātmā","m. nom. sg. bv.","(whose) self is unchanging"]],"lit":"\"Those who, indeed, live in the forest upon tapas and faith — tranquil, learned, practising the discipline of begging — they, free of impurity, go forth by the sun-door, to where the deathless Puruṣa, whose self is unchanging, (dwells).\"","para":"The alternative to ritualism. Those who live by tapas-śraddha (austerity-and-faith) in the forest (araṇye), tranquil (śāntāḥ), wise (vidvāṃsaḥ), practising bhaikṣa-caryā (the discipline of receiving alms), go by the sun-door (sūrya-dvāra) — purified of all impurity (virajāḥ) — to the dwelling of the deathless and unchanging Puruṣa. This is the description of the forest-renouncant on the deva-yāna route, but the destination is no longer brahma-loka; it is the avyaya-ātma Puruṣa himself.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> The sūrya-dvāreṇa here is the same \"sun-door\" of Bṛhad-Āraṇyaka 6.2.15 and Chāndogya 5.10.2, but its destination is differently characterised. The karmin's deva-yāna ends in brahma-loka (where he eventually re-falls); the vānaprastha-renouncant's path ends in the avyaya-ātma Puruṣa — the unchanging Self — from which there is no return. The verse therefore implicitly distinguishes two grades of the deva-yāna: karma-bound (ending in brahma-loka, with eventual return) and jñāna-bound (ending in the Puruṣa, with no return). The doctrinal innovation is to associate the bhaikṣa-caryā renouncant with the no-return version."],["Sivananda","The fourfold qualification — śānta (tranquil), vidvān (wise), bhaikṣa-caryā-cara (lives by alms), tapaḥ-śraddhā-yutaḥ (joined with tapas and faith) — is the Upaniṣad's portrait of the vānaprastha-saṃnyāsin. Sivananda's gloss insists the four are mutually entailed: without śānti the tapas is not real; without vidya the śraddhā is naïve; without bhaikṣa the renunciation is incomplete. All four together qualify for the sūrya-dvāra."],["Krishnananda","The verse names the destination as amṛtaḥ saḥ puruṣaḥ avyayātmā — \"the deathless Puruṣa, whose self is unchanging.\" This is the first time in the Upaniṣad the destination is named as Puruṣa rather than Brahman. The implicit identification puruṣa = akṣara = brahman = ātman is the Muṇḍaka's standard doctrine, but the change of name marks a shift: the destination is now a person-like Real, the same Puruṣa who will be elaborated in 2.1."],["Deussen","The contrast with 1.2.10 could not be sharper. Iṣṭa-pūrta-mānyamānāḥ end on \"the back of heaven\" and return; tapaḥ-śraddha-upavasitāḥ end in the Puruṣa and do not return. The same deva-yāna apparatus serves both destinations; the difference is the inner orientation. Deussen treats this as the verse establishing the antiquity of the saṃnyāsa-āśrama: the renouncant's path is already in this Upaniṣad a fully-developed alternative to the gṛhastha-karmin's."],["Aurobindo","virajāḥ — \"without dust\" — is the verse's psychologically most precise word. The dust is rāga (the attachment of 1.2.9); when the dust is gone, the sun-door opens. Aurobindo's gloss: the forest-renouncant does not earn the sun-door; he becomes virajāḥ, and the door opens. The destination — the avyaya-ātma Puruṣa — is the same as the akṣara of 1.1.5, but here named puruṣa to emphasise that what is reached is a conscious Real, not merely an indestructible substance."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 1.2.12","title":"Disgust with karma; approach a teacher with samit-pāṇi (the locus classicus of the Vedāntic guru-instruction)","sa":["परीक्ष्य लोकान् कर्मचितान् ब्राह्मणो निर्वेदमायान्नास्त्यकृतः कृतेन ।","तद्विज्ञानार्थं स गुरुमेवाभिगच्छेत् समित्पाणिः श्रोत्रियं ब्रह्मनिष्ठम् ॥ १२॥"],"iast":["parīkṣya lokān karma-citān brāhmaṇo nirvedam āyān nāsty a-kṛtaḥ kṛtena |","tad-vijñānārthaṃ sa gurum evābhigacchet samit-pāṇiḥ śrotriyaṃ brahma-niṣṭham || 12 ||"],"words":[["parīkṣya","ger. of pari-īkṣ","having examined"],["lokān","m. acc. pl.","worlds"],["karma-citān","m. acc. pl. tp.","piled up by action"],["brāhmaṇaḥ","m. nom. sg.","the Brāhmaṇa (= seeker of Brahman)"],["nirvedam","m. acc. sg.","disgust / dispassion / world-weariness"],["āyāt","3 sg. pot. of ā-i","should come to"],["na asti","indecl. + 3 sg. pres.","(there) is not"],["a-kṛtaḥ","m. nom. sg.","the un-made (the eternal)"],["kṛtena","n. instr. sg.","by (what is) made"],["tat-vijñāna-artham","indecl. adv.","for the sake of knowing That"],["saḥ","m. nom. sg.","he"],["gurum eva","m. acc. sg.","to a guru indeed"],["abhigacchet","3 sg. pot. of abhi-gam","should approach"],["samit-pāṇiḥ","m. nom. sg. bv.","fuel-stick in hand"],["śrotriyam","m. acc. sg.","(one) versed in the Veda"],["brahma-niṣṭham","m. acc. sg. tp.","established in Brahman"]],"lit":"\"Having examined the worlds piled up by karma, let a Brāhmaṇa come to disgust: 'There is no un-made (eternal) (that can be reached) by what is made.' For the sake of knowing That, let him, samit (fuel-stick) in hand, approach indeed a teacher who is versed in the Veda and established in Brahman.\"","para":"The locus classicus of the Vedāntic guru-pupil injunction. The seeker, a brāhmaṇa, having examined the worlds-built-by-karma (lokān karma-citān), arrives at nirveda — disgust, world-weariness — and makes the doctrinal recognition: nāsty a-kṛtaḥ kṛtena — \"there is no eternal (the un-made) to be attained by means of the impermanent (the made).\" For the sake of knowing That (tad-vijñānārtham) — the very akṣara of 1.1.5 — he must approach a teacher (gurum eva abhigacchet), samit-pāṇi (fuel-stick in hand, the formal mark of upanayana), and the teacher must be śrotriya (Veda-learned) and brahma-niṣṭha (established in Brahman). The verse is cited by every major Vedāntic ācārya — Śaṅkara, Rāmānuja, Madhva — as the canonical statement of the qualifications for guru and pupil.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> The principal verse of the Upaniṣad's first muṇḍaka and one of the most-cited in Vedānta. Three points: (1) parīkṣya — \"having examined\" — the renunciation is not impulsive; it follows direct examination of what karma can produce. (2) nāsti a-kṛtaḥ kṛtena — the central syllogism: the eternal cannot be produced by the non-eternal; therefore karma (which is non-eternal because it has a beginning and end) cannot produce the eternal Brahman. The whole basis of jñāna-yoga over karma-yoga is in this five-word epigram. (3) gurum eva abhigacchet samit-pāṇiḥ śrotriyaṃ brahma-niṣṭham — the formal teacher-approach. The fuel-stick (samit) is the upanayana token; the teacher must be both śrotriya (textually-learned) and brahma-niṣṭha (experientially-established). Śaṅkara emphasises that both are required: a śrotriya who is not brahma-niṣṭha is a scholar; a brahma-niṣṭha who is not śrotriya cannot transmit the teaching. Only their union qualifies as guru."],["Sivananda","The samit (fuel-stick) is symbolic: the student brings his own fuel — his own ego, his own conditioned mind — to be offered to the teacher's fire. The fuel is not a gift to the teacher; it is the student's own self brought as offering. The śrotriya-and-brahma-niṣṭha double-qualification is the only Upaniṣadic specification of the guru's required attainment. Sivananda's daily-life translation: do not accept a teacher on the strength of either learning alone or experience alone; require both, and require evidence of both."],["Krishnananda","nirveda is the precise emotional precondition — not despair, not disgust at the world, but the calm seeing that what-is-made cannot deliver what-is-not-made. nirveda is therefore neither emotional nor moral; it is cognitive. It is the recognition that the karma-cycle, however perfectly executed, cannot reach the akṣara. Once that recognition is made — but not before — the seeker is qualified to approach the guru. Krishnananda treats the verse as the pivot of the whole first muṇḍaka: everything before it is preparation (the description of karma's high reach and its hidden inadequacy); everything after it is the new teaching."],["Deussen","The verse is one of the four or five most-cited passages of the entire Upaniṣad corpus in classical Vedāntic literature. Śaṅkara cites it in the Brahma-Sūtra-Bhāṣya five times; Rāmānuja and Madhva also use it as a proof-text. Deussen translates samit-pāṇi precisely: \"with kindling in his hand\" — the gesture of formal upanayana, by which a pupil presents himself to a teacher."],["Aurobindo","The verse is the entire foundation of Vedāntic spiritual epistemology. nāsti a-kṛtaḥ kṛtena — \"the un-made cannot be (reached) by the made\" — is the principle. By extension: no finite effort can produce the infinite; no temporal practice can produce the eternal; no constructed knowledge can produce realisation. Therefore the seeker turns from his own efforts (the kṛta) to a transmission (the guru's grace), and the guru must be one who is not himself a construct but is brahma-niṣṭha — established in That. The śrotriya qualification is the lower; the brahma-niṣṭha qualification is the higher; both together are the indispensable. Aurobindo: \"the disciple must be qualified by nirveda; the guru must be qualified by niṣṭhā; only between these two qualifications does the transmission cross.\""]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 1.2.13","title":"The teacher to the qualified pupil speaks the truth","sa":["तस्मै स विद्वानुपसन्नाय सम्यक् प्रशान्तचित्ताय शमान्विताय ।","येनाक्षरं पुरुषं वेद सत्यं प्रोवाच तां तत्त्वतो ब्रह्मविद्याम् ॥ १३॥"],"iast":["tasmai sa vidvān upasannāya samyak praśānta-cittāya śam-anvitāya |","yenākṣaraṃ puruṣaṃ veda satyaṃ provāca tāṃ tattvato brahma-vidyām || 13 ||"],"words":[["tasmai","m. dat. sg.","to him"],["saḥ","m. nom. sg.","he (the teacher)"],["vidvān","m. nom. sg. pf. part. of vid","the knower"],["upasannāya","m. dat. sg. pp.","(to him) having (properly) approached"],["samyak","indecl. adv.","properly, rightly"],["praśānta-cittāya","m. dat. sg. bv.","of pacified mind"],["śam-anvitāya","m. dat. sg. bv.","endowed with self-control"],["yena","n. instr. sg. rel.","by which"],["akṣaram","m. acc. sg.","the Indestructible"],["puruṣam","m. acc. sg.","Person"],["veda","3 sg. pf. of vid (in pres. sense)","knows"],["satyam","m. acc. sg.","true"],["provāca","3 sg. pf. of pra-vac","spoke (fully)"],["tām","f. acc. sg.","that"],["tattvataḥ","indecl. adv.","in truth, as-it-really-is"],["brahma-vidyām","f. acc. sg.","the knowledge of Brahman"]],"lit":"\"To him — having properly approached, of pacified mind, endowed with self-control — that knower spoke, as-it-really-is, the knowledge of Brahman by which one knows the true, Indestructible Person.\"","para":"The closing verse of the first muṇḍaka. The teacher (saḥ vidvān) — described now in the same terms as in 1.2.12 (śrotriya, brahma-niṣṭha) — to the pupil who has approached samyak (properly), praśānta-cittāya (with pacified mind), and śam-anvitāya (with self-control) — speaks the brahma-vidyā tattvataḥ (as it really is): the knowledge by which one knows the true, indestructible Puruṣa. The two muṇḍakas to follow are this teaching as spoken.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> The pupil's qualifications are doubled here from 1.2.12: there nirveda was the sole qualification; here samyak upasanna (properly approached), praśānta-citta (pacified mind), śam-anvita (self-controlled) are added. Śaṅkara reads the three as the sādhanā-catuṣṭaya (less the viveka, which has already been accomplished by 1.2.12's parīkṣya): pacified mind = śama; self-control = dama; samyak upasanna = the procedural correctness that includes śraddhā and samādhāna. The verse therefore enumerates the full set of qualifications. The teacher's qualifications (śrotriya, brahma-niṣṭha) and the pupil's qualifications (nirveda + śama + dama + upasatti) together constitute the complete sādhanā-catuṣṭaya doctrine that becomes canonical in later Vedānta."],["Sivananda","samyak upasanna — \"properly approached\" — is the precondition. A pupil may have the inner qualifications but if he has not properly approached, the teaching does not transmit. The outer formalities (humility, fuel-stick, serving the teacher) are not optional; they shape the inner pupil into the capacity to receive. Sivananda's strong gloss: \"the form is the function. Approach properly, or do not approach.\""],["Krishnananda","The verse closes the introduction-section and opens the body of the teaching. From 2.1 onward, what is spoken is the brahmavidyā that this verse promises. The Upaniṣad's structure is therefore: 1.1 (frame, parā/aparā, cosmogony) + 1.2 (critique of karma, qualifications, the approach) = introductory section; 2.1 + 2.2 + 3.1 + 3.2 = the brahma-vidyā itself."],["Deussen","The transition is structurally similar to BĀU 4.5.1 (Maitreyī, after asking the question, is taught) and to Chāndogya 8.7 (Indra, having approached with samit twice, finally receives the teaching from Prajāpati). The Muṇḍaka's transition is more compressed."],["Aurobindo","tattvataḥ — \"as-it-is\" — is the verse's keyword. The teacher speaks the brahma-vidyā not by metaphor, not by approximation, but as the thing itself. This is the Vedāntic claim of direct cognition: brahma-vidyā is not knowledge-about-Brahman; it is the cognition that is the seeing of Brahman. The teacher does not transmit information; he transmits the cognition. Aurobindo's gloss: \"the speech of the guru, when uttered to the qualified pupil, is itself the knowing.\""]],"notes":["End of khaṇḍa 1.2 and end of Muṇḍaka 1."]}]},{"t":"Muṇḍaka 2, Khaṇḍa 1 — The fire-sparks cosmogony and the cosmic Puruṣa (10 mantras)","u":[{"ref":"Muṇḍaka 2, Khaṇḍa 1","title":"The fire-sparks cosmogony and the cosmic Puruṣa (10 mantras)","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["Muṇḍaka 2 begins the brahmavidyā proper. Khaṇḍa 2.1 expands the cosmogony of 1.1.7: the akṣara emits all beings as a fire emits sparks, and from the akṣara unfold prāṇa, mind, the elements, the cosmic body of the Puruṣa. The closing verse turns inward: knowing the Puruṣa as set in the cavity-of-the-heart cuts the knot of ignorance."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Mantra 2.1.1","title":"As sparks from fire, so beings from the akṣara","sa":["तदेतत्सत्यं ।","यथा सुदीप्तात्पावकाद्विस्फुलिङ्गाः सहस्रशः प्रभवन्ते सरूपाः ।","तथाऽक्षराद्विविधाः सोम्य भावाः प्रजायन्ते तत्र चैवापियन्ति ॥ १॥"],"iast":["tad etat satyam |","yathā sudīptāt pāvakād vi-sphuliṅgāḥ sahasraśaḥ prabhavante sa-rūpāḥ |","tathākṣarād vividhāḥ saumya bhāvāḥ prajāyante tatra caivāpiyanti || 1 ||"],"words":[["tat etat","n. nom. sg.","this same"],["satyam","n. nom. sg.","(is) the truth"],["yathā","indecl.","as"],["su-dīptāt","m. abl. sg. pp.","from a well-blazing"],["pāvakāt","m. abl. sg.","from a fire"],["vi-sphuliṅgāḥ","m. nom. pl.","sparks"],["sahasraśaḥ","indecl. adv.","by the thousand"],["prabhavante","3 pl. pres. mid. of pra-bhū","come forth"],["sa-rūpāḥ","m. nom. pl. bv.","of the same form (as the fire)"],["tathā","indecl.","so"],["akṣarāt","n. abl. sg.","from the Indestructible"],["vividhāḥ","m. nom. pl.","various"],["saumya","m. voc. sg.","O gentle one"],["bhāvāḥ","m. nom. pl.","beings, modes-of-being"],["prajāyante","3 pl. pres. mid. of pra-jan","are born forth"],["tatra","indecl.","into it"],["ca eva api","indecl.","and indeed also"],["yanti","3 pl. pres. of i","go (back)"]],"lit":"\"This is the truth: as from a well-blazing fire sparks of the same form come forth by the thousand, so, gentle one, from the Indestructible various beings are born and into it indeed they return.\"","para":"The most famous of the Upaniṣad's cosmogonic similes. Sparks (visphuliṅga) come forth from a blazing fire (sudīpta pāvaka) by the thousand (sahasraśaḥ), and each spark is sa-rūpa — of the same form (i.e., fiery) as the fire itself. In the same way, beings (bhāvāḥ) of varied kinds (vividhāḥ) are born from the akṣara, and tatra-eva-api-yanti — return into it. The address saumya (\"O gentle one,\" the standard Upaniṣadic vocative for the qualified pupil) marks this as a teaching given personally to a sat-śiṣya — the same vocative Uddālaka uses for Śvetaketu in Chāndogya 6.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> The simile establishes the akṣara as both abhinna-nimitta-upādāna (the same teaching as 1.1.7's spider) and ekāntaratam — the source from which beings emerge and into which they return. The sa-rūpāḥ qualifier is critical: the sparks are of the same form as the fire — they are not a different substance. This is the textual basis for the Advaita doctrine that the jīva is non-different from Brahman in essence (sva-rūpa) while differing in upādhi. The sparks are momentarily distinct, but they are fire-stuff; the beings are momentarily distinct, but they are akṣara-stuff. The phrase tatra ca eva api yanti — \"and into it they also return\" — closes the circle: the cosmos is not just emanated but is also reabsorbed; the akṣara is both fons and finis."],["Sivananda","The sparks-from-fire simile is the most accessible image of jīva-Brahman non-difference in the Upaniṣads. The student who finds tat-tvam-asi too abstract can hold the image: I am a spark from a fire, made of the same fire-stuff, briefly visible as distinct, eventually returning to merge in the fire. Sivananda offers it as a meditation: at sleep, see oneself as a spark dropping back into the parent fire; at waking, see oneself as a spark again leaping out."],["Krishnananda","The simile differs from the spider-thread simile of 1.1.7 in one critical respect: the spider's threads remain attached to the spider; the sparks fly free. The bhāva-srṣṭi modelled here is therefore one of free emanation: beings are released, not retained. They are sa-rūpa (same form) but they have travelled into their own arc before returning. Krishnananda treats this as the textual basis for the Advaita doctrine that the jīva enjoys (during its arc) the appearance of independence while remaining ontologically non-different from its source."],["Deussen","The simile occurs already in Bṛhad-Āraṇyaka 2.1.20 (\"As from a fire well-kindled, sparks issue forth in their thousands, so, dear one, from this Self all breaths, all worlds, all gods, all beings issue forth\"). The Muṇḍaka's version is a more compressed re-formulation. Deussen treats the BĀU as the source and the Muṇḍaka as the borrower, on the strength of the slightly more compact Muṇḍaka phrasing."],["Aurobindo","The simile establishes the deepest Advaitic point: identity of substance, distinction of form. The sparks are not analogues of the fire; they are the fire in spark-form. Aurobindo: \"each spark, while distinct, is whole fire; each being, while distinct, is whole akṣara.\" This is the basis of his later doctrine of jīva-mukti: the released soul does not lose its individuality on returning to its source, because its individuality was always fire-form, not other-than-fire."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 2.1.2","title":"The Puruṣa: divine, formless, inner-and-outer, unborn","sa":["दिव्यो ह्यमूर्तः पुरुषः सबाह्याभ्यन्तरो ह्यजः ।","अप्राणो ह्यमनाः शुभ्रो ह्यक्षरात्परतः परः ॥ २॥"],"iast":["divyo hy a-mūrtaḥ puruṣaḥ sa-bāhya-abhyantaro hy ajaḥ |","a-prāṇo hy a-manāḥ śubhro hy akṣarāt parataḥ paraḥ || 2 ||"],"words":[["divyaḥ","m. nom. sg.","divine, of the bright world"],["hi","indecl.","indeed"],["a-mūrtaḥ","m. nom. sg. bv.","formless"],["puruṣaḥ","m. nom. sg.","Person"],["sa-bāhya-abhyantaraḥ","m. nom. sg. bv.","with outer and inner (= both outside and inside)"],["ajaḥ","m. nom. sg. adj.","unborn"],["a-prāṇaḥ","m. nom. sg. bv.","without breath"],["a-manāḥ","m. nom. sg. bv.","without mind"],["śubhraḥ","m. nom. sg.","shining, pure"],["akṣarāt","n. abl. sg. comp.","than the (lower) akṣara"],["parataḥ paraḥ","m. nom. sg. (lit. \"higher than the higher\")","higher than the higher"]],"lit":"\"Divine, formless is the Puruṣa, with-outer-and-inner, unborn, without breath, without mind, shining, higher than the higher Indestructible.\"","para":"The Puruṣa is now described — and this verse's puruṣa is the Para-Puruṣa, the highest. Eight epithets: divya (divine), a-mūrta (formless), sa-bāhya-abhyantara (both outside and inside, i.e., both transcendent and immanent), a-ja (unborn), a-prāṇa (breath-less — i.e., not characterised by life-process as the jīva is), a-manas (mind-less — not characterised by cognition as the jīva is), śubhra (luminous), and akṣarāt parataḥ paraḥ (higher than the higher akṣara). The last phrase is doctrinally critical: the akṣara of 1.1.5 was identified as the source of beings; now this Puruṣa is identified as higher even than that akṣara. The Muṇḍaka distinguishes a higher and a lower akṣara — the lower akṣara is the indestructible source of beings; the higher is the Puruṣa beyond it.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> The two-akṣara doctrine of this verse is critical for Vedānta exegesis. The lower akṣara is the kāraṇa-brahman (the causal Brahman, with the power to emit creatures); the higher Puruṣa is the para-brahman (the absolute Brahman, prior even to causal status). Compare Bhagavad-Gītā 15.16–18, where Kṛṣṇa distinguishes the kṣara puruṣa (perishable), the akṣara puruṣa (imperishable), and the uttama puruṣa (supreme); the Muṇḍaka's akṣarāt parataḥ paraḥ = the Gītā's uttama puruṣa. Śaṅkara's reading: the akṣara of 1.1.5–9 is the brahman with creative power; the para-puruṣa of this verse is the brahman beyond all power, the pure-witness aspect."],["Sivananda","The list of eight attributes is given as a meditation: sit, breathe, and one by one consider — divine (not earthly), formless (not bodily), both outside and inside (not localised), unborn (not produced), without breath (not living-in-the-way-jīva-lives), without mind (not knowing-in-the-way-jīva-knows), luminous (not dark), beyond the indestructible (not even the immutable substance is its limit). The meditator finds that what survives this stripping is exactly oneself — the witness who has been positing all these negations."],["Krishnananda","The two-akṣara distinction is the technical heart of Vedānta. The lower akṣara = the kāraṇa-brahman, the highest causal level; the higher Puruṣa = the para-brahman, the trans-causal Real. The Muṇḍaka therefore distinguishes saguṇa-brahman (the kāraṇa, here called akṣara) and nirguṇa-brahman (the Puruṣa, here called akṣarāt parataḥ paraḥ). Krishnananda cross-cites BĀU 4.4.20 — the same para-brahman, otherwise called akṣara, is here re-qualified as parataḥ paraḥ."],["Deussen","a-prāṇa, a-manas are technically negating: the Puruṣa is not characterised by prāṇa (which is a feature of jīva-life) and not by manas (which is a feature of jīva-cognition). The Puruṣa is consciousness as such, prior to its individual instantiation in life-and-mind. Compare Praśna 3.3 where prāṇa is born from the ātman — i.e., prāṇa is a product of the Puruṣa, not its essence."],["Aurobindo","sa-bāhya-abhyantaraḥ — \"with outer and inner\" — is the verse's most precise statement of immanence-transcendence. The Puruṣa is not outside in the sense of distant; it is outside as encompassing and inside as filling. There is no inside-or-outside relative to the Puruṣa; both are within it. akṣarāt parataḥ paraḥ — \"higher than the higher\" — is the verse's most precise statement of non-finalisability. Even the highest concept (akṣara, the imperishable substance) is not the final word about the Puruṣa; the Puruṣa is parataḥ — beyond — even that."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 2.1.3","title":"From the Puruṣa: prāṇa, mind, the senses, the elements","sa":["एतस्माज्जायते प्राणो मनः सर्वेन्द्रियाणि च ।","खं वायुर्ज्योतिरापः पृथिवी विश्वस्य धारिणी ॥ ३॥"],"iast":["etasmāj jāyate prāṇo manaḥ sarvendriyāṇi ca |","khaṃ vāyur jyotir āpaḥ pṛthivī viśvasya dhāriṇī || 3 ||"],"words":[["etasmāt","m. abl. sg.","from this (Puruṣa)"],["jāyate","3 sg. pres. of jan","is born"],["prāṇaḥ","m. nom. sg.","breath / life"],["manaḥ","n. nom. sg.","mind"],["sarva-indriyāṇi","n. nom. pl. tp.","all the sense-organs"],["ca","indecl.","and"],["kham","n. nom. sg.","space / ether"],["vāyuḥ","m. nom. sg.","wind / air"],["jyotiḥ","n. nom. sg.","fire / light"],["āpaḥ","f. nom. pl.","the waters"],["pṛthivī","f. nom. sg.","earth"],["viśvasya","n. gen. sg.","of all (beings)"],["dhāriṇī","f. nom. sg.","supporter, bearer"]],"lit":"\"From this (Puruṣa) is born prāṇa, mind, and all the sense-organs; (also) space, wind, fire, the waters, and earth — the supporter of all.\"","para":"The cosmogonic emanation listed in order: from the Puruṣa are born (a) prāṇa, the life-principle, (b) manas, the mind, (c) all the sense-organs (sarva-indriyāṇi — five organs of knowledge plus five of action), and the five mahā-bhūtas: (d) kha (ākāśa / space), (e) vāyu (wind), (f) jyotis (= tejas / fire), (g) āpaḥ (water, plural to indicate the element), and (h) pṛthivī (earth, expressly called viśvasya dhāriṇī — the supporter of all). The order is the standard Vedāntic pañca-bhūta-utpatti sequence (space → wind → fire → water → earth, in decreasing subtlety) and matches the Taittirīya 2.1.1.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> Notes the technical sequence: prāṇa-manas-indriyāṇi (the inner, vital-and-cognitive aspect) listed first, then the five elements in the standard ākāśa-down sequence. The order follows the Taittirīya pañca-bhūta-utpatti exactly. The verse establishes the Puruṣa as the source of both jīva-internal (prāṇa, manas, indriyas) and external (the five elements) cosmos."],["Sivananda","This verse contains the entire Vedāntic cosmology compressed into a śloka. The seeker should memorise the eight items and the sequence: prāṇa-manas-indriyāṇi-ākāśa-vāyu-tejas-āpaḥ-pṛthivī. This is the order of emanation (and also, reversed, the order of dissolution at pralaya). It is the same sequence found in Taittirīya 2 and Praśna 4."],["Krishnananda","The verse anticipates 2.1.4 (the cosmic body of the Puruṣa). 2.1.3 lists the elements as products; 2.1.4 will identify them as the Puruṣa's body. This is the Vedāntic vyaṣṭi-samaṣṭi homology: what is produced by the Puruṣa is also the Puruṣa's own body. The cosmos is therefore not a separate creation but the Puruṣa's expanded form. Krishnananda treats this as the Atharvan version of Puruṣa-sūkta (ṚV 10.90)."],["Deussen","pṛthivī viśvasya dhāriṇī — \"the earth, supporter of all\" — is a standard Ṛgvedic epithet (cf. ṚV 1.185.1, 10.18.10). The qualifier signals the verse's Vedic source-vocabulary. The whole list is in Ṛgvedic / Atharvan rather than Sāṅkhya idiom — there is no mention of buddhi or ahaṅkāra, which are later Sāṅkhya categories. The Muṇḍaka here remains within Vedic categories."],["Aurobindo","The order is critical: prāṇa precedes manas, manas precedes the elements. The hierarchy is consciousness-down: life-force first, mind second, then matter. Aurobindo treats this as the textual proof that the Upaniṣadic cosmology is consciousness-fundamental: matter is the most external product, not the foundation. The verse refutes any reading of Vedānta as matter-first or matter-equal-to-mind: matter comes last."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 2.1.4","title":"The cosmic body: fire is head, sun-moon are eyes, the directions are ears…","sa":["अग्निर्मूर्धा चक्षुषी चन्द्रसूर्यौ दिशः श्रोत्रे वाग्विवृताश्च वेदाः ।","वायुः प्राणो हृदयं विश्वमस्य पद्भ्यां पृथिवी ह्येष सर्वभूतान्तरात्मा ॥ ४॥"],"iast":["agnir mūrdhā cakṣuṣī candra-sūryau diśaḥ śrotre vāg vivṛtāś ca vedāḥ |","vāyuḥ prāṇo hṛdayaṃ viśvam asya padbhyāṃ pṛthivī hy eṣa sarva-bhūta-antara-ātmā || 4 ||"],"words":[["agniḥ","m. nom. sg.","fire"],["mūrdhā","m. nom. sg.","(is his) head"],["cakṣuṣī","n. nom. du.","the two eyes"],["candra-sūryau","m. nom. du. dv.","(are) moon-and-sun"],["diśaḥ","f. nom. pl.","the directions"],["śrotre","n. nom. du.","(are his) two ears"],["vāk","f. nom. sg.","speech"],["vivṛtāḥ","m. nom. pl. pp.","(are) the revealed"],["ca","indecl.","and"],["vedāḥ","m. nom. pl.","Vedas"],["vāyuḥ","m. nom. sg.","wind"],["prāṇaḥ","m. nom. sg.","(is his) breath"],["hṛdayam","n. nom. sg.","(his) heart"],["viśvam","n. nom. sg.","(is) the whole (universe)"],["asya","m. gen. sg.","of him"],["padbhyām","m. instr. du.","from (his) two feet"],["pṛthivī","f. nom. sg.","(is) the earth"],["hi","indecl.","indeed"],["eṣaḥ","m. nom. sg.","this (Puruṣa)"],["sarva-bhūta-antara-ātmā","m. nom. sg. tp.","(is) the inner Self of all beings"]],"lit":"\"Fire is his head, the moon-and-sun are his two eyes, the directions are his ears, speech is the revealed Vedas; wind is his breath, the universe is his heart, from his two feet is the earth — for he is the inner Self of all beings.\"","para":"The Atharvan recapitulation of the Puruṣa-sūkta (ṚV 10.90.13–14). The Puruṣa's cosmic body is anatomised: fire = head, sun-moon = eyes, the directions = ears, the Vedas (issued from his mouth) = speech, wind = breath, the cosmos = heart, earth = feet. The closing line — eṣa sarva-bhūta-antara-ātmā — \"this (one) is the inner Self of all beings\" — is the doctrinal climax: the Puruṣa just described as the cosmic body is, simultaneously, the inner Self within every being. The cosmic and the individual coincide.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> The verse is the Muṇḍaka's compression of Puruṣa-sūkta. The body-parts assigned to cosmic phenomena are identical to ṚV 10.90.13 (\"the moon was born from his mind, from his eye the sun, from his mouth Indra and Agni, from his breath the wind, from his navel the mid-air, from his head the sky, from his feet the earth\") but the direction is reversed: ṚV 10.90 derives the cosmos from the Puruṣa's body; the Muṇḍaka identifies the cosmos with the Puruṣa's body. The doctrinal stake: not just origin but ongoing identity. The cosmos is the Puruṣa now, not just made-from-the-Puruṣa once. The closing sarva-bhūta-antara-ātmā doubles this with the inner identification."],["Sivananda","The cosmic-body meditation is one of the oldest in the Vedic tradition (Puruṣa-sūkta is sung in every formal pūjā). Sivananda recommends sitting with this verse at sunrise: see the sun in front and recognise: this is my Self's eye; see fire in the offering and recognise: this is my Self's head; etc. By the eighth identification (earth from feet), the meditator has experienced his own body as cosmos and the cosmos as his body. The verse trains the perceptual gestalt of advaita."],["Krishnananda","The verse establishes the doctrine of antaryāmin — the inner-controller — that becomes central to Bṛhad-Āraṇyaka 3.7 (Yājñavalkya's antaryāmi-brāhmaṇa). The Puruṣa is not just within (antaḥ) but is the antar-ātmā — the very Self interior to every being. This is the textual basis for the later Vedāntic identification jīva = ātmā = brahman, and is the precursor of the antaryāmin doctrine."],["Deussen","The Puruṣa-sūkta tradition is here in its Atharvan form. Compare ŚU 3.16 (sarvatāśvīrṣaḥ sarvataścakṣuḥ — \"hands and feet everywhere, eyes and heads everywhere\") which is the Śvetāśvatara variant. The Muṇḍaka's version is the most compressed of the three. Deussen's note: the Muṇḍaka does not include the four-varṇa derivation of ṚV 10.90.12 (brāhmaṇa from mouth, kṣatriya from arms, etc.) — a significant Upaniṣadic editing that removes the social-classification element."],["Aurobindo","The closing sarva-bhūta-antara-ātmā collapses the entire cosmology into a single identity. The cosmic body just described is not external to the meditator — it is the meditator's own inner Self. Aurobindo treats the verse as the Upaniṣadic equivalent of the Christian mystic claim that \"the kingdom of heaven is within you,\" but with greater precision: the cosmos that is within is not metaphorical but identical. The fire of one's own awareness is the same fire as the sun in the sky."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 2.1.5","title":"From the Puruṣa: fire, sun, rain, plants, the human couple and progeny","sa":["तस्मादग्निः समिधो यस्य सूर्यः सोमात्पर्जन्य ओषधयः पृथिव्याम् ।","पुमान् रेतः सिञ्चति योषितायां बह्वीः प्रजाः पुरुषात्संप्रसूताः ॥ ५॥"],"iast":["tasmād agniḥ samidho yasya sūryaḥ somāt parjanya oṣadhayaḥ pṛthivyām |","pumān retaḥ siñcati yoṣitāyāṃ bahvīḥ prajāḥ puruṣāt saṃprasūtāḥ || 5 ||"],"words":[["tasmāt","m. abl. sg.","from him"],["agniḥ","m. nom. sg.","(is) fire"],["samidhaḥ","f. nom. pl.","whose fuel-sticks"],["yasya","m. gen. sg. rel.","(are)"],["sūryaḥ","m. nom. sg.","the sun"],["somāt","m. abl. sg.","from the moon"],["parjanyaḥ","m. nom. sg.","the rain-cloud"],["oṣadhayaḥ","f. nom. pl.","plants"],["pṛthivyām","f. loc. sg.","on the earth"],["pumān","m. nom. sg.","a man"],["retaḥ","n. acc. sg.","semen"],["siñcati","3 sg. pres. of sic","pours, sheds"],["yoṣitāyām","f. loc. sg.","in a woman"],["bahvīḥ","f. nom. pl.","many"],["prajāḥ","f. nom. pl.","progeny"],["puruṣāt","m. abl. sg.","from the Puruṣa"],["saṃprasūtāḥ","f. nom. pl. pp.","(are) brought forth"]],"lit":"\"From him is the (sacrificial) fire whose fuel-sticks are the sun; from the moon, the rain-cloud; (from the rain), plants on the earth; a man sheds seed in a woman — (and so) many progeny are brought forth from the Puruṣa.\"","para":"The pañcāgni-vidyā (\"five-fire knowledge\") in compressed form. The five fires of Chāndogya 5.4–10 (the cosmos as a series of sacrifices: heaven → rain → earth → man → woman → embryo) are here condensed into a single śloka. From the Puruṣa: the sun-fueled cosmic fire → from the moon (= the cooled solar exhalation) the rain-cloud parjanya → on the earth, plants → man sheds seed in woman → many progeny. All are saṃprasūtāḥ puruṣāt — emanated from the Puruṣa. The cosmogony continues, descending now into biological reproduction.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> The verse is the Muṇḍaka's compression of the pañcāgni-vidyā elaborated in Chāndogya 5.4–10 and BĀU 6.2. The five fires: dyu-loka (the heaven-fire whose fuel is the sun), parjanya (the rain-cloud fire whose fuel is the wind), pṛthivī (the earth-fire whose fuel is the year), puruṣa (the man-fire whose fuel is speech), yoṣā (the woman-fire whose fuel is the vulva) — each takes an offering and yields a product. The Muṇḍaka condenses these into a six-step chain: Puruṣa → sun → moon-rain → plants → man-and-woman → progeny. The doctrinal claim: human reproduction is the same cosmogonic energy as cosmic emanation, just descended into the fifth stage."],["Sivananda","The verse establishes that biological reproduction is sacred — it is the lowest stage of the same emanation that begins with the Puruṣa. The implication for the gṛhastha: family-life is not separate from spiritual cosmology; it is its concluding stage. Sivananda treats this as the textual ground for the gṛhastha-āśrama's full inclusion within the spiritual life: progeny are the Puruṣa's saṃprasūta."],["Krishnananda","The compression of pañcāgni into one śloka is the Muṇḍaka's most elegant editing. Chāndogya 5 takes seven khaṇḍas to spell out what this verse compresses to four lines. The compression is possible because the Muṇḍaka's audience already knows the doctrine; the Upaniṣad cites it, not expounds it."],["Deussen","The pañcāgni-vidyā is one of the oldest items of Upaniṣadic doctrine, present in Chāndogya 5 (the Pravāhaṇa Jaivali instruction), BĀU 6.2 (the same instruction in a different form), and here. The Muṇḍaka treats it as established teaching. Deussen takes this as evidence of the Muṇḍaka's relatively late position within the principal Upaniṣad corpus."],["Aurobindo","The continuity from cosmic emanation to biological reproduction is one of the Upaniṣad's most precise statements of cosmology-as-continuum. There is no break between the akṣara's spark-emanation (2.1.1) and human birth (2.1.5); they are the same process at different scales. Aurobindo glosses: \"every birth is a step in the spark-emission of the akṣara.\""]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 2.1.6","title":"From the Puruṣa: the three Vedas, sacrifices, rites, the year, the worlds","sa":["तस्मादृचः साम यजूꣳषि दीक्षा यज्ञाश्च सर्वे क्रतवो दक्षिणाश्च ।","संवत्सरश्च यजमानश्च लोकाः सोमो यत्र पवते यत्र सूर्यः ॥ ६॥"],"iast":["tasmād ṛcaḥ sāma yajūṃṣi dīkṣā yajñāś ca sarve kratavo dakṣiṇāś ca |","saṃvatsaraś ca yajamānaś ca lokāḥ somo yatra pavate yatra sūryaḥ || 6 ||"],"words":[["tasmāt","m. abl. sg.","from him"],["ṛcaḥ","f. nom. pl.","Ṛc-mantras"],["sāma","n. nom. sg. (coll.)","Sāman-chants"],["yajūṃṣi","n. nom. pl.","Yajus-formulas"],["dīkṣā","f. nom. sg.","initiation"],["yajñāḥ","m. nom. pl.","sacrifices"],["ca","indecl.","and"],["sarve","m. nom. pl.","all"],["kratavaḥ","m. nom. pl.","rites (i.e., soma-rites)"],["dakṣiṇāḥ","f. nom. pl.","the dakṣiṇās (priestly fees)"],["ca","indecl.","and"],["saṃvatsaraḥ","m. nom. sg.","the year"],["yajamānaḥ","m. nom. sg.","the sacrificer"],["lokāḥ","m. nom. pl.","the worlds"],["somaḥ","m. nom. sg.","Soma"],["yatra","indecl.","where"],["pavate","3 sg. pres. mid. of pū","purifies (himself)"],["yatra","indecl.","where"],["sūryaḥ","m. nom. sg.","the sun (shines)"]],"lit":"\"From him are the Ṛcas, the Sāmans, the Yajus-formulas, the initiation, all sacrifices and rites and dakṣiṇā-fees, the year and the sacrificer and the worlds where Soma purifies (himself) and where the sun (shines).\"","para":"The aparā vidyā itself comes from the Puruṣa. The same Vedic-and-ritual apparatus that 1.1.5 named as aparā is here re-affirmed as Puruṣa-emanated: the Ṛc, Sāman, Yajus; dīkṣā (initiation); all yajñas; all kratus (soma-rites); the dakṣiṇās; the saṃvatsara (the ritual year); the yajamāna (the sacrificer); the worlds (lokāḥ) — both the soma-purifying world (the moon, somo yatra pavate) and the sun-illumined world (yatra sūryaḥ). All of this aparā has its source in the Puruṣa just as much as the parā does. The verse is therefore the Muṇḍaka's most precise inclusion of aparā within the cosmogony: it is not denied; it is given Puruṣa-origin.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> The verse restores the aparā to its honour. After khaṇḍa 1.2's hard critique, this verse re-affirms that the karma-kāṇḍa is Puruṣa-emanated and therefore real-at-its-level. The Vedic mantras, the sacrifices, the dakṣiṇās, the priestly year, the sacrificer himself — all emanate from the same source as the akṣara. The Upaniṣad therefore does not dismiss the aparā; it relativises it. The phrase saṃvatsaraś ca yajamānaś ca is the verse's deepest concession: even the time-structure of ritual (the year) and even the agent of ritual (the sacrificer himself) are Puruṣa-emanations."],["Sivananda","The verse gives the karma-kāṇḍin permission to perform his rites with full Vedic seriousness — they are not rejected; they are real. What 1.2 rejected was the karma-kāṇḍin's mistake about their result (the back-of-heaven, exhaustible), not the rites themselves. Sivananda's gloss: \"perform every yajña that you are competent for — they all come from the Puruṣa. Just do not call the result variṣṭha.\""],["Krishnananda","The list saṃvatsaraś ca yajamānaś ca lokāḥ is precise: the year (time-structure of ritual), the sacrificer (agent of ritual), the worlds (rewards of ritual) — all three are Puruṣa-emanations. The aparā is therefore a complete sub-world within the Puruṣa's emanation, not a foreign element to be excised. Krishnananda's deepest reading: jñāna does not abolish karma; it locates karma within its true ground."],["Deussen","The verse is one of the most precise inclusions in the Upaniṣad corpus of the karma-kāṇḍa within the jñāna-kāṇḍa. Compare Chāndogya 5.3 (where Ārtabhāga catechises Yājñavalkya) — but Chāndogya keeps the two more separate. The Muṇḍaka's integration is sharper: even the dakṣiṇās and the calendar come from the same Puruṣa as the akṣara."],["Aurobindo","somo yatra pavate yatra sūryaḥ — \"where Soma purifies, where the sun (shines)\" — names the two solar-lunar polarities that structure the karmic universe. Soma = the lunar half of the year, when offerings rise through smoke; sūrya = the solar half, when the bright path leads upward. Both halves are Puruṣa-emanations. The aparā is the entire calendrical-ritual cosmos; the parā is the Puruṣa from whom that cosmos hangs. Both are real; only their relation is to be understood."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 2.1.7","title":"From the Puruṣa: gods, sādhyas, men, animals, birds; prāṇa-apāna, food, tapas, śraddhā, satya, brahmacarya","sa":["तस्माच्च देवा बहुधा संप्रसूताः साध्या मनुष्याः पशवो वयाꣳसि ।","प्राणापानौ व्रीहियवौ तपश्च श्रद्धा सत्यं ब्रह्मचर्यं विधिश्च ॥ ७॥"],"iast":["tasmāc ca devā bahudhā saṃprasūtāḥ sādhyā manuṣyāḥ paśavo vayāṃsi |","prāṇāpānau vrīhi-yavau tapaś ca śraddhā satyaṃ brahma-caryaṃ vidhiś ca || 7 ||"],"words":[["tasmāt","m. abl. sg.","from him"],["ca","indecl.","and"],["devāḥ","m. nom. pl.","the gods"],["bahudhā","indecl.","in many ways"],["saṃprasūtāḥ","m. nom. pl. pp.","(are) brought forth"],["sādhyāḥ","m. nom. pl.","the Sādhyas (a class of demigods)"],["manuṣyāḥ","m. nom. pl.","humans"],["paśavaḥ","m. nom. pl.","animals"],["vayāṃsi","n. nom. pl.","birds"],["prāṇa-apānau","m. nom. du. dv.","in-breath and out-breath"],["vrīhi-yavau","m. nom. du. dv.","rice and barley"],["tapaḥ","n. nom. sg.","tapas"],["ca","indecl.","and"],["śraddhā","f. nom. sg.","faith"],["satyam","n. nom. sg.","truth"],["brahma-caryam","n. nom. sg.","celibacy / Veda-discipline"],["vidhiḥ","m. nom. sg.","rule / right procedure"],["ca","indecl.","and"]],"lit":"\"From him also the gods are brought forth in many ways, the Sādhyas, humans, animals, birds; in-breath and out-breath, rice and barley, tapas and faith, truth and brahmacarya and right rule.\"","para":"The catalogue continues. From the Puruṣa: (a) the four classes of beings — devas (gods), sādhyas (a vague class of demigods, also mentioned in Puruṣa-sūkta 10.90.7 and 10.90.16), manuṣyas (humans), and the broader living world (paśu = animals, vayas = birds); (b) the prāṇa-apāna pair (the two breath-poles, in-and-out); (c) vrīhi-yava (rice and barley, the two principal cereals — staple food); (d) the six virtues: tapas, śraddhā, satya, brahmacarya, vidhi (right procedure), and (the next mantra continues). The verse extends the Puruṣa-emanation to all living kinds, all biological process, and all moral discipline.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> The triple list — beings, biological pairs (breath-pair, food-pair), and virtues — is Puruṣa-sūkta-derived. Compare ṚV 10.90.10: \"From him the horses were born, and whatever animals have two rows of teeth; from him cattle, from him goats and sheep.\" The Muṇḍaka's version drops the specific animal-list and replaces it with the abstract paśu-vaya pair. The inclusion of virtues (tapas, śraddhā, etc.) among Puruṣa-emanations is the verse's Upaniṣadic innovation over the Vedic Puruṣa-sūkta: even moral qualities are not human inventions but Puruṣa-emanations. The aparā is therefore extended beyond ritual to include the entire moral order."],["Sivananda","The six virtues (tapas, śraddhā, satya, brahmacarya, vidhi, and dharma in 2.1.10) form the canonical dharma-list of the Muṇḍaka. They are not invented by humans; they are Puruṣa-emanated. To practise them is therefore to participate in cosmic order, not to add a human contribution to it. Sivananda treats this as the textual basis for the doctrine that dharma is sanātana — Puruṣa-given, not human-constructed."],["Krishnananda","The grouping in this verse — devas, sādhyas, humans, animals, birds — is the standard four-fold living-being classification (jarāyu-ja, aṇḍa-ja, sveda-ja, udbhij-ja in later doxography). The verse is the Muṇḍaka's most inclusive list of what emanates from the Puruṣa."],["Deussen","The Sādhyas are an enigmatic class; they appear in ṚV 10.90.7 (\"the Sādhyas were the first gods\") and in later texts as a category of demigods, but their exact identity is uncertain. Deussen notes the Muṇḍaka's preservation of this archaic category as further evidence of its inheritance of Atharvan/Vedic vocabulary."],["Aurobindo","The verse's hidden teaching is the inclusion of moral discipline within cosmic emanation. tapas, śraddhā, satya, brahmacarya are not products of human moral effort; they are powers of the Puruṣa that, when received by human consciousness, become virtues. Aurobindo treats this as the textual basis for his view that the spiritual life is not the cultivation of personal virtue but the receptivity to cosmic powers."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 2.1.8","title":"Seven breaths, seven flames, seven offerings, seven worlds","sa":["सप्त प्राणाः प्रभवन्ति तस्मा-त्सप्तार्चिषः समिधः सप्त होमाः ।","सप्त इमे लोका येषु चरन्ति प्राणा गुहाशया निहिताः सप्त सप्त ॥ ८॥"],"iast":["sapta prāṇāḥ prabhavanti tasmāt saptārciṣaḥ samidhaḥ sapta homāḥ |","sapta ime lokā yeṣu caranti prāṇā guhāśayā nihitāḥ sapta sapta || 8 ||"],"words":[["sapta","indecl. num.","seven"],["prāṇāḥ","m. nom. pl.","breaths / vital airs"],["prabhavanti","3 pl. pres. of pra-bhū","come forth"],["tasmāt","m. abl. sg.","from him"],["sapta","indecl. num.","seven"],["arciṣaḥ","f. nom. pl.","flames"],["samidhaḥ","f. nom. pl.","fuel-sticks"],["sapta","indecl. num.","seven"],["homāḥ","m. nom. pl.","offerings"],["sapta ime","m. nom. pl.","these seven"],["lokāḥ","m. nom. pl.","worlds"],["yeṣu","m. loc. pl.","in which"],["caranti","3 pl. pres. of car","move"],["prāṇāḥ","m. nom. pl.","the prāṇas"],["guhā-śayāḥ","m. nom. pl. bv.","lodged in the cavity"],["nihitāḥ","m. nom. pl. pp.","deposited"],["sapta sapta","indecl. (distributive)","seven (and) seven"]],"lit":"\"From him come forth the seven prāṇas, the seven flames, the (seven) fuel-sticks, the seven offerings, these seven worlds in which the prāṇas, deposited as those-lodged-in-the-cavity, move — seven and seven.\"","para":"The Atharvan seven-fold cosmology. Seven prāṇas (the seven openings of the head — two eyes, two ears, two nostrils, mouth — through which life-functions flow), seven arcis (the seven tongues of fire from 1.2.4), seven samidhas (seven fuel-sticks, one per tongue), seven homas (seven offerings), seven lokas (the seven worlds — bhūr, bhuvar, svar, mahar, janas, tapas, satya), and seven prāṇas again, this time as guhā-śayāḥ — lodged in the cavity (of the heart) — and deposited (nihitāḥ) seven-and-seven (sapta sapta — distributive: seven in each individual). The verse builds an elaborate seven-fold cosmology with seven-fold subdivisions throughout.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> The seven prāṇas of the verse are the seven head-openings (the cognitive prāṇas), distinct from the five body-prāṇas (prāṇa, apāna, vyāna, udāna, samāna). The seven flames and seven samidhas-and-homas elaborate 1.2.4. The seven lokas are the canonical septet of upper worlds. The closing phrase guhā-śayāḥ sapta sapta refers to the prāṇas as lodged in the heart-cavity in each individual, distributed seven-and-seven (one set of seven in cosmic emanation, one set of seven in the individual heart). The verse is the Atharvan signature: the Muṇḍaka's seven-fold cosmology is the same one elaborated in AV 10.2 and 10.8."],["Sivananda","The sapta-prāṇa scheme is older than the pañca-prāṇa scheme. It maps the seven openings of the face as the principal channels of life-force. Sivananda recommends sapta-prāṇa-darśana as a daily exercise: feel the prāṇa as it enters and exits each of the seven openings; recognise that these seven channels are all Puruṣa-emanated."],["Krishnananda","The doubled \"seven seven\" (sapta sapta) is critical. The cosmic seven (the worlds) and the individual seven (the head-openings) are not two seven-folds; they are the same seven, just viewed at different scales. The vyaṣṭi-samaṣṭi homology is again exact: what is seven in the cosmos is seven in the individual."],["Deussen","Compare AV 10.2.6 (\"The seven prāṇas were measured out\") and AV 10.8.43 (the seven-fold body of the cosmic Puruṣa). The Muṇḍaka inherits the Atharvan seven-fold scheme. Deussen notes that this scheme is older than the pañca-prāṇa / pañca-bhūta scheme that becomes canonical in later Vedānta, and that the Muṇḍaka's preservation of it is one of the marks of its Atharvan origin."],["Aurobindo","The seven prāṇas are the seven streams of consciousness-life that flow through the seven openings. They are not just biological breath but the seven powers of awareness — seeing, hearing, smelling, tasting, speaking, breathing, and the seventh that binds them (the ego-prāṇa). Aurobindo links these to his seven-fold cosmic hierarchy (sat, cit, ānanda, supermind, mind, life, matter) — the seven cosmic flames are the seven planes of being, the seven prāṇas in the individual are the seven streams by which those planes manifest in him."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 2.1.9","title":"From him: oceans, mountains, rivers, plants, the sap that sustains beings","sa":["अतः समुद्रा गिरयश्च सर्वेऽस्मा-त्स्यन्दन्ते सिन्धवः सर्वरूपाः ।","अतश्च सर्वा ओषधयो रसश्च येनैष भूतैस्तिष्ठते ह्यन्तरात्मा ॥ ९॥"],"iast":["ataḥ samudrā girayaś ca sarve'smāt syandante sindhavaḥ sarva-rūpāḥ |","ataś ca sarvā oṣadhayo rasaś ca yenaiṣa bhūtais tiṣṭhate hy antar-ātmā || 9 ||"],"words":[["ataḥ","indecl.","from this (Puruṣa)"],["samudrāḥ","m. nom. pl.","oceans"],["girayaḥ","m. nom. pl.","mountains"],["ca sarve","m. nom. pl.","and all"],["asmāt","m. abl. sg.","from him"],["syandante","3 pl. pres. mid. of syand","flow"],["sindhavaḥ","m. nom. pl.","rivers"],["sarva-rūpāḥ","m. nom. pl. bv.","of all forms"],["ataḥ ca","indecl.","and from this"],["sarvāḥ","f. nom. pl.","all"],["oṣadhayaḥ","f. nom. pl.","plants"],["rasaḥ","m. nom. sg.","sap / essence"],["ca","indecl.","and"],["yena","m. instr. sg. rel.","by which"],["eṣaḥ","m. nom. sg.","this"],["bhūtaiḥ","n. instr. pl.","with / in the elements"],["tiṣṭhate","3 sg. pres. mid. of sthā","stands / abides"],["hi","indecl.","indeed"],["antar-ātmā","m. nom. sg. tp.","the inner Self"]],"lit":"\"From him are the oceans and all the mountains; from him flow the rivers of all forms; and from him also are all plants and the sap by which this inner Self abides among/within the elements.\"","para":"The Puruṣa's emanation in the natural-geographical scale: oceans, mountains, rivers (in all their varied forms — sarva-rūpāḥ), plants, and the rasa — the sap, the essential moisture by which the antar-ātmā (the inner Self) maintains itself in association with the elements (bhūtaiḥ). The verse closes by re-naming the Puruṣa as antar-ātmā — the inner Self — and identifying the rasa-sap as the means by which it abides in the body-mind among the elements.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> The verse extends the emanation-list to the macro-geography. The closing phrase yena eṣa bhūtais tiṣṭhate hi antar-ātmā is doctrinally weighty: the rasa (sap) is the medium of the inner Self's abiding. In one reading, rasa = food — the digested essence by which the body maintains itself; the antar-ātmā uses rasa to abide in the elemental body. In another reading, rasa = essence in the more general sense — the essential energy by which the antar-ātmā remains associated with its embodiment. Both readings work; Śaṅkara prefers the food-reading."],["Sivananda","The verse links macrocosm (oceans, mountains, rivers) and microcosm (the antar-ātmā in the body-elements). Both are Puruṣa-emanations; both are continuous. Sivananda recommends contemplating one's own body as a small landscape — its bones as mountains, its blood as rivers, its mucous as ocean — and recognising the same antar-ātmā that abides in the macrocosmic landscape also abides in this microcosmic one."],["Krishnananda","The shift from \"saṃprasūta\" (brought forth) in 2.1.5–7 to \"syandante\" (flow) in this verse is critical. The earlier verbs were Puruṣa-active (he brings forth); this verb is recipient-active (they flow). The cosmos is now characterised by its own ongoing motion within the Puruṣa, not by an originary act of Puruṣa-emission. The shift marks the transition from cosmogony (origin-talk) to cosmology (ongoing-structure-talk)."],["Deussen","Compare ŚU 4.10 — \"He who has the māyā as his own, the Mahā-yogin from whom the whole world is born.\" The Muṇḍaka's version is older and more concrete (oceans, mountains, rivers, plants, sap); ŚU's is more abstract (māyā, yoga). The development is from concrete-cosmographic to abstract-philosophical."],["Aurobindo","The closing phrase yena eṣa bhūtaiḥ tiṣṭhate hi antar-ātmā is the verse's key. The antar-ātmā does not abide in the elements by accident; it abides by virtue of the rasa — the essential energy — which is also a Puruṣa-emanation. The inner Self is sustained in its incarnation by the same Puruṣa that emits the cosmos. The continuity is complete: macro-cosmos, body, life-sustenance, inner Self — all one Puruṣa-energetic."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 2.1.10","title":"All is Puruṣa: knowing him in the cavity, the knot of ignorance is severed","sa":["पुरुष एवेदं विश्वं कर्म तपो ब्रह्म परामृतम् ।","एतद्यो वेद निहितं गुहायां सोऽविद्याग्रन्थिं विकिरतीह सोम्य ॥ १०॥"],"iast":["puruṣa evedaṃ viśvaṃ karma tapo brahma parāmṛtam |","etad yo veda nihitaṃ guhāyāṃ so'vidyā-granthiṃ vikiratīha saumya || 10 ||"],"words":[["puruṣaḥ","m. nom. sg.","the Puruṣa"],["eva","indecl.","alone"],["idam","n. nom. sg.","this"],["viśvam","n. nom. sg.","(whole) universe"],["karma","n. nom. sg.","action"],["tapaḥ","n. nom. sg.","tapas"],["brahma","n. nom. sg.","Brahman"],["para-amṛtam","n. nom. sg. tp.","the supreme immortal"],["etat","n. acc. sg.","this"],["yaḥ","m. nom. sg. rel.","he who"],["veda","3 sg. pf. of vid (pres. sense)","knows"],["nihitam","n. acc. sg. pp.","(as) deposited / set"],["guhāyām","f. loc. sg.","in the cavity (of the heart)"],["saḥ","m. nom. sg.","he"],["avidyā-granthim","m. acc. sg. tp.","the knot of ignorance"],["vikirati","3 sg. pres. of vi-kṝ","scatters, severs"],["iha","indecl.","here"],["saumya","m. voc. sg.","O gentle one"]],"lit":"\"The Puruṣa alone is all this — the universe, action, tapas, Brahman, the supreme immortal. He who knows this as set in the cavity (of the heart), O gentle one, scatters here the knot of ignorance.\"","para":"The closing verse of khaṇḍa 2.1 collapses the cosmology into identity. The Puruṣa is the universe (viśvam — all that has just been enumerated in mantras 3–9), karma (the action that produces it), tapas (the discipline by which it ripens), brahma (the substantive ground), and parāmṛtam (the supreme immortal). One who knows This as set in the cavity-of-the-heart (guhāyām nihitam) — i.e., not merely as cosmic but as the very inhabitant of one's own heart — vikirati avidyā-granthim — scatters / severs the knot of ignorance — here, in this very life (iha), O gentle one (saumya, the same vocative as 2.1.1).","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> The verse is the doctrinal climax of khaṇḍa 2.1. Three points: (a) puruṣa eva idaṃ viśvam — the Puruṣa is identical with everything just emanated, not merely the source of it. The emanation is non-different from the source. This is the sat-kārya-vāda position: the effect pre-exists in the cause and is non-different from it. (b) nihitaṃ guhāyāṃ — the Puruṣa is to be known not as a remote cosmic principle but as set in the cavity of one's own heart. The cosmology is inside-out: the cosmic Puruṣa is the heart-Puruṣa. (c) avidyā-granthi — \"the knot of ignorance.\" This is the technical term that the Muṇḍaka introduces and that 2.2.8 will repeat (bhidyate hṛdaya-granthiḥ). The knot is the bound complex of ego, body-identification, and craving-aversion; knowing the Puruṣa severs it. The verse closes with the promise: iha — here, in this life — the severance happens; not after death, not in some future world."],["Sivananda","The verse compresses the whole soteriology: know the Puruṣa as set in your own heart, and the knot of avidyā is severed. The practice: sit; turn attention from the cosmic to the heart; recognise the Puruṣa just emanated through the elements as set in the heart-cavity within. The knot scatters by recognition, not by effort."],["Krishnananda","avidyā-granthi is the Muṇḍaka's most original technical term. \"Knot\" precisely captures the experience: ignorance is not absence but tangle — a knotted intertwining of body-identification, ego-affirmation, and craving-aversion that cannot be untied step by step but only severed all at once. The verb vikirati (scatters, throws apart) is appropriate: the knot does not loosen; it is scattered. The recognition is single, the result is total."],["Deussen","The verse closes khaṇḍa 2.1 with a typical Upaniṣadic conclusion-formula: doctrinal statement (\"the Puruṣa alone is all this\") + meditation-instruction (\"know this as set in the heart\") + soteriological promise (\"severs the knot of ignorance\"). Compare BĀU 4.4.7 (yadā sarve pramucyante kāmā…) and Kaṭha 6.14–15. The Muṇḍaka's formulation is the most compressed."],["Aurobindo","avidyā-granthi (knot of ignorance) is one of Aurobindo's most-cited Upaniṣadic terms. He treats it as the central image for the bondage-condition: not chains, not a prison, but a knot — a tangled self-binding by the consciousness itself. The granthi is therefore self-untieable in principle — and yet not by ordinary effort, because ordinary effort is itself within the knot. The verse's promise: when the Puruṣa is known as set in the heart, the knot scatters. Aurobindo's gloss: \"the seeing is the cutting; the knot is in the seeing of it as knot.\""]],"notes":["End of khaṇḍa 2.1."]}]},{"t":"Muṇḍaka 2, Khaṇḍa 2 — Oṃ as bow, ātman as arrow; Brahman before, Brahman behind (11 mantras)","u":[{"ref":"Muṇḍaka 2, Khaṇḍa 2","title":"Oṃ as bow, ātman as arrow; Brahman before, Brahman behind (11 mantras)","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["Khaṇḍa 2.2 is the most concentrated meditation-section of the Upaniṣad. It opens with the manifest-yet-hidden Brahman in the heart-cavity, names it as the akṣara to be pierced by the arrow of ātman from the bow of Oṃ, describes the lighted-from-within heart as the place where all elements gather, and culminates in the all-encompassing declaration brahmaivedam amṛtaṃ purastāt — \"Brahman, the immortal, alone is in front…\""],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Mantra 2.2.1","title":"The manifest-yet-hidden in the cavity: great seat, where all that moves is gathered","sa":["आविः संनिहितं गुहाचरं नाम महत्पदमत्रैतत्समर्पितम् ।","एजत्प्राणन्निमिषच्च यदेतज्जानथ सदसद्वरेण्यं परं विज्ञानाद्यद्वरिष्ठं प्रजानाम् ॥ १॥"],"iast":["āviḥ saṃnihitaṃ guhā-caraṃ nāma mahat padam atraitat samarpitam |","ejat prāṇan nimiṣac ca yad etaj jānatha sad-asad-vareṇyaṃ paraṃ vijñānād yad variṣṭhaṃ prajānām || 1 ||"],"words":[["āviḥ","indecl. adv.","manifest"],["saṃ-nihitam","n. nom. sg. pp.","well-placed, well-set"],["guhā-caram","n. nom. sg. bv.","moving in the cavity"],["nāma","indecl.","indeed (lit. \"by name\")"],["mahat padam","n. nom. sg.","the great seat"],["atra","indecl.","here"],["etat","n. nom. sg.","this"],["samarpitam","n. nom. sg. pp.","is placed / consigned"],["ejat","n. nom. sg. pres. part. of ej","moving"],["prāṇat","n. nom. sg. pres. part. of pra-an","breathing"],["nimiṣat","n. nom. sg. pres. part. of nimiṣ","winking"],["ca","indecl.","and"],["yat","n. nom. sg. rel.","(that) which"],["etat","n. acc. sg.","this"],["jānatha","2 pl. impv. of jñā","know!"],["sat-asat-vareṇyam","n. nom. sg. bv.","to be chosen above being and non-being"],["param","n. nom. sg.","highest"],["vijñānāt","n. abl. sg. comp.","than discursive knowledge"],["yat","n. nom. sg. rel.","(that) which"],["variṣṭham","n. nom. sg. sup. adj.","the best"],["prajānām","f. gen. pl.","of (all) beings"]],"lit":"\"Manifest, well-set, moving-in-the-cavity, the great seat — here this (whole world) is consigned; that which moves, breathes, and winks — that, know! It is to be chosen above being-and-non-being, the highest beyond discursive knowing, the best of beings.\"","para":"A dense opening. The Brahman is paradoxically āviḥ (\"manifest, evident\") and yet guhā-caram (\"moving in the cavity\" — i.e., hidden in the heart). It is mahat padam — the great seat — and in it the whole world (etat) is consigned (samarpita). All that moves (ejat), breathes (prāṇat), winks (nimiṣat — the standard Upaniṣadic phrase for \"is alive\") rests in it. Know this! — it is sad-asad-vareṇya — to be chosen above being-and-non-being — paraṃ vijñānāt — higher than discursive knowledge — variṣṭhaṃ prajānām — the best of all beings.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> The paradox āviḥ guhā-caram is the verse's most precise teaching: Brahman is simultaneously manifest (everywhere, in every sense-perception) and hidden (in the cavity of the heart, where ordinary perception does not reach). Manifest because it is the very basis of all sensing; hidden because it is not itself any object of sense. sad-asad-vareṇyam — \"to be chosen above being and non-being\" — is the verse's most precise apophatic statement: Brahman is neither the manifest existent (sat) nor the unmanifest non-existent (asat); it is to be selected as prior to that very contrast. Compare BG 13.12 (anādimat-paraṃ brahma na sat tan nāsad ucyate — \"the Brahman beyond which there is no beginning is said to be neither sat nor asat\")."],["Sivananda","The verse instructs the seeker on what to seek. Look not for something subtle (Brahman is also manifest — āviḥ) and not for something gross (it is also guhā-caram, hidden); not for something existing (sat) and not for something non-existing (asat). Sivananda's gloss: \"do not look in the directions you have been looking. Brahman is exactly where your looking did not look.\""],["Krishnananda","The verse is the doctrinal foundation of the bhedābheda formulation: Brahman is non-different from the manifest world (āviḥ samarpitam — \"this is consigned to it\") and yet not identical with it (guhā-caram — \"moving in the hidden cavity,\" not the same as the manifest). It is both and neither. Krishnananda treats the verse as the Upaniṣad's most precise pre-formulation of what later Vedānta will dispute under the names advaita, viśiṣṭa-advaita, and bhedābheda."],["Deussen","Notes the technical force of saṃnihitam — well-set, fully deposited — versus merely \"placed.\" The Brahman is not loosely placed in the heart-cavity but firmly set there, as if anchored. The phrase guhā-caram is paradoxical: caram = moving; guhā = the cavity (which is small and fixed). What \"moves in the cavity\"? Deussen's answer: the Brahman moves in the heart in the sense of being active there (the source of life), even though the heart itself is fixed."],["Aurobindo","The verse's deepest teaching is in the triple-participle ejat-prāṇat-nimiṣat. All that moves, breathes, and winks is consigned to the Brahman in the heart-cavity. Aurobindo treats this as the precise inventory of life: motion (action), breath (life-energy), winking (sense-presence). Every act of living is an instance of Brahman's presence in the cavity. To know Brahman is therefore to know that one's own life — every movement, every breath, every eye-blink — is its presence."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 2.2.2","title":"That which is luminous, smaller than the small, the dwelling of worlds and world-dwellers: know it as Brahman","sa":["यदर्चिमद्यदणुभ्योऽणु च यस्मिँल्लोका निहिता लोकिनश्च ।","तदेतदक्षरं ब्रह्म स प्राणस्तदु वाङ्मनः तदेतत्सत्यं तदमृतं तद्वेद्धव्यं सोम्य विद्धि ॥ २॥"],"iast":["yad arcimat yad aṇubhyo'ṇu ca yasmiṃl lokā nihitā lokinaś ca |","tad etad akṣaraṃ brahma sa prāṇas tad u vāṅ-manaḥ tad etat satyaṃ tad amṛtaṃ tad veddhavyaṃ saumya viddhi || 2 ||"],"words":[["yat","n. nom. sg. rel.","that which"],["arci-mat","n. nom. sg. adj.","is endowed with flame, luminous"],["yat","n. nom. sg. rel.","that which"],["aṇubhyaḥ aṇu","n. nom. sg. comp.","smaller than the small"],["ca","indecl.","and"],["yasmin","n. loc. sg. rel.","in which"],["lokāḥ","m. nom. pl.","the worlds"],["nihitāḥ","m. nom. pl. pp.","are deposited"],["lokinaḥ","m. nom. pl.","and the world-dwellers"],["ca","indecl.","and"],["tat etat","n. nom. sg.","that, this"],["akṣaram","n. nom. sg.","(is) the Indestructible"],["brahma","n. nom. sg.","Brahman"],["saḥ","m. nom. sg.","he"],["prāṇaḥ","m. nom. sg.","(is) breath"],["tat u","indecl.","that indeed"],["vāk-manaḥ","n. nom. sg. dv.","(is) speech-and-mind"],["tat etat","n. nom. sg.","this"],["satyam","n. nom. sg.","(is) the truth"],["tat amṛtam","n. nom. sg.","the immortal"],["tat","n. acc. sg.","that"],["veddhavyam","n. nom. sg. gerundive","is to be pierced"],["saumya","m. voc. sg.","O gentle one"],["viddhi","2 sg. impv. of vid","know!"]],"lit":"\"That which is luminous, that which is smaller than the small, in which the worlds and world-dwellers are deposited — that, this, is the Indestructible Brahman; he is prāṇa; he is speech-and-mind; this is the truth, this is the immortal — that, O gentle one, is to be pierced (by the arrow of meditation): know (it)!\"","para":"The Brahman is now described as both arcimat (\"flaming, luminous\") and aṇubhyo'ṇu (\"smaller than the small\"), in whom the worlds (lokāḥ) and their dwellers (lokinaḥ) are deposited (nihitāḥ). This is the akṣara brahma. It is prāṇa (life), vāk-and-manas (speech-and-mind — the two organs of intelligence), satya (the truth), amṛta (the deathless). The verse closes with the instruction: tad veddhavyaṃ saumya viddhi — \"that is to be pierced (vyadh = to pierce, the verb of archery), O gentle one — know (it)!\" The next verse spells out the bow-and-arrow meditation.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> The verse's vocabulary is a compressed inventory of Upaniṣadic Brahman-epithets. arcimat (luminous) cf. ŚU 6.6 (jyotīṣām api taj jyotiḥ); aṇubhyo'ṇu cf. KaU 2.20 (aṇor aṇīyān mahato mahīyān); lokā nihitā cf. BĀU 4.4.18 (yasmin loke ime niviṣṭāḥ); akṣaram cf. BĀU 3.8.8 (Gārgī's akṣara-passage). The list is the Upaniṣad's affirmation that what is taught here is the same Brahman the other Upaniṣads teach, under the same descriptions. The closing veddhavyam (\"to be pierced\") is the technical term that will be expanded into the bow-arrow meditation in the next two verses."],["Sivananda","arcimat-aṇu — the luminous-yet-minute Brahman — is given as the meditation-image. Sit; visualise a tiny flame in the heart, small as a mustard-seed, brighter than any external flame; expand awareness from that flame to the whole cosmos (in which the worlds are deposited); contract awareness back to the flame. Repeat morning and evening."],["Krishnananda","The verse precisely combines two opposing scales — cosmic (in which the worlds are deposited) and atomic (smaller than the small). This is the Upaniṣad's bhūmā-aṇimā teaching: Brahman is the great-greatest and the small-smallest at once, and this paradox is the doctrinal mark of the akṣara."],["Deussen","The closing veddhavyam links to the next verse's bow-arrow meditation. Deussen treats 2.2.2–4 as a unit: this verse names Brahman as veddhavya, 2.2.3 introduces the upaniṣadic bow, 2.2.4 names Om as bow and ātman as arrow."],["Aurobindo","The combination arcimat-aṇu (luminous-and-minute) is one of Aurobindo's favourite formulae. It captures consciousness in its precise epistemological form: smaller than any object of attention (it cannot be objectified) and brighter than any object of perception (it is the very seeing). Aurobindo's gloss: \"consciousness is the minutest and the brightest — it is the eye seeing all eyes and itself smaller than any of them.\""]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 2.2.3","title":"Take up the bow of the Upaniṣads, fit the arrow sharpened by meditation, draw, fix on the target","sa":["धनुर्गृहीत्वौपनिषदं महास्त्रं शरं ह्युपासानिशितं संधयीत ।","आयम्य तद्भावगतेन चेतसा लक्ष्यं तदेवाक्षरं सोम्य विद्धि ॥ ३॥"],"iast":["dhanur gṛhītvaupaniṣadaṃ mahāstraṃ śaraṃ hy upāsā-niśitaṃ saṃdhayīta |","āyamya tad-bhāva-gatena cetasā lakṣyaṃ tad evākṣaraṃ saumya viddhi || 3 ||"],"words":[["dhanuḥ","n. acc. sg.","the bow"],["gṛhītvā","ger. of grah","having taken up"],["aupaniṣadam","n. acc. sg.","(coming from) the Upaniṣads"],["mahā-astram","n. acc. sg.","the great weapon"],["śaram","m. acc. sg.","the arrow"],["hi","indecl.","indeed"],["upāsā-niśitam","m. acc. sg. tp.","sharpened by meditation"],["saṃdhayīta","3 sg. pot. caus. mid. of sam-dhā","should fit (to the bowstring)"],["āyamya","ger. of ā-yam","having drawn back"],["tat-bhāva-gatena","n. instr. sg. tp.","(with a mind) gone to / absorbed in That-state"],["cetasā","n. instr. sg.","with a mind"],["lakṣyam","n. acc. sg.","as the target"],["tat eva","n. acc. sg.","that alone"],["akṣaram","n. acc. sg.","the Indestructible"],["saumya","m. voc. sg.","O gentle one"],["viddhi","2 sg. impv. of vyadh","pierce!"]],"lit":"\"Taking up the bow of the Upaniṣads — the great weapon — fit (to it) the arrow sharpened by meditation; drawing (the bow) with a mind gone-into-That-state, pierce that very Indestructible as the target, O gentle one!\"","para":"The bow-arrow meditation, set up in this verse and defined in the next. The bow is aupaniṣada — \"the upaniṣadic bow,\" i.e., the great weapon (mahā-astra) the Upaniṣads provide. The arrow is the meditator's own consciousness, sharpened (niśita) by repeated meditation (upāsā). The bowman draws (āyamya — bending the bow back) with a mind already absorbed in That-state (tad-bhāva-gata). The target (lakṣya) is the akṣara itself. The instruction: viddhi — pierce!","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> The bow-arrow image is the Muṇḍaka's distinctive contribution to the Upaniṣadic meditation-vocabulary. It encodes a precise psychological structure. The bow (the Upaniṣadic teaching) is the medium that translates the meditator's force into directed effort. The arrow (consciousness) must be sharpened (upāsā-niśita — by repeated meditative effort, not by a single attempt). The drawing-back (āyāmya) — the mental gathering — requires a mind already absorbed in That-state (tad-bhāva-gata). And only then does the loosing — the actual cognition of the akṣara — become possible. The whole apparatus is technical: each component has a precise psychological referent."],["Sivananda","The bow-arrow meditation is one of the most-used in Sivananda's lineage. The seeker actually visualises the bow in his hand, the arrow on the string, the akṣara as a glowing target in the heart-cavity. The verse instructs four steps: take the bow (study the Upaniṣads), fit the arrow (focus consciousness), sharpen by meditation (upāsana), draw back with mind absorbed (tad-bhāva-gata), pierce. The piercing is not a forced act; it is the natural completion of a properly prepared sequence."],["Krishnananda","Each element of the meditation has a precise psychological referent: bow = the disciplining medium (Upaniṣadic instruction); arrow = the directed attention; sharpening = practice (the same attention used many times becomes effective); drawing back = the mental gathering preliminary to penetration; piercing = the actual cognitive insight. The verse is therefore a complete description of dhyāna technique."],["Deussen","The bow-arrow image is unique to the Muṇḍaka. Other Upaniṣads use chariot-imagery (Kaṭha 3.3), boat-imagery (Bhagavad-Gītā 4.36), or wheel-imagery (Praśna 6.6); none use bow-and-arrow. The image is Atharvan in flavour — the Atharva-Veda being notably warlike — and Deussen treats this as another mark of the Muṇḍaka's Atharvan origin."],["Aurobindo","The bow-arrow image's deepest teaching is that meditation is directional and penetrative, not diffuse and receptive. Many spiritual traditions emphasise opening, receptivity, surrender. The Muṇḍaka's image is the opposite: focus, draw, aim, pierce. Aurobindo treats this as one of two equally valid spiritual movements — the active (Muṇḍaka, bow-arrow) and the receptive (other Upaniṣads, listening, opening). Both are needed; the seeker who has only one is incomplete."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 2.2.4","title":"Praṇava is the bow, ātman is the arrow, Brahman is the target","sa":["प्रणवो धनुः शरो ह्यात्मा ब्रह्म तल्लक्ष्यमुच्यते ।","अप्रमत्तेन वेद्धव्यं शरवत्तन्मयो भवेत् ॥ ४॥"],"iast":["praṇavo dhanuḥ śaro hy ātmā brahma tal-lakṣyam ucyate |","a-pramattena veddhavyaṃ śaravat tan-mayo bhavet || 4 ||"],"words":[["praṇavaḥ","m. nom. sg.","the praṇava (Oṃ)"],["dhanuḥ","n. nom. sg.","(is) the bow"],["śaraḥ","m. nom. sg.","the arrow"],["hi","indecl.","indeed"],["ātmā","m. nom. sg.","(is) the Self"],["brahma","n. nom. sg.","Brahman"],["tat","n. nom. sg.","(is) that"],["lakṣyam","n. nom. sg.","target"],["ucyate","3 sg. pres. pass. of vac","is said (to be)"],["a-pramattena","m. instr. sg. bv.","by (one who is) un-heedless"],["veddhavyam","n. nom. sg. gerundive","is to be pierced"],["śara-vat","indecl. adv.","like an arrow"],["tat-mayaḥ","m. nom. sg. bv.","one whose substance is That"],["bhavet","3 sg. pot. of bhū","should become"]],"lit":"\"Oṃ is the bow; the Self is the arrow; Brahman is said to be the target of that. It is to be pierced by one who is un-heedless; like an arrow, (the meditator) should become That-substanced.\"","para":"The bow-arrow meditation now identified explicitly. The praṇava (the syllable Oṃ) is the bow; the ātman is the arrow; Brahman is the target. The piercer must be a-pramatta — \"un-heedless,\" focused, vigilant. And the result: śaravat tan-mayo bhavet — \"like an arrow, (the meditator) becomes substantially-That.\" The arrow, after striking the target, is no longer distinct from it — it is absorbed into the target's material; in the same way, the ātman, having pierced Brahman, is no longer distinct from it. The verse's final teaching: the meditator becomes the meditated.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> The most-cited verse of the Upaniṣad in later Vedānta after 1.2.12. Śaṅkara's commentary is elaborate: praṇava (the syllable Oṃ) functions as the bow because the syllable, recited correctly, is what propels the consciousness toward Brahman. The ātman is the arrow because it is what is propelled. The piercing is the actual cognitive identification. The image śaravat tan-mayo bhavet is critical: an arrow that has pierced its target is no longer separable from it — the wood of the arrow and the wood of the target have become one wood. In the same way, the meditator's ātman, having struck Brahman, is not removable from it — it has become tat-mayaḥ, of-That-substance. This is the Advaitic mokṣa: not arrival at a place but identification of substance. Śaṅkara contrasts this with the Vaiśeṣika theory of contact, where an arrow remains distinct from its target even after piercing; in the Upaniṣadic sense, the arrow disappears into the target."],["Sivananda","The praṇava as bow is the meditational use of Oṃ — chant Oṃ as the act of preparing the meditative state; with the bow drawn, fit the arrow of self-attention; with un-heedless mind, pierce the target. Sivananda gives precise method: ten minutes of slow Oṃ-chant, then one minute of arrow-drawing (gathering attention to a point in the heart), then the unbroken attention on Brahman. After many such sessions, the arrow does not return — śaravat tan-mayo bhavet — it sticks in the target."],["Krishnananda","a-pramattena (un-heedless) is the key qualifier. Meditation is wasted if the meditator is heedless — the arrow does not pierce, it falls. The whole apparatus (bow, arrow, target) is useless without un-heedlessness. Krishnananda emphasises that pramāda (heedlessness) is the technical name for the loss-of-attention that destroys meditation — it is not laziness, not sleep, not distraction; it is the subtle inattention by which the meditator no longer sees what he is meditating on. Vigilance (apramattatā) is the prerequisite."],["Deussen","Compares with Praśna 5.5 (the praṇava as the means of reaching Brahman) and with the use of Oṃ as a meditation-object in Māṇḍūkya 1. The Muṇḍaka's image is the most precise of all Upaniṣadic uses of Oṃ: not just as syllable-to-recite or symbol-to-meditate-on, but as the propulsive medium that translates meditative effort into cognitive achievement."],["Aurobindo","śaravat tan-mayo bhavet is, for Aurobindo, the deepest verse of the Upaniṣad on the nature of mukti. Mukti is not an arrival; it is an identification. The arrow does not reach Brahman in the sense of \"now I am near it\"; it becomes Brahman in the sense of \"now I am made of the same substance.\" The image precisely captures Aurobindo's later teaching of identity-realisation as opposed to vision-realisation: not seeing Brahman from outside but being Brahman from inside, with the inside-outside distinction itself dissolved in the piercing."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 2.2.5","title":"Heaven, earth, mid-air, mind, prāṇas all woven in the ātman: know Him alone; let other words go","sa":["यस्मिन् द्यौः पृथिवी चान्तरिक्षमोतं मनः सह प्राणैश्च सर्वैः ।","तमेवैकं जानथ आत्मानमन्या वाचो विमुञ्चथामृतस्यैष सेतुः ॥ ५॥"],"iast":["yasmin dyauḥ pṛthivī cāntarikṣam otaṃ manaḥ saha prāṇaiś ca sarvaiḥ |","tam evaikaṃ jānatha ātmānam anyā vāco vimuñcathāmṛtasyaiṣa setuḥ || 5 ||"],"words":[["yasmin","m. loc. sg. rel.","in whom"],["dyauḥ","f. nom. sg.","heaven"],["pṛthivī","f. nom. sg.","earth"],["ca","indecl.","and"],["antarikṣam","n. nom. sg.","mid-air"],["otam","n. nom. sg. pp. of ve","woven"],["manaḥ","n. nom. sg.","mind"],["saha","indecl.","together with"],["prāṇaiḥ","m. instr. pl.","the prāṇas"],["ca sarvaiḥ","m. instr. pl.","and all (sense-organs)"],["tam eva ekam","m. acc. sg.","Him alone, the one"],["jānatha","2 pl. impv. of jñā","know!"],["ātmānam","m. acc. sg.","as the Self"],["anyāḥ","f. acc. pl.","other"],["vācaḥ","f. acc. pl.","speeches / words"],["vimuñcatha","2 pl. impv. of vi-muc","let go!"],["amṛtasya","n. gen. sg.","of the immortal"],["eṣaḥ","m. nom. sg.","this"],["setuḥ","m. nom. sg.","(is) the bridge"]],"lit":"\"In whom heaven, earth, and mid-air are woven, along with the mind and all the prāṇas — Him alone know as the Self! Let other words go. This is the bridge to the immortal.\"","para":"The Brahman is now described as the loom in which the three worlds (heaven-earth-mid-air, the standard Vedic tri-loka) and the inner faculties (manas plus all the prāṇas — i.e., the sense-organs) are woven (otam). Know Him alone as the Self (ātmānam) — one — let other words (anyā vācaḥ) go. The verse closes with the metaphor: this knowing is amṛtasya setuḥ — \"the bridge to the immortal.\" The previous mantra's bow-arrow image is replaced by the bridge-image.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> The image otam (\"woven\") is the same image used in BĀU 3.6 (Gārgī's catechism — \"in what is space woven?\") and ŚU 3.10 (\"in him alone are the worlds woven\"). The Muṇḍaka uses it here to identify the Brahman as the loom-of-the-cosmos. The instruction anyā vāco vimuñcatha — \"let other words go\" — is the Upaniṣad's most concise injunction against dispersion of intellect: do not seek through many discourses; concentrate on this one Self-knowledge. Brahmavidyā is jealous in the technical sense — it requires the abandonment of all other intellectual pursuits during its practice."],["Sivananda","amṛtasya setuḥ — \"the bridge to the immortal\" — is the verse's central image. A bridge spans a gap: the mortal-immortal gap is spanned by Self-knowledge. Without the bridge, no crossing; with the bridge, crossing is sure. Sivananda's gloss: \"do not search for the immortal in the distance; build the bridge by knowing the Self.\""],["Krishnananda","anyā vāco vimuñcatha — \"let other words go\" — is the Upaniṣad's most precise scholarly injunction: stop reading other texts. Once the Muṇḍaka's teaching has been received, further reading is not deepening but distracting. Krishnananda treats this as the Upaniṣad's instruction against scholasticism: brahmavidyā is exclusive; it cannot share the meditator's attention with anything else."],["Deussen","The bridge-image (amṛtasya setuḥ) is striking. Compare BĀU 4.4.22 (the same image — \"this Self is the bridge that separates the worlds, that they may not be confused\") and Chāndogya 8.4 (\"the Self is the bank that holds these worlds apart\"). The Muṇḍaka's setu is the more positive version: not a bank that holds apart, but a bridge that joins."],["Aurobindo","anyā vāco vimuñcatha — \"let other words go\" — is for Aurobindo the verse's most modern instruction. The contemporary spiritual student is overwhelmed by texts, schools, methods, gurus — anyā vācaḥ in surplus. The Upaniṣad says: vimuñcatha — let them all go. Choose one bridge and cross it. The bridge does not need to be the Upaniṣadic one — but it must be one, and the others must be vimukta."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 2.2.6","title":"Where the channels meet like spokes at the hub: he moves within, born in many forms — meditate by Oṃ","sa":["अरा इव रथनाभौ संहता यत्र नाड्यः । स एषोऽन्तश्चरते बहुधा जायमानः ।","ओमित्येवं ध्यायथ आत्मानं स्वस्ति वः पाराय तमसः परस्तात् ॥ ६॥"],"iast":["arā iva ratha-nābhau saṃhatā yatra nāḍyaḥ | sa eṣo'ntaś carate bahudhā jāyamānaḥ |","oṃ ity evaṃ dhyāyatha ātmānaṃ svasti vaḥ pārāya tamasaḥ parastāt || 6 ||"],"words":[["arāḥ iva","m. nom. pl. + indecl.","as spokes"],["ratha-nābhau","m. loc. sg. tp.","in the chariot-hub"],["saṃhatāḥ","m. nom. pl. pp.","joined together"],["yatra","indecl.","where"],["nāḍyaḥ","f. nom. pl.","the channels (of the body)"],["saḥ eṣaḥ","m. nom. sg.","this (very) one"],["antaḥ","indecl.","within"],["carate","3 sg. pres. mid. of car","moves"],["bahudhā","indecl.","in many ways"],["jāyamānaḥ","m. nom. sg. pres. part. mid. of jan","being born"],["oṃ iti","indecl.","as 'Oṃ'"],["evam","indecl.","thus"],["dhyāyatha","2 pl. impv. of dhyai","meditate!"],["ātmānam","m. acc. sg.","on the Self"],["svasti","indecl.","may there be welfare"],["vaḥ","m. gen. pl.","to you"],["pārāya","m. dat. sg.","for crossing-over"],["tamasaḥ parastāt","n. abl. sg. + indecl.","beyond the darkness"]],"lit":"\"Where the (bodily) channels are joined together like spokes in a chariot-hub — this very (Self) moves within, being born in many ways. Meditate on the Self thus, as 'Oṃ'! May welfare attend you for crossing-over beyond the darkness.\"","para":"The image of the chariot-wheel: at the hub (nābhi), all the spokes (arāḥ) converge. In the body, all the nāḍīs (subtle channels) converge at a centre (the heart), and the Self moves within them all, being born in many ways (bahudhā jāyamānaḥ — taking on the various forms of life-functioning). The instruction: oṃ ity evaṃ dhyāyatha ātmānaṃ — \"meditate on the Self thus, as 'Oṃ'.\" The blessing: svasti vaḥ pārāya tamasaḥ parastāt — \"may welfare attend you for the crossing-over beyond the darkness.\"","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> The chariot-hub image is anticipated in the Praśna (6.6 — the sixteen kalās converge at the puruṣa as spokes at the hub) and recurs in BĀU 2.5.15. The Muṇḍaka uses it to describe the heart-centre as the convergence-point of all subtle channels. The Self is to be meditated upon as Oṃ — i.e., the praṇava is the verbal-form of the Self for purposes of meditation. The blessing pārāya tamasaḥ parastāt is significant: the meditator is wished swift crossing to the further shore of darkness — i.e., release from avidyā. The verse is the meditational complement to 1.2.13's promise: there the teacher promised; here the Upaniṣad wishes the pupil success in implementing the teaching."],["Sivananda","The image of the chariot-hub maps directly onto the subtle-body anatomy: all the 72,000 nāḍīs converge at the heart (or in the tantric variation, at the navel). The Self is in the hub. Meditation as 'Oṃ' on the Self in the hub draws the diffuse attention to its centre. Sivananda's daily practice: sit, breathe, recite Oṃ slowly while feeling the breath converge to the heart-centre, then dwell at the centre with awareness alone."],["Krishnananda","bahudhā jāyamānaḥ — \"being born in many ways\" — is the precise description of the Self's appearance in multiple bodily functions. It is single in itself but appears as many: as seeing in the eye, as hearing in the ear, as breath in the lungs, as digestion in the stomach. The hub-image captures this perfectly: one hub, many spokes; one Self, many manifestations."],["Deussen","Compare KaU 6.16–17 (the 101 channels of the heart, of which one (the suṣumnā) leads upward to immortality). The Muṇḍaka's image is more compressed — it does not enumerate the 101 channels but speaks generally of all channels converging at the hub. The blessing pārāya tamasaḥ parastāt is the standard Upaniṣadic farewell-formula."],["Aurobindo","oṃ ity evaṃ dhyāyatha — \"meditate (on the Self) as Oṃ\" — is the Upaniṣad's most precise indication of the role of the praṇava in dhyāna. Oṃ is not a mantra-to-repeat; it is the verbal-form of the Self for meditation. The meditator does not chant Oṃ alongside contemplating the Self; he contemplates the Self under the form Oṃ. The two are not parallel acts but a single act. Aurobindo: \"to think Oṃ truly is to think the Self truly.\""]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 2.2.7","title":"The all-knower whose greatness is here; the mind-made one stationed in food, in the heart","sa":["यः सर्वज्ञः सर्वविद्यस्यैष महिमा भुवि ।","दिव्ये ब्रह्मपुरे ह्येष व्योम्न्यात्मा प्रतिष्ठितः ॥","मनोमयः प्राणशरीरनेता प्रतिष्ठितोऽन्ने हृदयं संनिधाय ।","तद्विज्ञानेन परिपश्यन्ति धीरा आनन्दरूपममृतं यद्विभाति ॥ ७॥"],"iast":["yaḥ sarva-jñaḥ sarva-vid yasyaiṣa mahimā bhuvi |","divye brahma-pure hy eṣa vyomny ātmā pratiṣṭhitaḥ ||","mano-mayaḥ prāṇa-śarīra-netā pratiṣṭhito 'nne hṛdayaṃ saṃnidhāya |","tad-vijñānena paripaśyanti dhīrā ānanda-rūpam amṛtaṃ yad vibhāti || 7 ||"],"words":[["yaḥ","m. nom. sg. rel.","he who"],["sarva-jñaḥ","m. nom. sg. tp.","all-knowing"],["sarva-vit","m. nom. sg. tp.","all-knower"],["yasya","m. gen. sg. rel.","whose"],["eṣaḥ mahimā","m. nom. sg.","this greatness (is)"],["bhuvi","f. loc. sg.","on earth"],["divye","n. loc. sg.","in the divine"],["brahma-pure","n. loc. sg. tp.","in the city of Brahman (the heart)"],["hi","indecl.","indeed"],["eṣaḥ ātmā","m. nom. sg.","this Self"],["vyomni","n. loc. sg.","in the (heart-)space"],["pratiṣṭhitaḥ","m. nom. sg. pp.","is established"],["mano-mayaḥ","m. nom. sg. adj.","made of mind"],["prāṇa-śarīra-netā","m. nom. sg. tp.","the leader of the body-and-prāṇa"],["pratiṣṭhitaḥ anne","m. nom. sg. + loc. sg.","established in food (= body)"],["hṛdayam","n. acc. sg.","the heart"],["saṃnidhāya","ger. of sam-ni-dhā","having deposited (himself) in"],["tat-vijñānena","n. instr. sg.","by knowledge of That"],["paripaśyanti","3 pl. pres. of pari-paś","see-around, perceive (fully)"],["dhīrāḥ","m. nom. pl.","the wise"],["ānanda-rūpam","n. acc. sg. bv.","of-bliss-form"],["amṛtam","n. acc. sg.","the immortal"],["yat","n. nom. sg. rel.","which"],["vibhāti","3 sg. pres. of vi-bhā","shines forth"]],"lit":"\"He who is all-knowing, all-knower — whose greatness is here on earth — this Self is established in the divine city of Brahman, in the (heart-)space. Mind-made, the leader of the body-and-prāṇa, established in food (the body), having deposited (himself) in the heart — the wise behold (him) by knowledge of That, as the bliss-formed Immortal that shines forth.\"","para":"This long mantra is two ślokas combined and is often editorially split as 2.2.7a–7b. The first half identifies the Self with the sarva-jña / sarva-vid of 1.1.9 — but now stationed in the brahma-pura (the city of Brahman, i.e., the body) and in the heart-space (vyoman). The second half adds: this Self is mano-mayaḥ (mind-made — i.e., of the substance of mind), prāṇa-śarīra-netā (leader of the prāṇa and body), pratiṣṭhito 'nne (stationed in the food-body — the annamaya kośa), hṛdayaṃ saṃnidhāya (having deposited itself in the heart). The wise (dhīrāḥ) perceive it through tad-vijñāna (knowledge-of-That) as ānanda-rūpam amṛtam (the bliss-formed Immortal) that shines forth.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> The verse is the Muṇḍaka's only explicit kośa-vocabulary use. mano-mayaḥ corresponds to the manomaya-kośa of Taittirīya 2.3; prāṇa-śarīra-netā corresponds to the prāṇamaya-kośa; pratiṣṭhito 'nne corresponds to the annamaya-kośa; ānanda-rūpam corresponds to the ānandamaya-kośa. The vijñānamaya-kośa is implicit in tad-vijñānena (\"by knowledge of That\"). All five Taittirīya kośas are therefore present in this single verse — though not enumerated as kośas. The doctrinal stake: the Self is to be sought through these five layers and is found as their inmost ānanda-rūpa. The verse is therefore the Muṇḍaka's compressed Taittirīya pañca-kośa-vidyā."],["Sivananda","The verse describes the inhabitant of the heart-cavity (brahma-pure vyomni) as four-fold: mind-made (the cognitive aspect), prāṇa-and-body's leader (the vital aspect), stationed in the food-body (the material aspect), and ānanda-rūpa (the blissful aspect). All four aspects are present in the heart-cavity simultaneously. Sivananda recommends meditation on each in turn — see the cognitive aspect, feel the vital, sense the bodily, rest in the blissful — and finally see them as one Self."],["Krishnananda","brahma-pura — \"the city of Brahman\" — is the body. Compare Chāndogya 8.1.1 (the brahma-pure dahara puṇḍarīka — the small lotus in the city of Brahman). The Muṇḍaka inherits the image and adds the qualification divye (\"divine\"): the body is not merely brahma-pura, it is divye brahma-pura — the divine city. The body is therefore not to be despised but recognised as the divine dwelling."],["Deussen","The double-śloka structure of this verse is the only such in the Muṇḍaka. Deussen splits it as 2.2.7a + 2.2.7b and treats the two halves as distinct mantras, increasing the total mantra count to 65 in his edition. Other editions (Adyar, Śaṅkara) keep them as one mantra (the 64-count). The split is editorial; both halves are unbroken in the bhāṣya."],["Aurobindo","hṛdayaṃ saṃnidhāya — \"having deposited (himself) in the heart\" — is the verse's most precise statement of the Self's voluntary descent into embodiment. The Self has consigned itself to the heart; it is there by its own act, not by accident. The body is therefore not a prison but a chosen residence. ānanda-rūpam amṛtam yad vibhāti — \"the bliss-formed Immortal which shines forth\" — names the goal: not a distant immortality but a present-shining bliss, located in the very heart that the Self has just entered."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 2.2.8","title":"The heart-knot is cut, doubts split, karmas exhausted, when That higher-and-lower is seen","sa":["भिद्यते हृदयग्रन्थिश्छिद्यन्ते सर्वसंशयाः ।","क्षीयन्ते चास्य कर्माणि तस्मिन्दृष्टे परावरे ॥ ८॥"],"iast":["bhidyate hṛdaya-granthiś chidyante sarva-saṃśayāḥ |","kṣīyante cāsya karmāṇi tasmin dṛṣṭe parāvare || 8 ||"],"words":[["bhidyate","3 sg. pres. pass. of bhid","is split / broken"],["hṛdaya-granthiḥ","m. nom. sg. tp.","the heart-knot"],["chidyante","3 pl. pres. pass. of chid","are severed"],["sarva-saṃśayāḥ","m. nom. pl. tp.","all doubts"],["kṣīyante","3 pl. pres. pass. of kṣi","are exhausted"],["ca","indecl.","and"],["asya","m. gen. sg.","his"],["karmāṇi","n. nom. pl.","karmas"],["tasmin","m. loc. sg.","when (That is)"],["dṛṣṭe","m. loc. sg. pp.","seen"],["parāvare","m. loc. sg. dv.","(That) higher-and-lower (One)"]],"lit":"\"The heart-knot is split, all doubts are severed, and his karmas are exhausted when That higher-and-lower (One) is seen.\"","para":"The Muṇḍaka's most concise statement of jīvanmukti. Three results follow upon the seeing of parāvara (the higher-and-lower One, the same compound that closed mantra 1.1.2): (a) the heart-knot (hṛdaya-granthi, the avidyā-granthi of 2.1.10 reformulated) is split (bhidyate); (b) all doubts (saṃśayāḥ) are severed (chidyante); (c) the seer's karmas (karmāṇi) are exhausted (kṣīyante). The verse is the standard mantra recited in many later jīvanmukti texts as the textual confirmation of the threefold result of brahma-darśana.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> The triple-result is canonical: (a) bhidyate hṛdaya-granthiḥ — the avidyā-granthi of 2.1.10 here renamed hṛdaya-granthi; the splitting is total, not partial. (b) chidyante sarva-saṃśayāḥ — every doubt about the nature of the Self is removed. The implication: the Self-knower has no further questions; samśaya-cheda is the cognitive mark of realisation. (c) kṣīyante asya karmāṇi — the accumulated karmas exhaust themselves; this is the textual basis for the doctrine that the realised one is no longer karma-bound. Three karma-categories: prārabdha (the karma already begun, which continues until exhausted), sañcita (the karma accumulated but not yet begun, which is destroyed by knowledge), āgāmi (the karma that would be begun in the future, which no longer accrues). Śaṅkara reads kṣīyante as referring chiefly to sañcita and āgāmi; prārabdha continues until the body falls. The verse therefore precisely describes jīvanmukti: the realised one is no longer accumulating karma (āgāmi destroyed) and no longer carrying past unbegun karma (sañcita destroyed), but is still living out the already-begun karma (prārabdha) until natural body-fall."],["Sivananda","This is the verse the seeker keeps in mind throughout sādhanā: it is the precise inventory of what to expect at realisation. (a) the heart-knot splits — a felt event, not metaphorical; meditators report a definite sense of bonds dissolving in the heart. (b) all doubts are severed — not just answered, but rendered ungenerative; the seer no longer can have a doubt about the Self. (c) karmas are exhausted — the karmic momentum no longer drives toward new births. Sivananda treats this as the verse to recite at the moment of difficulty in sādhanā: see what awaits."],["Krishnananda","parāvare — \"in the higher-and-lower\" — is the verse's key compound, repeating 1.1.2's parāvarām. The Self is seen as both higher (parā, the akṣara) and lower (avara, the manifest cosmos) simultaneously. The seeing is not a seeing of the higher to the exclusion of the lower; it is the seeing of the one as both. This is the Muṇḍaka's most precise pre-formulation of non-dual realisation."],["Deussen","The triplet bhidyate-chidyante-kṣīyante is verbally striking — three passive present-tense verbs, all third-person. The action is automatic upon the seeing; no agency intervenes. This is the verse's psychological observation: the results are not produced by effort but emerge automatically upon the cognition. Compare BG 4.37 (jñānāgni-dagdha-karma — \"karma burnt by the fire of knowledge\")."],["Aurobindo","hṛdaya-granthi (heart-knot) is one of Aurobindo's most-cited Upaniṣadic terms. The bondage is not a chain but a knot: a tangle of self-affirmation, ego-identification, and craving-aversion that interweaves at the heart-centre. The realisation does not loosen the knot strand by strand; it splits it (bhidyate). The verb is precise: not chidyate (severed by cutting) but bhidyate (split by inner force). The knot bursts from within when the realisation occurs."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 2.2.9","title":"In the golden, highest sheath is Brahman, stainless, partless; pure, the light of lights","sa":["हिरण्मये परे कोशे विरजं ब्रह्म निष्कलम् ।","तच्छुभ्रं ज्योतिषां ज्योतिस्तद्यदात्मविदो विदुः ॥ ९॥"],"iast":["hiraṇ-maye pare kośe virajaṃ brahma niṣ-kalam |","tac chubhraṃ jyotiṣāṃ jyotis tad yad ātma-vido viduḥ || 9 ||"],"words":[["hiraṇ-maye","m. loc. sg. adj.","in the golden"],["pare","m. loc. sg.","supreme / highest"],["kośe","m. loc. sg.","in the sheath"],["virajam","n. acc. sg. adj.","stainless"],["brahma","n. acc. sg.","Brahman"],["niḥ-kalam","n. acc. sg. bv.","partless"],["tat","n. acc. sg.","that"],["śubhram","n. acc. sg.","pure"],["jyotiṣām jyotiḥ","n. acc. sg. tp.","the light of lights"],["tat yat","n. acc. sg.","that which"],["ātma-vidaḥ","m. nom. pl.","the knowers of the Self"],["viduḥ","3 pl. pf. of vid (pres. sense)","know"]],"lit":"\"In the golden, highest sheath is the stainless, partless Brahman; that is the pure light-of-lights — that which the knowers of the Self know.\"","para":"The Brahman is now located in the hiraṇ-maya kośa — the \"golden sheath,\" the same hiraṇ-maya pātra of Īśā 15 and the hiraṇ-maya puruṣa of Chāndogya 1.6.6 — i.e., the innermost luminous core of the heart-cavity, the highest of the kośas (paramaḥ koṣaḥ). In that sheath: the Brahman, virajam (stainless), niṣkalam (partless / divisionless). It is śubhram (pure), jyotiṣāṃ jyotis (light-of-lights — the same epithet found in BG 13.18 and ŚU 6.14). What the Self-knowers know is exactly this.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> The hiraṇmaya kośa is technical: it is the innermost luminous sheath of the heart-space, the dwelling of the para-ātman. The qualifier paraḥ — \"highest\" — distinguishes it from the lower kośas (annamaya, prāṇamaya, manomaya, vijñānamaya, ānandamaya) of Taittirīya 2; the hiraṇmaya is the still-finer sheath beyond even ānandamaya, the sheath in which the Brahman itself is found. virajam (stainless) excludes all defilement; niṣkalam (partless) excludes all division. The closing tad yad ātma-vido viduḥ — \"that which the Self-knowers know\" — distinguishes this knowledge from all derivative knowledge: it is the seeing-by-the-knower of the very thing the knower is."],["Sivananda","The hiraṇmaya kośa is the innermost meditation-image: visualise a small lotus in the heart, golden, with the para-Brahman shining in its centre. The image is canonical in dhyāna instructions and is the basis of many later tantric heart-cakra meditations. Sivananda recommends sitting before sleep and entering the heart-cavity in imagination; locating the golden sheath; seeing the light-of-lights within; resting there."],["Krishnananda","jyotiṣāṃ jyotiḥ — \"light of lights\" — is the verse's central phrase. It appears also in BG 13.18 (jyotiṣām api taj jyotiḥ) and ŚU 6.14. The image: every visible light (sun, fire, lamp) is light only by some borrowed luminosity; their source-light is Brahman. The Self-knower does not see Brahman as a brighter light alongside the others; he recognises every light as Brahman's light."],["Deussen","The hiraṇmaya kośa is one of the oldest images in the Upaniṣadic corpus, present in ṚV 10.121 (the Hiraṇyagarbha-sūkta), Īśā 15, Chāndogya 1.6.6, and here. Deussen treats this verse as the Upaniṣad's most precise summary of the kośa-doctrine — the Brahman is found in the innermost golden sheath, identifiable as the light-of-lights, and known by the Self-knowers as such."],["Aurobindo","virajam-niṣkalam — \"stainless, partless\" — is the perfect dual negation. virajam excludes the malām (stain, impurity); niṣkalam excludes the kalām (part, division). What is left: an unstained, undivided pure luminosity. Aurobindo's gloss: \"the seeker who finds the Brahman finds something that has been there all along, behind all the screens of stain and division; the kośas are not steps to a distant Brahman but veils over an ever-present one.\""]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 2.2.10","title":"There the sun shines not, nor moon-and-stars; this lightning gives no light; whence then this fire? — He alone shines, by His light all this shines","sa":["न तत्र सूर्यो भाति न चन्द्रतारकं नेमा विद्युतो भान्ति कुतोऽयमग्निः ।","तमेव भान्तमनुभाति सर्वं तस्य भासा सर्वमिदं विभाति ॥ १०॥"],"iast":["na tatra sūryo bhāti na candra-tārakaṃ nemā vidyuto bhānti kuto'yam agniḥ |","tam eva bhāntam anubhāti sarvaṃ tasya bhāsā sarvam idaṃ vibhāti || 10 ||"],"words":[["na","indecl.","not"],["tatra","indecl.","there"],["sūryaḥ","m. nom. sg.","the sun"],["bhāti","3 sg. pres. of bhā","shines"],["na","indecl.","not"],["candra-tārakam","n. nom. sg. dv. coll.","moon-and-stars"],["na ime","f. nom. pl.","not these"],["vidyutaḥ","f. nom. pl.","lightnings"],["bhānti","3 pl. pres. of bhā","shine"],["kutaḥ","indecl.","whence (then)"],["ayam agniḥ","m. nom. sg.","this fire?"],["tam eva","m. acc. sg.","Him alone"],["bhāntam","m. acc. sg. pres. part. of bhā","shining"],["anubhāti","3 sg. pres. of anu-bhā","shines-after, shines-in-imitation"],["sarvam","n. nom. sg.","everything"],["tasya","m. gen. sg.","by his"],["bhāsā","f. instr. sg.","by light"],["sarvam idam","n. nom. sg.","all this"],["vibhāti","3 sg. pres. of vi-bhā","shines forth"]],"lit":"\"There the sun does not shine, nor moon-and-stars; these lightnings shine not — how then this (earthly) fire? Him alone, shining, all (the rest) shines after; by His light all this shines forth.\"","para":"One of the most-cited verses in the Upaniṣadic corpus. Repeated verbatim in Kaṭha 2.2.15 and Śvetāśvatara 6.14 and quoted in BG 15.6 (na tad bhāsayate sūryo na śaśāṅko na pāvakaḥ — \"that the sun does not illuminate, nor moon, nor fire\"). The verse describes the realm of Brahman: there, the sun gives no light; nor moon-and-stars; nor lightning; whence then this earthly fire? — i.e., none of the manifest sources of light illuminate that place. The reason: tam eva bhāntam anubhāti sarvam — \"Him alone shining, all (else) shines-after\" — every manifest light is only an imitation-shining following on Brahman's primary shining. tasya bhāsā sarvam idaṃ vibhāti — \"by His light all this shines forth\" — the whole manifest cosmos is luminous only by His luminosity.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> The most-cited verse of all the principal Upaniṣads (after possibly tat tvam asi and aham brahmāsmi). Its doctrinal force: Brahman is not a source of light among other sources; it is the source of all sources of light. Every visible luminosity (sun, moon, stars, lightning, fire) is anu-bhā — \"shining-after\" Brahman, deriving its visibility from Brahman's prior shining. The verb anu-bhāti is precisely chosen: not bhāti (shines) but anubhāti (shines-after, shines-in-imitation, shines-in-derivation). The whole cosmos is therefore reflective; Brahman alone is self-luminous. Śaṅkara cites the verse extensively in his Brahma-Sūtra-Bhāṣya to establish the sva-prakāśa-vāda — the doctrine that Brahman is self-luminous and that all other luminosities are derivative."],["Sivananda","The verse is the standard meditation-mantra for the recognition of Brahman as the underlying light of all phenomena. Sit at dawn; watch the sky brighten; notice that every brightening object is shining derivatively; ask: by what is the brightening seen? — the seeing itself is the prior light, and that is Brahman. The same meditation at every sunset, every glance at a fire, every flash of lightning."],["Krishnananda","tasya bhāsā sarvam idaṃ vibhāti — \"by His light all this shines forth\" — is the precise Vedāntic formulation of consciousness-as-illumination. Every cognition is a shining-forth of its object; that shining is borrowed from the prior shining of consciousness. The objects do not shine in themselves; they shine in the seeing. The seeing is Brahman. This is, for Krishnananda, the heart of Advaita epistemology."],["Deussen","The verse occurs verbatim in KaU 2.2.15, ŚU 6.14, and the present location, and is paraphrased in BG 15.6. Deussen treats the Muṇḍaka and Kaṭha as the two possible sources; the others (ŚU, BG) are clearly later borrowers. Between MuU and KaU, Deussen leans toward the Muṇḍaka as the source on the strength of contextual fit: the verse caps the description of the hiraṇmaya kośa, the innermost light; in the Kaṭha it is placed in a less integrated position."],["Aurobindo","The verse is for Aurobindo the foundational text on the nature of consciousness. Consciousness is not one among many phenomena; it is the prior shining by which any phenomenon at all becomes visible. The sun is seen by the eye, the eye sees because consciousness illumines the eye-act; consciousness is therefore prior to the sun in the order of illumination. Aurobindo's gloss: \"the sun shines on the world only because consciousness shines on the seeing. Brahman is the seeing's seeing.\""]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 2.2.11","title":"Brahman alone is the deathless, before, behind, right, left, below, above; Brahman alone is all this great universe","sa":["ब्रह्मैवेदममृतं पुरस्ताद्ब्रह्म पश्चाद्ब्रह्म दक्षिणतश्चोत्तरेण ।","अधश्चोर्ध्वं च प्रसृतं ब्रह्मैवेदं विश्वमिदं वरिष्ठम् ॥ ११॥"],"iast":["brahmaivedam amṛtaṃ purastād brahma paścād brahma dakṣiṇataś cottareṇa |","adhaś cordhvaṃ ca prasṛtaṃ brahmaivedaṃ viśvam idaṃ variṣṭham || 11 ||"],"words":[["brahma eva","n. nom. sg.","Brahman alone"],["idam","n. nom. sg.","(is) this"],["amṛtam","n. nom. sg.","deathless"],["purastāt","indecl.","before / in front"],["brahma","n. nom. sg.","Brahman"],["paścāt","indecl.","behind"],["brahma","n. nom. sg.","Brahman"],["dakṣiṇataḥ","indecl.","on the right (south)"],["ca uttareṇa","indecl.","and on the left (north)"],["adhaḥ ca ūrdhvam ca","indecl.","below and above"],["prasṛtam","n. nom. sg. pp. of pra-sṛ","extended"],["brahma eva","n. nom. sg.","Brahman alone"],["idam","n. nom. sg.","this"],["viśvam","n. nom. sg.","universe"],["idam","n. nom. sg.","this"],["variṣṭham","n. nom. sg. sup.","the highest"]],"lit":"\"Brahman alone is this deathless (one) in front, Brahman behind, Brahman on the right and on the left; extended below and above — Brahman alone is this universe, this highest!\"","para":"The crescendo of Muṇḍaka 2. Brahman alone is the deathless: in front (purastāt), behind (paścāt), on the right (dakṣiṇataḥ), on the left (uttareṇa), below (adhas), above (ūrdhvam) — extended in all six directions (prasṛtam). Brahman alone is this universe — this highest one. The verse is the Upaniṣad's most expansive affirmation of Brahman's all-pervasion: the six directions of space are systematically filled with the same Brahman, and the universe-totality is identified with it.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> The six-directional enumeration is the standard Vedic dik-vibhāga (purvā, paścimā, dakṣiṇā, uttarā, adhaḥ, ūrdhvam — east, west, south, north, below, above). Brahman fills all six. The closing brahmaivedaṃ viśvam idaṃ variṣṭham is the verse's most expansive identity-claim: the whole universe (viśva) is Brahman; and this Brahman/universe is variṣṭha — the highest. The verse is the Muṇḍaka's most precise pre-formulation of the Mahāvākya-tradition's universal-identity formulae (sarvaṃ khalv idaṃ brahma of Chāndogya 3.14.1, ahaṃ brahmāsmi of BĀU 1.4.10). The verse closes Muṇḍaka 2 with a doctrinal climax: all the descriptions so far (cosmogonic, anatomical, meditational, luminous) culminate in this simple all-affirmation."],["Sivananda","The six-directional meditation: stand or sit facing east. Repeat brahma eva idam amṛtam purastāt — \"Brahman alone is this deathless in front.\" Then face south, repeat for the south. Continue through all six directions including up and down. Conclude by recognising oneself as Brahman in the centre. Sivananda recommends this meditation as a daily morning exercise: it grounds the seeker's spatial awareness in Advaita."],["Krishnananda","The verse is the climactic statement of Muṇḍaka 2 and one of the highest in the Upaniṣadic corpus. brahma eva idaṃ viśvam idaṃ variṣṭham — \"Brahman alone is this universe, this highest\" — is the universal-identity formulation. Krishnananda emphasises that the formulation is not idealist (the universe is mere idea) but advaitic (the universe is Brahman, real-because-Brahman-real). The Muṇḍaka does not deny the world; it affirms the world as Brahman."],["Deussen","Compares with Chāndogya 7.25.2 (\"this Self is below, above, in front, behind, to the south, to the north — this Self is all this\") — the Sanātkumāra-instruction. The Muṇḍaka borrows this six-directional structure but condenses it into a single śloka. Deussen treats the verse as the climax of the doctrine of Brahman's bhūmā (vastness)."],["Aurobindo","The verse's deepest teaching is in the closing variṣṭham. The universe is not merely Brahman; it is the highest Brahman. There is no higher Brahman beyond the universe to which one might escape. The transcendence the seeker is searching for is not other than the immanence he is in. Aurobindo's gloss: \"the realised one does not exit the universe to find Brahman; he sees the universe as Brahman and stays exactly where he is.\""]],"notes":["End of khaṇḍa 2.2 and end of Muṇḍaka 2."]}]},{"t":"Muṇḍaka 3, Khaṇḍa 1 — The two birds, satyam eva jayate, the deathless Puruṣa (10 mantras)","u":[{"ref":"Muṇḍaka 3, Khaṇḍa 1","title":"The two birds, satyam eva jayate, the deathless Puruṣa (10 mantras)","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["Muṇḍaka 3.1 opens with the most famous image in the Upaniṣad — the two birds on one tree, of which the eating one sorrows and the watching one is at ease. It then transitions through the seer's vision of the golden Puruṣa to the canonical declaration satyam eva jayate (\"truth alone triumphs\") and culminates in the threefold sacred path of tapas, brahmacarya, and truth."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Mantra 3.1.1","title":"Two birds, friends, on one tree; one eats the sweet fruit, the other watches","sa":["द्वा सुपर्णा सयुजा सखाया समानं वृक्षं परिषस्वजाते ।","तयोरन्यः पिप्पलं स्वाद्वत्त्यनश्नन्नन्यो अभिचाकशीति ॥ १॥"],"iast":["dvā suparṇā sayujā sakhāyā samānaṃ vṛkṣaṃ pariṣasvajāte |","tayor anyaḥ pippalaṃ svādv atty an-aśnan anyo abhi-cākaśīti || 1 ||"],"words":[["dvā","m. nom. du.","two"],["suparṇā","m. nom. du.","well-feathered ones (birds)"],["sayujā","m. nom. du. bv.","yoked-together"],["sakhāyā","m. nom. du.","friends"],["samānam","m. acc. sg.","the same"],["vṛkṣam","m. acc. sg.","tree"],["pariṣasvajāte","3 du. pf. mid. of pari-svaj","have embraced (around)"],["tayoḥ","m. gen. du.","of the two"],["anyaḥ","m. nom. sg.","one"],["pippalam","n. acc. sg.","the fig-fruit"],["svādu","n. acc. sg. adj.","sweet"],["atti","3 sg. pres. of ad","eats"],["an-aśnan","m. nom. sg. pres. part. neg.","not-eating"],["anyaḥ","m. nom. sg.","the other"],["abhi-cākaśīti","3 sg. pres. intens. of kāś","looks (attentively / fixedly), watches"]],"lit":"\"Two well-feathered (birds), yoked-together, friends, have embraced (around) the same tree. Of the two, one eats the sweet pippala-fruit; the other, not eating, watches.\"","para":"Three versions, same wording, three contexts. The image: two suparṇāḥ — \"well-feathered ones\" (the standard Vedic poetic word for a bird, often used for an eagle, here probably for a more generic forest bird) — sayuja (yoked together — joined by some bond) and sakhāyā (friends) have embraced (pariṣasvajāte — wrapped themselves around) the same tree. The tree is the body, or the world, or saṃsāra; the two birds are jīva (the eating one) and Ātman/Īśvara (the watching one). One eats the pippala (the fruit of the sweet pīpal/aśvattha tree) — the karmic fruit of action. The other does not eat — abhi-cākaśīti (intensive of kāś, \"to be visible / to look\") — \"watches attentively.\" The Vedic verb is intensive: not merely \"watches\" but \"watches with fixed gaze.\"","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> The Muṇḍaka's resolution of the image (in 3.1.2) makes it doctrinally precise: the eating bird is the jīva (the individual self, embodied and karmically entangled); the watching bird is the para-Ātman (the universal Self, witness-only, non-acting). The single tree is the body / the cosmos in which both reside. The image therefore precisely encodes the Advaitic doctrine of two ātmans (the jīvātman and paramātman) of one substance, in one body, of which one suffers (because eating-and-being-eaten-by saṃsāra) and the other is at ease (because witness-only). Śaṅkara emphasises that the two birds are not two substances but two functions of one consciousness: the same consciousness as agent-and-experiencer is jīva; as witness-only is para-Ātman. The verse therefore does not assert two selves; it asserts two modes of one Self. The Muṇḍaka's contribution (over the bare Ṛgvedic figure) is the resolution: the sorrowful eater can see the watcher, and seeing leads to release."],["Sivananda","The two-birds image is the most-used image in Advaita exposition for the seeker just beginning. It is concrete (everyone has seen a bird in a tree), it is dualistic in form (two birds) but unitary in solution (they are friends, on the same tree). Sivananda's daily meditation: morning, sit; recognise the eater-self (the one who has cravings, fears, attachments); recognise also the watcher-self (the one who sees the eater); rest as the watcher."],["Krishnananda","The two-birds image is one of the rare Upaniṣadic figures that precisely encodes the witness-doctrine without abstracting it. The witness (sākṣin) is here a bird — a creature, a fellow on the same tree, a friend (sakhāyā). It is not a remote impersonal absolute; it is intimate. This is the verse's most psychologically resonant teaching: the witness-Self is your friend, present on the same tree, watching with care."],["Deussen","One of the most important verses in the Veda. Deussen treats the Ṛgvedic version (1.164.20) as the original — embedded in the Dīrghatamas riddle-hymn, where the figure is one of several paradoxes without resolution. The Atharva-Veda Muṇḍaka and the Yajurveda Śvetāśvatara then both inherit it and provide differing resolutions (witness-Self in MuU; theistic Lord in ŚU). The shared verbatim text across three different Vedic traditions is, for Deussen, the strongest evidence of the antiquity and the cross-school authority of the image."],["Aurobindo","The two-birds image is one of Aurobindo's most-cited Upaniṣadic figures. The eating bird is the praviṣṭa-puruṣa (the entered-puruṣa, the active soul); the watching bird is the akṣara-puruṣa (the imperishable witness). Both are the same puruṣa under two operative modes: as praviṣṭa it eats and suffers; as akṣara it watches and is at ease. The realisation is not to abolish one bird and become the other; it is to recognise that you are both, and to identify primarily as the watcher. The eater continues to eat (the body continues to act); but the centre of identity has shifted to the watcher. Aurobindo treats this as the precise figure of jīvanmukti: the body-life continues, the karma continues to bear its fruit (the bird continues to eat the pippala), but the seer has identified as the akṣara-watcher and is no longer the karma-experiencer."]],"notes":["This mantra is shared with Śvetāśvatara 4.6 and Ṛgveda 1.164.20. The three versions are presented here in parallel.","<table><tr><td>Muṇḍaka 3.1.1 (Atharva-Veda, Śaunaka)</td><td>Śvetāśvatara 4.6 (Kṛṣṇa-Yajurveda)</td><td>Ṛgveda 1.164.20 (Dīrghatamas hymn)</td></tr><tr><td>dvā suparṇā sayujā sakhāyā<br>samānaṃ vṛkṣaṃ pariṣasvajāte |<br>tayor anyaḥ pippalaṃ svādv atti<br>an-aśnan anyo abhi-cākaśīti ||</td><td>dvā suparṇā sayujā sakhāyā<br>samānaṃ vṛkṣaṃ pariṣasvajāte |<br>tayor anyaḥ pippalaṃ svādv atti<br>an-aśnan anyo abhi-cākaśīti ||</td><td>dvā suparṇā sayujā sakhāyā<br>samānaṃ vṛkṣaṃ pari ṣasvajāte |<br>tayor anyaḥ pippalaṃ svādv atti<br>an-aśnann anyo abhi cākaśīti ||</td></tr><tr><td>Two well-feathered ones, yoked-together, friends, have embraced the same tree; one eats the sweet fruit, the other watches without eating.</td><td>Two well-feathered ones, yoked-together, friends, have embraced the same tree; one eats the sweet fruit, the other watches without eating.</td><td>Two well-feathered ones, yoked-together, friends, have embraced the same tree; one eats the sweet fruit, the other watches without eating.</td></tr><tr><td>Context: closes Muṇḍaka 2's all-pervasion teaching with the individual psychological figure of jīva and Self. Followed by 3.1.2's resolution: the sorrowful eater sees the watching Lord and is freed.</td><td>Context: in a chapter on bhakti and the Lord's grace; followed by 4.7 which uses the eaten fruit as the symbol of karmic suffering.</td><td>Context: the asya vāmasya pālitasya gor hymn, where Dīrghatamas presents a series of paradoxes. The two-birds figure is one of many cryptic images here. The padapāṭha separates pari and ṣasvajāte (preserving the verb's morphology); the Upaniṣadic recensions write it as a single word.</td></tr></table>","All three versions are textually identical save trivial sandhi (an-aśnan vs. anaśnann, the writing of pari-ṣasvajāte as one word in the Upaniṣads and two in the padapāṭha-Ṛgveda). The wording is fixed; only the context shifts. In the Ṛgveda, it is a riddle within a riddle-hymn — no resolution offered. In the Śvetāśvatara, it is set within a theistic Sāṅkhya-Yoga framework, and the eating bird = jīva, the watching bird = Lord. In the Muṇḍaka, the next two mantras (3.1.2–3) resolve it: the sorrowful eater, seeing the other as Lord and his greatness, becomes free of sorrow."]},{"ref":"Mantra 3.1.2","title":"The same tree: the puruṣa, sunk in helplessness, sorrows; seeing the other as Lord and his greatness, he is freed of sorrow","sa":["समाने वृक्षे पुरुषो निमग्नो-ऽनीशया शोचति मुह्यमानः ।","जुष्टं यदा पश्यत्यन्यमीश-मस्य महिमानमिति वीतशोकः ॥ २॥"],"iast":["samāne vṛkṣe puruṣo nimagno'nīśayā śocati muhyamānaḥ |","juṣṭaṃ yadā paśyaty anyam īśam asya mahimānam iti vīta-śokaḥ || 2 ||"],"words":[["samāne","m. loc. sg.","in the same"],["vṛkṣe","m. loc. sg.","tree"],["puruṣaḥ","m. nom. sg.","the puruṣa (jīva)"],["nimagnaḥ","m. nom. sg. pp. of ni-majj","sunk-into"],["anīśayā","f. instr. sg.","by helplessness / powerlessness"],["śocati","3 sg. pres. of śuc","sorrows"],["muhyamānaḥ","m. nom. sg. pres. part. mid. of muh","(being) confused / deluded"],["juṣṭam","m. acc. sg. pp.","honoured / worshipped / loved"],["yadā","indecl.","when"],["paśyati","3 sg. pres. of paś","sees"],["anyam","m. acc. sg.","the other"],["īśam","m. acc. sg.","as the Lord"],["asya","m. gen. sg.","his / of him"],["mahimānam","m. acc. sg.","greatness"],["iti","indecl.","(seeing) thus"],["vīta-śokaḥ","m. nom. sg. bv.","free of sorrow"]],"lit":"\"In the same tree, the puruṣa, sunk (in its branches), sorrows out of helplessness, being deluded; when he sees the other — the worshipped Lord — and his greatness, he becomes free of sorrow.\"","para":"The resolution of the two-birds figure. The puruṣa — the eating bird of 3.1.1 — has sunk into the tree (nimagnaḥ — fully submerged), sorrows out of anīśā (\"un-lordliness,\" powerlessness — the technical opposite of īśa-tā, lordship), and is muhyamāna (being deluded — the regenerative process of confusion). When he sees the other bird as juṣṭa īśa (the worshipped Lord) and (sees) his greatness (mahimā), he becomes vīta-śoka — freed of sorrow.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> The verse spells out the Upaniṣad's distinctive resolution of the two-birds image. The eater-bird is the puruṣa (jīva); his suffering is from anīśā — helplessness, the felt lack of lordship over his own situation. He sees the other not as a remote witness but as a juṣṭa īśa — \"worshipped/loved Lord\" — and the seeing precisely includes the recognition of His mahimā — His greatness. The two recognitions (Lord-ness and greatness) together free the seer of sorrow. The doctrinal stake: the resolution involves a strongly theistic moment — the watching bird is not just a witness but a Lord; not just a Self but a worshipped one. This is the Muṇḍaka's most theistic verse and is the one most often cited by Vaiṣṇava ācāryas (Rāmānuja, Madhva, Vallabha) against pure-Advaita readings. Śaṅkara's defence: \"Lord\" here is the saguṇa aspect of the same para-Ātman, used as the meditational object for the qualified seeker; the resolution then proceeds (in 3.1.3) into the nirguṇa identification."],["Sivananda","The image of \"sinking into the tree\" (nimagnaḥ) precisely captures the felt experience of saṃsāric entanglement: not a place one inhabits but a substance one is sunk into. Liberation begins with the upward look — seeing the watcher-bird, recognising him as a worshipped Lord, recognising his greatness. The recognition does not extract the sinker from the tree; it changes his relation to the sinking. The body remains in the tree (saṃsāra continues), but the seer is no longer the helpless one."],["Krishnananda","The compound juṣṭam īśam is critical. juṣṭa = worshipped, loved, honoured. The watcher is not a neutral witness but a beloved-by-the-seeker Lord. This is the Muṇḍaka's clearest endorsement of bhakti as an element of jñāna: the seeing that liberates is a seeing-with-love, not a cold observation. Krishnananda treats this as the textual precedent for the bhakti-jñāna unity that becomes canonical in the Gītā."],["Deussen","anīśayā — \"by un-lordliness\" — is the verse's precise diagnosis of the jīva's defect. The jīva does not suffer from impurity or sin in a moral sense; he suffers from a lack — the lack of lordship over his own circumstances. Liberation is therefore the recovery of lordship by identification with the īśa. Deussen treats this as the Upaniṣadic anticipation of the theistic Advaita that becomes dominant in ŚU and BG."],["Aurobindo","The deepest teaching is in the compound juṣṭam īśam — \"the worshipped Lord.\" The seer does not see a cold witness; he sees a Lord who is loved (the verb juṣṭa includes both loving and being loved — the watcher is loved by the seer in the moment of being seen). The Upaniṣad's psychology is precise: liberation involves recognition + worship + recognition-of-greatness. All three are needed. The verse therefore validates devotion as part of liberation, not as preparation-for-liberation but as constituent of it."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 3.1.3","title":"When the seer sees the golden Puruṣa, doer, Lord, womb of Brahman — shaking off merit-and-demerit, stainless, he attains supreme equality","sa":["यदा पश्यः पश्यते रुक्मवर्णं कर्तारमीशं पुरुषं ब्रह्मयोनिम् ।","तदा विद्वान् पुण्यपापे विधूय निरञ्जनः परमं साम्यमुपैति ॥ ३॥"],"iast":["yadā paśyaḥ paśyate rukma-varṇaṃ kartāram īśaṃ puruṣaṃ brahma-yonim |","tadā vidvān puṇya-pāpe vidhūya nirañjanaḥ paramaṃ sāmyam upaiti || 3 ||"],"words":[["yadā","indecl.","when"],["paśyaḥ","m. nom. sg.","the seer (the visionary)"],["paśyate","3 sg. pres. mid. of paś","sees"],["rukma-varṇam","m. acc. sg. bv.","of-golden-colour"],["kartāram","m. acc. sg.","the doer (= creator)"],["īśam","m. acc. sg.","the Lord"],["puruṣam","m. acc. sg.","the Person"],["brahma-yonim","m. acc. sg. tp.","the womb-of-Brahman (i.e., the source of brahmavidyā or = identified-with-Brahman)"],["tadā","indecl.","then"],["vidvān","m. nom. sg.","the knower"],["puṇya-pāpe","n. acc. du. dv.","merit and demerit"],["vidhūya","ger. of vi-dhū","having shaken off"],["niḥ-añjanaḥ","m. nom. sg. bv.","stainless / pigmentless"],["paramam","n. acc. sg. adj.","the highest"],["sāmyam","n. acc. sg.","equality / sameness"],["upaiti","3 sg. pres. of upa-i","attains / approaches"]],"lit":"\"When the seer sees the golden-coloured (Puruṣa) — the doer, the Lord, the Person, the womb of Brahman — then the knower, shaking off merit-and-demerit, stainless, attains the highest equality.\"","para":"The vision of the Puruṣa is now described. He is rukma-varṇa (golden-coloured — the same epithet of Hiraṇyagarbha and of the highest Puruṣa); kartā (the doer / creator); īśa (the Lord); puruṣa (the Person); brahma-yoni (the womb of Brahman — either: the source from which brahmavidyā issues, or: he-whose-yoni-is-Brahman, the one in whom Brahman is the source). When the seer sees This, the vidvān (knower) does three things: (a) vidhūya puṇya-pāpe — shakes off merit-and-demerit (the karmic burden of both kinds); (b) becomes nir-añjana — stainless, pigment-less (the technical word for one free of all conditioning); (c) upaiti paramaṃ sāmyam — attains the supreme sāmya — \"equality / sameness\" — i.e., identity with the Lord.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> The compound brahma-yoni admits two readings — (a) \"having Brahman as womb,\" i.e., born from Brahman; (b) \"the womb of Brahman,\" i.e., the source from which Brahman-knowledge emerges. Śaṅkara prefers (b): the Puruṣa is the source of brahmavidyā. The triple-result of the vision is the canonical Advaita description of mokṣa: (a) puṇya-pāpa-vidhūnana — both merit and demerit are shaken off, not just demerit; mokṣa is freedom from both karmic streams, not just punishment-karma. (b) nir-añjanatva — stainlessness; añjana = pigment, eye-makeup; nir-añjana = without any pigment / without any conditioning. (c) paramaṃ sāmyam — the highest equality / sameness. This is the doctrinally critical phrase: sāmya can be read either as \"equality\" (the seer becomes equal to the Lord — Rāmānuja's preferred reading, supporting viśiṣṭa-advaita) or as \"sameness/identity\" (the seer becomes identical with the Lord — Śaṅkara's preferred reading, supporting strict advaita). Śaṅkara cites the parallel passage in BG 14.2 (sādharmyam āgataḥ) for the identity-reading."],["Sivananda","The triple-result is the canonical inventory of mokṣa: shake off karma, become stainless, attain equality. Sivananda emphasises shake-off both merit and demerit: spiritual aspirants often think that they must accumulate puṇya to gain mokṣa; the Upaniṣad says merit and demerit are equally shed. Mokṣa is not the maximum of merit; it is the dissolution of the merit-demerit axis."],["Krishnananda","The vision-content is precise: rukma-varṇa (golden), kartṛ (creator), īśa (Lord), puruṣa (Person), brahma-yoni (source of brahmavidyā). The five epithets together give a complete picture: the Puruṣa is light, agency, lordship, personhood, and source-of-knowledge. The vision integrates these five into one. Krishnananda treats this as the verse's epistemological precision: realisation is the seeing of these five as one, not the seeing of any one of them in isolation."],["Deussen","paramaṃ sāmyam — \"the highest equality\" — is the verse's key phrase. The relevant comparison: Plato's homoiosis theō (\"likeness to god\") in Theaetetus 176a–b. Deussen notes the structural similarity but a key difference: the Greek is becoming-like-god; the Upaniṣadic is becoming-the-same-as-god (or, for Śaṅkara, recognising-that-one-was-always-the-same). The Sanskrit term sāmya is therefore stronger than Greek homoiosis."],["Aurobindo","The three-fold result is the Upaniṣad's most complete inventory of mokṣa: (a) puṇya-pāpa-vidhūnana — the karmic burden lifted entirely; (b) nirañjanatva — the seer becomes a colourless transparent medium, no longer pigmented by individual conditioning; (c) paramaṃ sāmyam — supreme sāmya, which Aurobindo prefers to translate as \"oneness\" rather than \"equality.\" The vision is therefore not the seeing of a god but the dissolving of the seer's distinct conditioning into the Lord's transparent medium. Aurobindo's gloss: \"the seer becomes a window through which the Lord shines, with no glass-tint remaining.\""]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 3.1.4","title":"He is prāṇa; the knower is no fault-finder, sports in the Self, takes joy in the Self, is active — he is the best of brahma-knowers","sa":["प्राणो ह्येष यः सर्वभूतैर्विभाति विजानन् विद्वान् भवते नातिवादी ।","आत्मक्रीड आत्मरतिः क्रियावा-नेष ब्रह्मविदां वरिष्ठः ॥ ४॥"],"iast":["prāṇo hy eṣa yaḥ sarva-bhūtair vibhāti vijānan vidvān bhavate nāti-vādī |","ātma-krīḍa ātma-ratiḥ kriyāvān eṣa brahma-vidāṃ variṣṭhaḥ || 4 ||"],"words":[["prāṇaḥ","m. nom. sg.","(is) prāṇa"],["hi","indecl.","indeed"],["eṣaḥ","m. nom. sg.","this (Self)"],["yaḥ","m. nom. sg. rel.","who"],["sarva-bhūtaiḥ","n. instr. pl.","by/through all beings"],["vibhāti","3 sg. pres. of vi-bhā","shines forth"],["vijānan","m. nom. sg. pres. part.","knowing"],["vidvān","m. nom. sg.","the knower"],["bhavate","3 sg. pres. mid. of bhū","becomes"],["na ati-vādī","m. nom. sg. bv.","not over-speaking"],["ātma-krīḍaḥ","m. nom. sg. bv.","one who sports in the Self"],["ātma-ratiḥ","m. nom. sg. bv.","one who takes joy in the Self"],["kriyāvān","m. nom. sg.","active / endowed with action"],["eṣaḥ","m. nom. sg.","this (one)"],["brahma-vidām","m. gen. pl.","of the knowers of Brahman"],["variṣṭhaḥ","m. nom. sg. sup.","(is) the best"]],"lit":"\"This (Self) who shines forth through all beings is indeed prāṇa; the knower, knowing (this), does not become over-speaking. Sporting in the Self, taking joy in the Self, (yet) active — he is the best of the knowers of Brahman.\"","para":"The jīvanmukta's psychology. The Self who shines forth through all beings (sarva-bhūtair vibhāti) is prāṇa — the life-principle present in all. The knower who knows this becomes na ati-vādī — not given to excess speech / not boastful / not over-asserting. He is ātma-krīḍa (sporting in the Self), ātma-rati (taking joy in the Self), kriyāvān (yet active — performing actions). Such a one is the best (variṣṭha) of brahma-knowers. The verse is one of the canonical descriptions of the jīvanmukta — and notable for kriyāvān: the realised one is not inactive.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> The verse describes the jīvanmukta. Four marks: (a) not over-speaking — he does not boast of his realisation or argue endlessly about it. Compare KaU 1.2.23 (nāyam ātmā pravacanena labhyo — \"the Self is not gained by speech\"); the realised one knows this and does not over-speak. (b) ātma-krīḍa — sporting in the Self. The Self is his playground; not occasionally but constantly. (c) ātma-rati — taking joy in the Self. Both krīḍa (play) and rati (delight) — two distinct kinds of inner activity, both grounded in the Self. (d) kriyāvān — active. This is the verse's most striking word: the realised one is not akṣara (action-less); he is kriyāvān (action-full). Mokṣa does not abolish action; it transforms its source. The verse therefore refutes any reading of Advaita as quietist: the variṣṭha brahma-vit is active."],["Sivananda","na ati-vādī — \"not over-speaking\" — is the verse's quietest but most telling phrase. The realised one does not need to talk about realisation. He may answer when asked; he may teach when sought; he does not announce. The mark of un-realisation is excess speech about Self-knowledge; the mark of realisation is its absence. Sivananda's daily-check: how much have I spoken today about the Self? If much, examine the realisation."],["Krishnananda","kriyāvān is the verse's most important word for the present age. The popular misconception that mokṣa = passivity (or that the realised one withdraws from action) is here refuted. The variṣṭha brahma-vit is kriyāvān — active. The action is not karmic accumulation (āgāmi is destroyed) but is the natural play of the realised — what Aurobindo will call līlā. Krishnananda emphasises that this is the textual basis for the karma-jñāna integration of the Gītā."],["Deussen","ātma-krīḍa-ātma-rati-kriyāvān is the verse's distinctive three-fold characterisation. The triplet is repeated in Chāndogya 7.25.2 (ātma-krīḍa ātma-rati kriyāvān eṣa brahma-vidāṃ variṣṭhaḥ — identical wording), but in the Chāndogya it is part of Sanatkumāra's exposition and is preceded by a longer enumeration. The Muṇḍaka's version is the more compressed."],["Aurobindo","ātma-krīḍa — \"sporting in the Self\" — captures the Upaniṣad's deepest psychological observation about the realised one. The Self is not a destination at which to settle but a playground in which to play. The realised one plays in his own Self — and his external activity is the natural extension of that play. kriyāvān is therefore not despite ātma-krīḍa but because of it: the inner play overflows into outer action. Aurobindo treats this verse as the textual basis of his doctrine of supramentalised action: action that is play and play that is action, both grounded in the Self."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 3.1.5","title":"The Self is obtained by truth, by tapas, by right knowledge, by continuous brahmacarya","sa":["सत्येन लभ्यस्तपसा ह्येष आत्मा सम्यग्ज्ञानेन ब्रह्मचर्येण नित्यम् ।","अन्तःशरीरे ज्योतिर्मयो हि शुभ्रो यं पश्यन्ति यतयः क्षीणदोषाः ॥ ५॥"],"iast":["satyena labhyas tapasā hy eṣa ātmā samyag-jñānena brahma-caryeṇa nityam |","antaḥ-śarīre jyotir-mayo hi śubhro yaṃ paśyanti yatayaḥ kṣīṇa-doṣāḥ || 5 ||"],"words":[["satyena","n. instr. sg.","by truth"],["labhyaḥ","m. nom. sg. gerundive of labh","(is) to be attained"],["tapasā","n. instr. sg.","by tapas"],["hi","indecl.","indeed"],["eṣaḥ ātmā","m. nom. sg.","this Self"],["samyak-jñānena","n. instr. sg.","by right knowledge"],["brahma-caryeṇa","n. instr. sg.","by brahmacarya"],["nityam","indecl. adv.","continuously"],["antaḥ-śarīre","n. loc. sg. tp.","within the body"],["jyotir-mayaḥ","m. nom. sg. adj.","made of light"],["hi","indecl.","indeed"],["śubhraḥ","m. nom. sg.","pure"],["yam","m. acc. sg. rel.","whom"],["paśyanti","3 pl. pres. of paś","see"],["yatayaḥ","m. nom. pl.","the strivers"],["kṣīṇa-doṣāḥ","m. nom. pl. bv.","whose faults are exhausted"]],"lit":"\"This Self is to be attained by truth, by tapas, by right knowledge, by continuous brahmacarya. Made of light, pure — (he is) within the body. (Him,) the strivers whose faults are exhausted see.\"","para":"The verse names the four means of Self-attainment: (a) satya (truth — meaning both speaking truth and being-true to the Self); (b) tapas (concentrated discipline); (c) samyag-jñāna (right knowledge — discriminative cognition); (d) nityaṃ brahma-carya (continuous brahmacarya — both celibacy and Veda-discipline). All four together, not any one alone. The Self so attained is jyotir-maya (light-made), śubhra (pure), present antaḥ-śarīre (within the body). Those who see him are yatayaḥ (strivers, ascetics) kṣīṇa-doṣāḥ (with their faults exhausted).","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> The four means — satya, tapas, samyag-jñāna, brahma-carya — are the canonical sādhanā-list of the Muṇḍaka. Compared with the Yoga-Sūtra's eight aṅgas (yama, niyama, āsana, prāṇāyāma, pratyāhāra, dhāraṇā, dhyāna, samādhi), the Muṇḍaka's four-fold list is older and more concentrated: it includes ethical (satya), disciplinary (tapas), cognitive (samyag-jñāna), and continent (brahmacarya) dimensions in a single list. The qualifier nityam — \"continuously\" — applies to all four: not occasional truth, not occasional tapas, but continuous practice. The Self is then seen within the body (antaḥ-śarīre) — not in some external place. The verse's vision is therefore inner and the means is constancy."],["Sivananda","This is the canonical Muṇḍaka sādhanā-mantra. Sivananda recommends memorising it and reciting it daily as a self-instruction: by truth, by tapas, by right knowledge, by continuous brahmacarya — these four together. None of them works alone; all four are needed. The yatayaḥ kṣīṇa-doṣāḥ — \"strivers whose faults are exhausted\" — describes the qualified seer: faults are exhausted before the seeing, not after. Reduce faults; then the seeing comes."],["Krishnananda","brahma-carya here cannot be reduced to celibacy in the modern sense. The compound brahma + carya = \"moving / acting in Brahman.\" Celibacy is the outward sign; the inner reality is the constant maintenance of consciousness in the Brahman-direction. nityam — \"continuously\" — applies to brahmacarya most precisely: not just during student-years (as in the gṛhastha tradition) but constantly, in every state of life."],["Deussen","The four-fold sādhanā-list (satya, tapas, samyag-jñāna, brahmacarya) is the Muṇḍaka's distinct contribution. It does not appear in this exact form in earlier Upaniṣads; later (BG 10.4–5, ŚU 6.21) related lists appear. The Muṇḍaka's combination of moral, ascetic, cognitive, and continent dimensions is uniquely four-fold."],["Aurobindo","satya — \"truth\" — is the verse's first and most-emphasised means. Truth is not just truth-speaking; it is truth-being: living one's life in conformity with what one knows to be the case. The realised one's life is consonant with his cognition; the unrealised one's life is at odds with it. The first sādhanā is therefore the alignment of life with seeing. Without satya, tapas becomes self-mortification, samyag-jñāna becomes intellectualism, brahmacarya becomes mere abstinence."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 3.1.6","title":"Truth alone triumphs, not untruth; by truth is the deva-yāna path laid out","sa":["सत्यमेव जयते नानृतं सत्येन पन्था विततो देवयानः ।","येनाक्रमन्त्यृषयो ह्याप्तकामा यत्र तत्सत्यस्य परमं निधानम् ॥ ६॥"],"iast":["satyam eva jayate nānṛtaṃ satyena panthā vitato deva-yānaḥ |","yenākramanty ṛṣayo hy āpta-kāmā yatra tat satyasya paramaṃ nidhānam || 6 ||"],"words":[["satyam eva","n. nom. sg.","truth alone"],["jayate","3 sg. pres. mid. of ji","triumphs"],["na anṛtam","n. nom. sg.","not untruth"],["satyena","n. instr. sg.","by truth"],["panthāḥ","m. nom. sg.","(the) path"],["vitataḥ","m. nom. sg. pp.","is extended / laid out"],["deva-yānaḥ","m. nom. sg. tp.","(the) path of the gods"],["yena","m. instr. sg. rel.","by which"],["ākramanti","3 pl. pres. of ā-kram","ascend / step up"],["ṛṣayaḥ","m. nom. pl.","the seers"],["hi","indecl.","indeed"],["āpta-kāmāḥ","m. nom. pl. bv.","(whose) desires are obtained"],["yatra","indecl.","where"],["tat","n. nom. sg.","that"],["satyasya","n. gen. sg.","of truth"],["paramam","n. nom. sg. adj.","supreme"],["nidhānam","n. nom. sg.","(is) the storehouse"]],"lit":"\"Truth alone triumphs, not untruth; by truth the path of the gods is laid out, by which the seers, their desires fulfilled, ascend — where (is) that supreme storehouse of truth.\"","para":"The most-quoted half-mantra in modern India. satyam eva jayate (\"truth alone triumphs\") is the motto of the Republic of India, inscribed on the national emblem (under the four-lion capital of Aśoka's Sārnāth pillar). The full verse is more comprehensive than the half-quotation suggests: satya alone wins, not falsehood; by satya the deva-yāna (the path of the gods, the bright-half post-mortem route of Chāndogya 5.10) is laid out (vitata); by it the ṛṣis whose desires are fulfilled (āpta-kāmāḥ — \"of obtained desire\") ascend; the destination is the supreme storehouse (paramaṃ nidhānam) of satya itself. The verse is the Upaniṣad's most precise statement of the cosmic role of truth: it is not just a moral injunction but the very pathway-substance of the deva-yāna.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> The most-cited half-mantra in modern India. Śaṅkara's reading: satya is here both the moral quality (truthfulness) and the cosmic reality (the True / Brahman). The half-verse satyam eva jayate nānṛtam therefore has two layers: (a) the moral — truth-speaking always eventually wins out over lying; (b) the cosmic — the True (Brahman) alone is real; the un-true (cosmic untruth = māyā / non-Brahman) is unreal. The two readings are not alternatives; they are the same statement at two levels. The cosmic deva-yāna path (the same one of Muṇḍaka 1.2.5) is here said to be laid out by truth — meaning that only the truth-aligned can travel it. āpta-kāmāḥ — \"of fulfilled desire\" — is the ṛṣi's qualification: he is one whose desires are fulfilled before the ascent, not in expectation of being fulfilled by it. The destination is satyasya paramaṃ nidhānam — \"the supreme storehouse of truth\" — the Brahman as the consummation of all that is true."],["Sivananda","The mantra is the moral foundation of the entire Indian dharma-tradition. satyam eva jayate is recited at every formal occasion in modern India. Sivananda's gloss insists on both the moral and the cosmic readings: speak the truth, live the truth — and through that very living the cosmic Truth manifests in your life as the path-of-the-gods."],["Krishnananda","āpta-kāmāḥ — \"of fulfilled desire\" — is precise. The seers who ascend the deva-yāna are not in pursuit of further fulfilment; they are already āpta-kāma, having no remaining desire. The desire-fulfilment is therefore not the achievement of the path; it is the precondition. Krishnananda treats this as the verse's psychological observation: the path of truth is not for the still-desiring but for those who have already exhausted desire."],["Deussen","The half-mantra satyam eva jayate has become the motto of the Indian republic, and Deussen presciently treats the full verse as the textual basis for what he calls \"the Vedic ethics of truth.\" The cosmic statute (only the True triumphs) and the moral statute (speak the truth) are inseparable in the Vedic outlook."],["Aurobindo","satya is for Aurobindo the central concept of the Veda. He distinguishes ṛta (cosmic order) from satya (the Being-that-is): ṛta is the right; satya is the real. The path of the gods is laid out by both — but the deeper layer is satya. The mantra's deepest teaching: the cosmos is held together by the True; falsehood is metaphysically secondary, has no enduring substance, and cannot win. The ethical statement (truth wins) is grounded in the metaphysical statement (only the True is)."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 3.1.7","title":"Vast, divine, of inconceivable form, finer than the fine, brighter than the bright, farther than the far, near here in the cavity for those who see","sa":["बृहच्च तद्दिव्यमचिन्त्यरूपं सूक्ष्माच्च तत्सूक्ष्मतरं विभाति ।","दूरात्सुदूरे तदिहान्तिके च पश्यत्स्विहैव निहितं गुहायाम् ॥ ७॥"],"iast":["bṛhac ca tad divyam acintya-rūpaṃ sūkṣmāc ca tat sūkṣma-taraṃ vibhāti |","dūrāt sudūre tad ihāntike ca paśyatsv ihaiva nihitaṃ guhāyām || 7 ||"],"words":[["bṛhat","n. nom. sg. adj.","vast"],["ca","indecl.","and"],["tat","n. nom. sg.","that"],["divyam","n. nom. sg.","divine"],["acintya-rūpam","n. nom. sg. bv.","of-inconceivable-form"],["sūkṣmāt","n. abl. sg. comp.","than the fine"],["ca","indecl.","and"],["tat","n. nom. sg.","that"],["sūkṣma-taram","n. nom. sg. comp.","finer"],["vibhāti","3 sg. pres. of vi-bhā","shines forth"],["dūrāt","indecl.","than the far"],["sudūre","n. loc. sg.","in the very-far"],["tat","n. nom. sg.","that"],["iha","indecl.","here"],["antike","n. loc. sg.","in the near"],["ca","indecl.","and"],["paśyatsu","m. loc. pl. pres. part. of paś","in / for those who see"],["iha eva","indecl.","right here"],["nihitam","n. nom. sg. pp.","deposited"],["guhāyām","f. loc. sg.","in the cavity"]],"lit":"\"Vast and divine, of inconceivable form — that shines forth finer than the fine; far in the very-far, and (yet) near right here, deposited in the cavity for those who see.\"","para":"The Brahman's paradox of nearness and distance. It is bṛhat (vast), divya (divine), acintya-rūpa (of inconceivable form), sūkṣmāt sūkṣma-tara (finer than the fine — the comparative form of KaU 1.2.20's aṇor aṇīyān). It is dūrāt sudūre (farther than the far — for the unseeing) and iha-antike (near here — for the seeing). For those who see (paśyatsu), it is iha-eva nihitaṃ guhāyām — \"right here, deposited in the cavity.\" The verse therefore inverts the usual sense of distance: what is most far in the spatial sense is most near in the cognitive sense, for the qualified seer.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> The verse precisely parallels KaU 1.2.20 (\"aṇor aṇīyān mahato mahīyān ātmāsya jantor nihito guhāyām\" — \"finer than the fine, vaster than the vast — the Self set in the cavity of this creature\"). The Muṇḍaka adds the dūrāt sudūre / iha-antike paradox: Brahman is in the extreme distance (for the non-seer) and in immediate nearness (for the seer). This is the precise epistemic point: distance and nearness are not properties of Brahman; they are properties of the cognising consciousness's relation to Brahman. For those who see, what is most far becomes nearest; for those who do not see, what is most near appears farthest."],["Sivananda","The verse is the canonical reminder for the seeker: do not seek Brahman in the distance. Seek where you have not looked — right here, in the cavity. Sivananda's daily practice: each morning, before any external task, sit briefly and recall iha-eva — \"right here\" — and antike — \"near.\" The seeker who treats Brahman as far has not yet understood the Upaniṣad."],["Krishnananda","The verse's strength is the doubled-paradox: vast-yet-fine, far-yet-near. Both pairs collapse the spatial-quantitative framework on which ordinary cognition relies. Brahman is not somewhere on a scale; it is the substrate of the scale. The verse therefore is not just paradoxical; it is anti-spatial."],["Deussen","The dūrāt sudūre / antike phrase is reminiscent of Īśā 5 (tad dūre tad v antike — \"that is far, and that is near\"). The Upaniṣads use the formula consistently. Deussen treats this as the Upaniṣadic anticipation of Christian / Sufi later formulations of God-as-immanent-and-transcendent."],["Aurobindo","The verse's deepest teaching is in paśyatsu — \"for those who see.\" Brahman's nearness is conditional on the seeing. The same Brahman is far for the non-seeing and near for the seeing — and the difference between far and near is not Brahman's but the seeker's. Aurobindo: \"Brahman does not approach the seer; the seer approaches Brahman by seeing.\""]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 3.1.8","title":"Not by eye, not by speech, not by other senses or tapas or karma; by the grace of knowledge, in purified essence — by meditation, the partless is seen","sa":["न चक्षुषा गृह्यते नापि वाचा नान्यैर्देवैस्तपसा कर्मणा वा ।","ज्ञानप्रसादेन विशुद्धसत्त्व-स्ततस्तु तं पश्यते निष्कलं ध्यायमानः ॥ ८॥"],"iast":["na cakṣuṣā gṛhyate nāpi vācā nānyair devais tapasā karmaṇā vā |","jñāna-prasādena viśuddha-sattvas tatas tu taṃ paśyate niṣkalaṃ dhyāyamānaḥ || 8 ||"],"words":[["na","indecl.","not"],["cakṣuṣā","n. instr. sg.","by the eye"],["gṛhyate","3 sg. pres. pass. of grah","is grasped"],["na api vācā","f. instr. sg.","nor by speech"],["na anyaiḥ devaiḥ","m. instr. pl.","nor by the other (sense-)gods"],["tapasā","n. instr. sg.","by tapas"],["karmaṇā","n. instr. sg.","by action / ritual"],["vā","indecl.","or"],["jñāna-prasādena","m. instr. sg. tp.","by the grace of knowledge"],["viśuddha-sattvaḥ","m. nom. sg. bv.","(he whose) essence is purified"],["tataḥ tu","indecl.","from that (state) indeed"],["tam","m. acc. sg.","him"],["paśyate","3 sg. pres. mid. of paś","sees"],["niṣkalam","m. acc. sg. adj.","(the) partless one"],["dhyāyamānaḥ","m. nom. sg. pres. part. mid. of dhyai","meditating"]],"lit":"\"Not by the eye is (He) grasped, nor by speech, nor by other (sense-)gods, nor by tapas, nor by ritual. By the grace of knowledge — (he whose) essence is purified — from that (state), meditating, sees the partless One.\"","para":"The verse rules out five conventional means and prescribes the actual one. Brahman is not grasped (a) by the eye (sense-perception), (b) by speech (verbal expression / discussion), (c) by other devas (the other sense-organs — eye, ear, etc., are \"sense-gods\"), (d) by tapas (ascetic discipline alone), (e) by karma (ritual action alone). The way: jñāna-prasāda — \"the grace of knowledge.\" The seer must be viśuddha-sattva — of purified essence (his sattva-substance must be clear of rajas-and-tamas admixture). Then he sees the partless (niṣkala) — by meditation (dhyāyamānaḥ). The verse is the Upaniṣad's most explicit refutation of all alternative paths.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> The verse's five negations are exhaustive: every standard means of cognition is ruled out. Eye (the sense-organs collectively), speech (the discursive faculty), other devas (the other sense-organs in their divine personifications), tapas (austerity), karma (ritual action). All five fail. The means that succeeds: jñāna-prasāda — \"the grace of knowledge.\" prasāda is the technical term for the spontaneous clarity that arises in a purified mind, not by effort but by grace (the Lord's, or the teacher's, or the mind's own clarity-when-purified). The pre-condition: viśuddha-sattva — purification of the sattva-substance of the mind, meaning the removal of rajas and tamas admixture. In that state, dhyāna can act — \"meditating, he sees.\" The verse is therefore the Upaniṣad's most precise specification of the means: not effort, not technique, but the spontaneous prasāda of a purified mind in meditation."],["Sivananda","jñāna-prasāda is the Upaniṣad's most under-translated phrase. \"Grace of knowledge\" loses the technical sense: prasāda is the clarity-of-mind that arises when the mind has settled — like the clarity of water after silt has settled. The mind in meditation, having settled, becomes clear; that clarity is jñāna-prasāda; in that clarity, the seeing happens. Sivananda's gloss: \"do not chase the seeing. Settle the mind. The seeing then arises by itself.\""],["Krishnananda","viśuddha-sattva is the key precondition. sattva, in the technical Sāṅkhya-Vedānta sense, is the guṇa of clarity; viśuddha-sattva = sattva purified of admixture with rajas (activity) and tamas (inertia). When the mind has reached this state, jñāna-prasāda follows automatically. Krishnananda emphasises that no amount of dhyāna succeeds in an un-purified-sattva mind, and that no purification of sattva fails to be followed by dhyāna-success. The two are causally linked."],["Deussen","jñāna-prasāda is one of the most important phrases in the Upaniṣad. It anticipates the BG's prasāda concept (BG 2.65 — prasāde sarva-duḥkhānāṃ hānir asyopajāyate — \"in clarity all his sufferings are destroyed\") and is the technical term for the spontaneous mental clarity that follows meditation-by-grace."],["Aurobindo","jñāna-prasāda is for Aurobindo the most profound description of how realisation occurs. It is not an act of the will (cakṣuṣā, vācā, etc.) but a grace (prasāda) given to the purified consciousness. The seeker prepares (viśuddha-sattva); the seeing is given. The active and the passive moments of realisation are integrated: effort produces the purification, grace produces the seeing. Aurobindo's gloss: \"the seer does not see by force of his own seeing; he sees because the seen has illumined itself in his cleared mind.\""]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 3.1.9","title":"The minute Self is to be known by the mind, in which prāṇa five-fold enters; in him, mind together with prāṇas, the Self shines","sa":["एषोऽणुरात्मा चेतसा वेदितव्यो यस्मिन्प्राणः पञ्चधा संविवेश ।","प्राणैश्चित्तं सर्वमोतं प्रजानां यस्मिन्विशुद्धे विभवत्येष आत्मा ॥ ९॥"],"iast":["eṣo'ṇur ātmā cetasā veditavyo yasmin prāṇaḥ pañcadhā saṃviveśa |","prāṇaiś cittaṃ sarvam otaṃ prajānāṃ yasmin viśuddhe vibhavaty eṣa ātmā || 9 ||"],"words":[["eṣaḥ","m. nom. sg.","this"],["aṇuḥ","m. nom. sg. adj.","minute"],["ātmā","m. nom. sg.","Self"],["cetasā","n. instr. sg.","by the mind"],["veditavyaḥ","m. nom. sg. gerundive","is to be known"],["yasmin","m. loc. sg. rel.","in which"],["prāṇaḥ","m. nom. sg.","(the) prāṇa"],["pañcadhā","indecl.","fivefold"],["saṃviveśa","3 sg. pf. of sam-viś","has entered"],["prāṇaiḥ","m. instr. pl.","by/with the prāṇas"],["cittam","n. nom. sg.","the mind"],["sarvam","n. nom. sg.","all"],["otam","n. nom. sg. pp.","woven"],["prajānām","f. gen. pl.","of (all) creatures"],["yasmin viśuddhe","n. loc. sg. + pp.","when (it is) purified"],["vibhavati","3 sg. pres. of vi-bhū","becomes manifest"],["eṣaḥ ātmā","m. nom. sg.","this Self"]],"lit":"\"This minute Self is to be known by the mind — (the Self) in which prāṇa has entered fivefold. The mind of all creatures is woven (together) with the prāṇas; when (the mind is) purified, this Self becomes manifest.\"","para":"The Self is aṇu (minute) and is to be known cetasā — by the citta (mind). Two characterisations of the Self: (a) the Self in which prāṇa has entered fivefold (the pañca-prāṇa — prāṇa, apāna, vyāna, udāna, samāna — has its source in the Self); (b) the Self with which the citta of all creatures is otam (woven) by the prāṇas. The conclusion: yasmin viśuddhe vibhavaty eṣa ātmā — when the (mind) is purified, this Self becomes manifest. The means is again purification of the mind.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> The verse repeats and extends 3.1.8: the means of knowing is the mind (citta) — but only the purified mind. The unpurified mind sees the prāṇas and the cittam-prāṇa-fabric and stops there; the purified mind sees through this fabric to the Self in which both prāṇa and citta are woven. The image of cittam-prāṇa-otam (the mind-and-prāṇas woven together) is the Muṇḍaka's precise account of psychophysical structure: mind is woven through prāṇa, prāṇa through mind; both are woven into the Self. The verse therefore confirms what 3.1.8 stated: the medium of knowing is the mind, but only when purified."],["Sivananda","The five-fold entry of prāṇa is the standard pañca-prāṇa scheme (prāṇa — chest; apāna — pelvis; samāna — navel; udāna — throat; vyāna — whole body). All five are entries of the one Self-prāṇa. Sivananda's prāṇāyāma instruction: meditate on each of the five in turn; recognise that all five issue from the one Self. The pañca-prāṇa exercise is the canonical preparation for the realisation this verse describes."],["Krishnananda","yasmin viśuddhe — \"when (the mind is) purified\" — repeats the precondition from 3.1.8 (viśuddha-sattva). The Muṇḍaka relentlessly returns to this precondition: realisation depends on purification of the mind. Neither tapas nor karma nor verbal study replaces it."],["Deussen","The pañca-prāṇa-vidyā is here in compressed form. The full doctrine is elaborated in Praśna 3 (the genesis, division, and exit of prāṇa). The Muṇḍaka mentions only its conclusion: the fivefold prāṇa enters the Self. Deussen treats the verse as the Muṇḍaka's acknowledgement that the full pañca-prāṇa doctrine is elsewhere."],["Aurobindo","The verse's deepest teaching is that the mind itself is the means of seeing. The Upaniṣad does not require the abolition of mind; it requires the purification of mind. cittam viśuddham — purified mind — is the very organ by which the Self is seen. Aurobindo treats this as one of his most important Upaniṣadic texts: the spiritual life is not the destruction of the instruments (mind, prāṇa) but their purification into transparency."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 3.1.10","title":"The worlds and desires he envisions — he wins; the seeker-of-prosperity should worship the Self-knower","sa":["यं यं लोकं मनसा संविभाति विशुद्धसत्त्वः कामयते यांश्च कामान् ।","तं तं लोकं जयते तांश्च कामां-स्तस्मादात्मज्ञं ह्यर्चयेद्भूतिकामः ॥ १०॥"],"iast":["yaṃ yaṃ lokaṃ manasā saṃvibhāti viśuddha-sattvaḥ kāmayate yāṃś ca kāmān |","taṃ taṃ lokaṃ jayate tāṃś ca kāmāṃs tasmād ātma-jñaṃ hy arcayed bhūti-kāmaḥ || 10 ||"],"words":[["yam yam","m. acc. sg. (distributive)","whichever"],["lokam","m. acc. sg.","world"],["manasā","n. instr. sg.","by the mind"],["saṃvibhāti","3 sg. pres. of sam-vi-bhā","he envisions / brings to light together"],["viśuddha-sattvaḥ","m. nom. sg. bv.","one whose essence is purified"],["kāmayate","3 sg. pres. mid. of kam","desires"],["yān ca kāmān","m. acc. pl.","and whatever desires"],["tam tam lokam","m. acc. sg. (distributive)","that very world"],["jayate","3 sg. pres. mid. of ji","wins"],["tān ca kāmān","m. acc. pl.","and those desires"],["tasmāt","indecl.","therefore"],["ātma-jñam","m. acc. sg. tp.","the Self-knower"],["hi","indecl.","indeed"],["arcayet","3 sg. pot. of arc","should worship"],["bhūti-kāmaḥ","m. nom. sg. bv.","one who desires prosperity"]],"lit":"\"Whichever world the one of purified essence envisions in his mind, and whatever desires he desires — that very world he wins, and those very desires. Therefore the one who desires prosperity should worship the Self-knower.\"","para":"The verse closes khaṇḍa 3.1 with a doctrinal corollary aimed at the world-engaged seeker. The viśuddha-sattva (the one whose essence is purified, of 3.1.8–9) has acquired the power to bring about whatever world he envisions (yaṃ yaṃ lokam manasā saṃvibhāti) and whatever desires he desires (yān ca kāmān). The implication: even worldly success follows the realised one. Therefore (tasmāt) the one who himself desires prosperity (bhūti-kāmaḥ) should worship the Self-knower (ātma-jñam arcayet). The verse provides a practical motivation for the lay devotee: associate with and serve the realised one, and worldly success follows by association.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> The verse is the Muṇḍaka's only explicit appeal to the bhūti-kāmin — the prosperity-seeker. The doctrinal stake: the realised one has gained command over the world such that whatever he envisions or desires comes about. This is the Upaniṣad's most precise statement of siddhi-acquisition: not as the goal of yoga, not as a side-pursuit, but as a natural consequence of viśuddha-sattvatā. Therefore the Upaniṣad recommends: even the worldly should reverence the realised one, since proximity to such a one yields prosperity by association. Śaṅkara treats this verse as a concession to the un-renouncant audience: the Upaniṣad does not require renunciation as a precondition for benefiting from brahmavidyā; even the gṛhastha seeking prosperity can do so by worshipping the realised one."],["Sivananda","The verse is the Muṇḍaka's most practical teaching for the layperson. Even if you have no time for sādhanā, even if you cannot withdraw to the forest, even if you remain bhūti-kāmaḥ (a seeker of prosperity) — at least worship the Self-knower. Service to a jñānī is itself spiritual practice and produces worldly prosperity as side-effect. Sivananda's lifelong message at Rishikesh: serve the saints; prosperity, peace, and eventually realisation will follow."],["Krishnananda","The verse describes the siddhi-state precisely. The viśuddha-sattva mind acts on the world: what it envisions, comes about; what it desires, is given. This is not magic but the natural causality of a purified mind. The realised one's thoughts are aligned with the cosmic substance; therefore they manifest. Krishnananda treats this as the Upaniṣadic background for the later yoga-sūtra doctrine of siddhis (YS 3.16–55)."],["Deussen","bhūti-kāmaḥ is the Vedic householder's standard motivation — desire for worldly success. The Upaniṣad does not condemn this motivation; it gives it a means. Worship the realised one, and your worldly desires will be fulfilled. Deussen treats this verse as evidence of the Upaniṣad's pragmatic accommodation of its lay audience."],["Aurobindo","The verse's deepest teaching is that the realised consciousness has command over manifestation. The viśuddha-sattva mind is not a passive recipient of cosmic events; it is a centre of cosmic projection. Whatever it envisions (saṃvibhāti — \"shines forth together [with cosmic reality]\") manifests. This is for Aurobindo the textual basis for his doctrine of supramentalised will: the supramentally transformed consciousness projects what it sees, and what is projected is manifested."]],"notes":["End of khaṇḍa 3.1."]}]},{"t":"Muṇḍaka 3, Khaṇḍa 2 — The Self not gained by reasoning; rivers into ocean; the akṣara-bound seers (11 mantras)","u":[{"ref":"Muṇḍaka 3, Khaṇḍa 2","title":"The Self not gained by reasoning; rivers into ocean; the akṣara-bound seers (11 mantras)","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["The final khaṇḍa addresses the qualifications and the destination of the realised one. The Self cannot be reached by reasoning, by mere learning, or by mere physical austerity; only by him whom the Self itself chooses. The seer who has reached, abandoning name-and-form, becomes the Puruṣa as rivers become the ocean. The final verses set the conditions under which this teaching may be transmitted — only to those who have performed the śiro-vrata, the head-vow."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Mantra 3.2.1","title":"He knows that supreme Brahman-abode where the universe shines forth radiant; those passion-less ones worship the Puruṣa and pass beyond the seed","sa":["स वेदैतत्परमं ब्रह्म धाम यत्र विश्वं निहितं भाति शुभ्रम् ।","उपासते पुरुषं ये ह्यकामास्ते शुक्रमेतदतिवर्तन्ति धीराः ॥ १॥"],"iast":["sa vedaitat paramaṃ brahma dhāma yatra viśvaṃ nihitaṃ bhāti śubhram |","upāsate puruṣaṃ ye hy akāmās te śukram etad ati-vartanti dhīrāḥ || 1 ||"],"words":[["saḥ","m. nom. sg.","he"],["veda","3 sg. pf. of vid (pres. sense)","knows"],["etat","n. acc. sg.","this"],["paramam","n. acc. sg. adj.","supreme"],["brahma","n. acc. sg.","Brahman"],["dhāma","n. acc. sg.","abode"],["yatra","indecl.","where"],["viśvam","n. nom. sg.","the universe"],["nihitam","n. nom. sg. pp.","is deposited"],["bhāti","3 sg. pres. of bhā","shines"],["śubhram","n. nom. sg.","radiant"],["upāsate","3 pl. pres. mid. of upa-ās","worship / meditate on"],["puruṣam","m. acc. sg.","the Puruṣa"],["ye","m. nom. pl. rel.","those who"],["hi","indecl.","indeed"],["a-kāmāḥ","m. nom. pl. bv.","desireless"],["te","m. nom. pl.","they"],["śukram","n. acc. sg.","the bright seed (= the source-substance of beings)"],["etat","n. acc. sg.","this"],["ati-vartanti","3 pl. pres. of ati-vṛt","pass beyond"],["dhīrāḥ","m. nom. pl.","the wise"]],"lit":"\"He knows that supreme Brahman-abode where the universe is deposited and shines forth radiant. Those wise (ones), desireless, who worship the Puruṣa — they pass beyond this bright (seed-)source.\"","para":"Khaṇḍa 3.2 opens with the destination of the realised. He knows the paramaṃ brahma-dhāma — \"the supreme Brahman-abode\" — yatra viśvaṃ nihitaṃ bhāti śubhram (\"where the universe is deposited and shines forth radiant\"). Those who worship the Puruṣa, being a-kāma (desireless), are dhīrāḥ (wise) — they pass beyond (ati-vartanti) the śukra — the \"bright seed-source\" that is the substance of beings — i.e., they transcend the cosmogonic process itself and reach what is beyond the cycle of emanation.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> The verse opens the final teaching with the destination: paramaṃ brahma-dhāma — the supreme abode that is identical with Brahman itself, not a separate place. The universe is deposited there and shines forth from there. The śukra (\"bright seed\") is the source-substance from which beings emanate (cf. the cosmogony of 2.1.1); the Puruṣa-worshippers ati-vartanti — pass beyond — even this source. The verse therefore takes the seeker past the cosmogonic level (the akṣara as source of beings) to the para-Brahman beyond the source-status."],["Sivananda","a-kāma — \"desireless\" — is the precondition. Only the desireless can worship the Puruṣa in the sense the verse means. Worship-with-desire is still karma; worship-without-desire is jñāna in worship-form. Sivananda's daily-check: when you sit in worship, examine your motives; if there is desire, the worship is not what this verse describes."],["Krishnananda","śukram — \"bright seed-source\" — is technical: it is the source-substance from which beings emanate, the same as the akṣara of 1.1.5 considered as cause of beings. The Puruṣa-worshippers pass beyond even this — beyond cosmogony itself. Krishnananda treats this as the verse's most precise pre-formulation of the para-Brahman / apara-Brahman distinction."],["Deussen","brahma-dhāma — \"the Brahman-abode\" — is a compound that admits two readings: (a) the abode that Brahman has (i.e., a place in which Brahman dwells); (b) the abode that is Brahman (i.e., Brahman itself as the abode). Śaṅkara prefers (b); other commentators waver. Deussen's translation prefers (b) on doctrinal grounds."],["Aurobindo","yatra viśvaṃ nihitaṃ bhāti śubhram — \"where the universe is deposited and shines forth radiant\" — is the verse's deepest teaching about the destination. The destination is not somewhere where the universe is excluded; it is somewhere where the universe is included and radiant. Realisation does not abolish the cosmos; it sees the cosmos as cosmic-radiance-of-Brahman. The Puruṣa-worshippers do not leave the universe; they pass beyond the cosmogonic source while seeing the cosmos as radiant."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 3.2.2","title":"The desire-thinker is born wherever his desires; for the desire-fulfilled, all desires dissolve here","sa":["कामान् यः कामयते मन्यमानः स कामभिर्जायते तत्र तत्र ।","पर्याप्तकामस्य कृतात्मनस्तु इहैव सर्वे प्रविलीयन्ति कामाः ॥ २॥"],"iast":["kāmān yaḥ kāmayate manyamānaḥ sa kāmabhir jāyate tatra tatra |","paryāpta-kāmasya kṛtātmanas tu ihaiva sarve pravilīyanti kāmāḥ || 2 ||"],"words":[["kāmān","m. acc. pl.","desires"],["yaḥ","m. nom. sg. rel.","he who"],["kāmayate","3 sg. pres. mid. of kam","desires"],["manyamānaḥ","m. nom. sg. pres. part. mid. of man","thinking, dwelling-in-thought"],["saḥ","m. nom. sg.","he"],["kāmabhiḥ","m. instr. pl.","with / on account of (his) desires"],["jāyate","3 sg. pres. of jan","is born"],["tatra tatra","indecl. (distributive)","in this and that (situation/birth)"],["paryāpta-kāmasya","m. gen. sg. bv.","of one whose desires are accomplished/satiated"],["kṛta-ātmanaḥ","m. gen. sg. bv.","of one whose Self is made (= realised)"],["tu","indecl.","but"],["iha eva","indecl.","right here"],["sarve","m. nom. pl.","all"],["pravilīyanti","3 pl. pres. mid. of pra-vi-lī","dissolve"],["kāmāḥ","m. nom. pl.","(his) desires"]],"lit":"\"He who, dwelling-in-thought, desires desires — he is born along with (those) desires in this and that (situation). But for one whose desires are satiated, whose Self is realised, all desires dissolve right here.\"","para":"The verse contrasts the karmic and the realised conditions. The karmic seeker (yaḥ kāmān kāmayate manyamānaḥ — \"he who, dwelling-in-thought, desires desires\") is born along with his desires tatra tatra (\"in this and that situation\") — i.e., reborn in whatever circumstance his unfulfilled desires require. The realised one (paryāpta-kāma — \"of satisfied desires\"; kṛtātmā — \"whose Self is realised\") finds that all desires dissolve here (ihaiva) — in this very life, before death. The verse is the Muṇḍaka's clearest statement that the realised one no longer accumulates birth-producing desire-residues.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> The compound paryāpta-kāma admits two readings: (a) \"of completed desires\" (his desires have all been fulfilled); (b) \"of saturated desire\" (his desire-faculty is satiated). Śaṅkara prefers (b) — it is not that the realised one has accumulated everything he wanted, but that his very desire-faculty has reached a saturation-point where no further desires arise. The compound kṛtātmā is similarly technical: literally \"made-Self,\" meaning \"one whose Self is accomplished\" — i.e., realised. The two compounds together describe the jīvanmukta: realised, and with no remaining desire-residue to produce future births. ihaiva — \"right here\" — emphasises the in-this-life dissolution: not after death, not in some future state, but here in the present life."],["Sivananda","manyamānaḥ — \"dwelling-in-thought\" — is the verse's psychological precision. The desire is not generated by external objects but by the mind's dwelling on them. To dissolve desire is to stop the dwelling. The kṛtātmā does not first satisfy and then transcend his desires; he allows them to dissolve by withdrawing the dwelling. Sivananda's practice: when a desire arises, do not pursue it and do not suppress it; just stop dwelling on it; it dissolves."],["Krishnananda","The verse's most precise teaching is that desires produce births. The mind dwelling on objects creates desire; desire produces the birth-situation. To stop the dwelling is to stop the births. The realised one's freedom from rebirth is therefore not granted by an external agent; it is the natural consequence of the dissolution of desire-dwelling."],["Deussen","Compare BĀU 4.4.6 (yad kāmo bhavati tat kratur bhavati, yat kratur bhavati tat karma kurute, yat karma kurute tat-abhi-sampadyate — \"as is his desire, so is his will; as is his will, so is the act he does; as is the act, so does he attain\"). The Muṇḍaka's version is more compressed but doctrinally identical."],["Aurobindo","paryāpta-kāma — \"saturated desire\" — captures the deepest psychological truth. Desire is not abolished by force; it is allowed to reach its own saturation. The realised one is not the one who has suppressed desires; he is the one whose desire-faculty has spontaneously saturated and dissolved. Aurobindo's gloss: \"do not fight the desires; let them ripen and they will of themselves fall away.\""]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 3.2.3","title":"The Self is not gained by reasoning, nor by intellect, nor by much hearing; only by him whom the Self chooses — to him this Self reveals its own form","sa":["नायमात्मा प्रवचनेन लभ्यो न मेधया न बहुना श्रुतेन ।","यमेवैष वृणुते तेन लभ्य-स्तस्यैष आत्मा विवृणुते तनूं स्वाम् ॥ ३॥"],"iast":["nāyam ātmā pravacanena labhyo na medhayā na bahunā śrutena |","yam evaiṣa vṛṇute tena labhyas tasyaiṣa ātmā vivṛṇute tanūṃ svām || 3 ||"],"words":[["na","indecl.","not"],["ayam","m. nom. sg.","this"],["ātmā","m. nom. sg.","Self"],["pravacanena","n. instr. sg.","by exposition / reasoning"],["labhyaḥ","m. nom. sg. gerundive of labh","is to be attained"],["na medhayā","f. instr. sg.","nor by intellect"],["na bahunā śrutena","n. instr. sg.","nor by much hearing (= much study)"],["yam eva","m. acc. sg.","whom alone"],["eṣaḥ","m. nom. sg.","this (Self)"],["vṛṇute","3 sg. pres. mid. of vṛ","chooses"],["tena","m. instr. sg.","by him"],["labhyaḥ","m. nom. sg. gerundive","(it is) to be attained"],["tasya","m. gen. sg.","to him"],["eṣaḥ ātmā","m. nom. sg.","this Self"],["vivṛṇute","3 sg. pres. mid. of vi-vṛ","reveals / uncovers"],["tanūm","f. acc. sg.","(its own) form / body"],["svām","f. acc. sg.","own"]],"lit":"\"This Self is not to be attained by exposition, nor by intellect, nor by much hearing (= study). Whom alone this (Self) chooses, by him it is attained; to him this Self reveals its own form.\"","para":"The most-quoted verse of the Upaniṣad after satyam eva jayate. Word-for-word identical to Kaṭha 1.2.23 except for the closing tasyaiṣa ātmā vivṛṇute tanūṃ svām (which is also identical). The Self is not attained by (a) pravacana (exposition / reasoning — the verbal-philosophical means), (b) medhā (intellect — sharp natural cleverness), (c) bahu śruta (much hearing — extensive learning). What attains the Self: being chosen by the Self. yam eva eṣa vṛṇute tena labhyaḥ — \"by whom alone this (Self) chooses, by him is it attained.\" To that chosen one, the Self reveals its own form (tanūṃ svām).","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> The most theologically charged verse in the Upaniṣad. Three standard means of attainment are negated: (a) pravacana (reasoning/exposition — the karma-mīmāṃsā tradition), (b) medhā (natural intellect — the wisdom-tradition), (c) bahu-śruta (multiplied learning — the scholastic tradition). All three fail. What succeeds: being chosen by the Self. The doctrinal stake: realisation is not earned by effort but given by grace. Śaṅkara's reading: the Self chooses whomever has reached the proper preparation (purified sattva, samyak-jñāna, etc., from 3.1.5–9); the choice is not arbitrary but is the natural recognition of preparedness. The Self is not capricious; it chooses the prepared. But — and this is the verse's important qualification — the preparation does not itself produce the choosing; the choosing is the Self's act, not the prepared one's. The realised one therefore takes no credit; the credit is to the Self that chose him. Compare Kaṭha 1.2.23 — identical wording, identical doctrinal force. Either MuU borrows from KaU or both quote a now-lost source."],["Sivananda","This is the verse that reminds the student to stop trying. After all the sādhanā — after tapas, satya, brahmacarya, samyag-jñāna — there comes a point at which the seeker realises that no amount of further effort will produce realisation. At that point, he stops. And in the stopping, the Self reveals itself. The verse is therefore the canonical instruction in spiritual surrender: surrender is not abandoning sādhanā but completing it and then ceasing."],["Krishnananda","The verse's most precise teaching is that realisation requires both effort and grace, but not in equal measure. Effort prepares; grace gives. The seeker who only prepares (effort) never receives; the seeker who only waits (grace) is never ready when grace comes. Both must occur. The verse emphasises the second moment (the choosing) because the first moment (preparation) was emphasised in 3.1.5–9. The complete teaching is in the combination."],["Deussen","This is the canonical Upaniṣadic statement of grace-doctrine. The Self chooses; the seeker is chosen. The doctrine anticipates later Vaiṣṇava theology (Madhva, Rāmānuja) in which the Lord's grace is the sole effective cause of liberation. Deussen treats this verse and Kaṭha 1.2.23 as the textual ground of all later Indian grace-doctrines. The wording is identical in both Upaniṣads; one borrows from the other or both quote a common source."],["Aurobindo","The verse is for Aurobindo the most precise statement of the relation between sādhanā and grace. The seeker prepares by all the means (1.2.12's nirveda, 3.1.5's satya-tapas-jñāna-brahmacarya); the realisation is then given. The giving is not separate from the preparing; the same Self that the seeker has been preparing for is the Self that chooses him. yam eva eṣa vṛṇute — \"whom this (Self) chooses\" — the Self chooses the one who has chosen the Self. Aurobindo: \"the seeker who has fully sought finds; the seeking and the finding meet in the choosing.\""]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 3.2.4","title":"Not by the weakling, not by heedlessness, not by tapas without lakṣaṇa; for one who strives by these means — that knower's Self enters the Brahman-abode","sa":["नायमात्मा बलहीनेन लभ्यो न च प्रमादात्तपसो वाप्यलिङ्गात् ।","एतैरुपायैर्यतते यस्तु विद्वां-स्तस्यैष आत्मा विशते ब्रह्मधाम ॥ ४॥"],"iast":["nāyam ātmā bala-hīnena labhyo na ca pramādāt tapaso vāpy a-liṅgāt |","etair upāyair yatate yas tu vidvāṃs tasyaiṣa ātmā viśate brahma-dhāma || 4 ||"],"words":[["na","indecl.","not"],["ayam ātmā","m. nom. sg.","this Self"],["bala-hīnena","m. instr. sg. bv.","by the strength-deficient"],["labhyaḥ","m. nom. sg. gerundive","(is) to be attained"],["na ca","indecl.","nor"],["pramādāt","m. abl. sg.","from heedlessness"],["tapasaḥ","n. abl. sg.","from tapas"],["vā api","indecl.","or also"],["a-liṅgāt","m. abl. sg. bv.","(from tapas) without (the right) mark / sign / characteristic"],["etaiḥ upāyaiḥ","m. instr. pl.","by these (proper) means"],["yatate","3 sg. pres. mid. of yat","strives"],["yaḥ tu","m. nom. sg.","but who"],["vidvān","m. nom. sg.","(as) a knower"],["tasya","m. gen. sg.","his"],["eṣaḥ ātmā","m. nom. sg.","this Self"],["viśate","3 sg. pres. mid. of viś","enters"],["brahma-dhāma","n. acc. sg.","the Brahman-abode"]],"lit":"\"This Self is not to be attained by the strength-deficient, nor by heedlessness, nor by tapas without (the right) characteristic. But for the knower who strives by the proper means — his Self enters the Brahman-abode.\"","para":"The verse adds three more negated paths to the three of 3.2.3, and then re-affirms the positive prescription. The Self is not attained by: (a) bala-hīna — the strength-less (one without spiritual energy); (b) pramāda — heedlessness; (c) tapaso a-liṅgāt — tapas without the proper liṅga (sign / mark / characteristic — i.e., tapas not directed toward the Self, ungrounded tapas, mere physical austerity). The vidvān (the knower) who strives by the proper means (etair upāyaiḥ — referring back to 3.1.5's four-fold satya-tapas-jñāna-brahmacarya, with 3.1.8's jñāna-prasāda) — his Self enters the Brahman-abode.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> The three additional negations complete the picture. (a) bala-hīna — the spiritually-weak. The Upaniṣad explicitly says: spiritual strength is required. Realisation is not for the timid or the irresolute. (b) pramāda — heedlessness. The same disqualification as 2.2.4's a-pramatta requirement. (c) tapas without liṅga — tapas without the right characteristic. liṅga here is the technical mark of correctly-directed tapas: tapas aimed at Self-realisation, conducted with proper attitude. Mere physical austerity (fasting, exposure, sleep-deprivation) without the proper liṅga is fruitless. The verse's positive prescription: the vidvān (knower) who strives by the proper means — his Self enters the Brahman-abode. The agent is the Self, not the seeker: tasya eṣa ātmā viśate — \"his Self enters\" — the Self does the entering; the seeker provides the preparation."],["Sivananda","bala here is not physical strength but spiritual energy — the energy needed to sustain sādhanā through dry periods, through doubt, through external opposition. Without this energy, even the most refined practice fails. Sivananda's gloss: \"do not begin sādhanā until you have the strength to continue it. Premature practice without strength is the most common cause of spiritual failure.\""],["Krishnananda","a-liṅgāt tapasaḥ is the verse's most precise diagnosis of misdirected austerity. Many seekers practise tapas without the proper liṅga — without the inner attitude of Self-direction. Such tapas produces siddhis (KaU 1.2.23's medhā), prestige, even worldly power — but not realisation. Krishnananda treats this as the standard warning against tapas-without-jñāna."],["Deussen","bala-hīna and pramāda are standard Upaniṣadic disqualifications; tapaso a-liṅgāt is a more unusual specification. liṅga here means \"distinguishing mark\" — the mark that distinguishes Self-directed tapas from worldly tapas. The verse implicitly requires that tapas have a correct accompanying intention."],["Aurobindo","bala-hīnena is, for Aurobindo, the verse's most modern statement. The contemporary spiritual seeker often lacks bala — the inner energy required for sustained spiritual life. Without bala, no practice succeeds. The seeker must first build bala (through preparatory disciplines, through serving the realised one of 3.1.10, through grace) before launching into the higher practices. Aurobindo treats bala as one of the basic requirements his own sādhanā emphasises."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 3.2.5","title":"Having attained Him, the seers, knowledge-content, with realised Selves, passion-less, tranquil — they reach the All-pervading on every side","sa":["संप्राप्यैनमृषयो ज्ञानतृप्ताः कृतात्मानो वीतरागाः प्रशान्ताः ।","ते सर्वगं सर्वतः प्राप्य धीरा युक्तात्मानः सर्वमेवाविशन्ति ॥ ५॥"],"iast":["saṃprāpyainam ṛṣayo jñāna-tṛptāḥ kṛtātmāno vīta-rāgāḥ praśāntāḥ |","te sarva-gaṃ sarvataḥ prāpya dhīrā yuktātmānaḥ sarvam evāviśanti || 5 ||"],"words":[["saṃprāpya","ger. of sam-pra-āp","having fully attained"],["enam","m. acc. sg.","him"],["ṛṣayaḥ","m. nom. pl.","the seers"],["jñāna-tṛptāḥ","m. nom. pl. bv.","content with knowledge"],["kṛta-ātmānaḥ","m. nom. pl. bv.","(with) realised Self"],["vīta-rāgāḥ","m. nom. pl. bv.","free of passion"],["praśāntāḥ","m. nom. pl.","tranquil"],["te","m. nom. pl.","they"],["sarva-gam","m. acc. sg. tp.","the all-going / all-pervading"],["sarvataḥ","indecl.","on every side"],["prāpya","ger. of pra-āp","having reached"],["dhīrāḥ","m. nom. pl.","the wise"],["yukta-ātmānaḥ","m. nom. pl. bv.","(with) yoked Self"],["sarvam","n. acc. sg.","the All"],["eva","indecl.","indeed"],["āviśanti","3 pl. pres. of ā-viś","enter into"]],"lit":"\"Having fully attained Him — the seers, content with knowledge, realised, passion-less, tranquil, wise, with yoked Self — reach the All-pervading on every side and enter the All.\"","para":"The state of the realised seers. Six adjectives describe them: jñāna-tṛpta (content with knowledge — no further intellectual seeking), kṛtātmā (realised Self), vīta-rāga (passion-less), praśānta (tranquil), dhīra (wise), yuktātmā (with yoked / disciplined Self). Having attained the Self, they reach sarva-gam sarvataḥ (the all-pervading on every side) and āviśanti sarvam eva (enter into the All). The verse is the Muṇḍaka's most complete description of the jīvanmukta in his post-realisation state.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> The six-adjective characterisation is the canonical inventory of the realised seer. jñāna-tṛpta — \"content with knowledge\" — no further desire for further knowledge; the seeker has saturated. kṛtātmā — \"realised Self\" — the Self has done its work. vīta-rāga — \"passion-less\" — rāga (attachment), the root of saṃsāra, is dissolved. praśānta — \"tranquil\" — the mind is steady. dhīra — \"wise\" — the cognition is settled. yuktātmā — \"with yoked Self\" — the inner being is integrated, not scattered. The result: sarva-gam sarvataḥ prāpya — \"having reached the all-pervading on every side.\" The all-pervading is reached not in any one direction but in all directions simultaneously; the realised one is no longer localised in body-mind but identified with the cosmic Self. The closing sarvam eva āviśanti — \"they enter the All\" — completes the picture: the realised do not just reach the cosmic Self; they enter into it as substance."],["Sivananda","jñāna-tṛpta is the verse's most distinctive phrase. The realised one has had enough of knowing. He is no longer chasing further knowledge; the knowledge he has is sufficient. This is the standard mark of realisation: the cessation of the seeking-for-knowledge impulse. Sivananda's self-check: if the impulse to read further still drives the seeker, realisation has not happened."],["Krishnananda","sarva-gam sarvataḥ — \"the all-going on every side\" — captures the realised state precisely. The realised one is not at a place; he is everywhere simultaneously. He has not travelled to the Self; he has dissolved the centring-in-body that made travel necessary. Krishnananda treats this as the textual basis of his lifelong teaching that mokṣa is dimensionless, not directional."],["Deussen","yuktātmā — \"of yoked Self\" — uses the technical yoga-vocabulary that becomes canonical in BG (e.g., BG 6.10 — yuktātmā satatam). The Muṇḍaka is here on the boundary between the older Upaniṣadic vocabulary and the emerging Yoga-Sūtra vocabulary."],["Aurobindo","jñāna-tṛpta — \"content with knowledge\" — is for Aurobindo the most subtle mark. Most seekers, even after considerable progress, still feel the un-satisfied curiosity that drives further inquiry. The realised one has lost this. Not because he knows nothing further; but because the seeking-impulse has been satisfied at its root. jñāna-tṛpti is therefore a metaphysical state, not a state of intellectual sufficiency."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 3.2.6","title":"Vedānta-knowers, sure of the supreme aim, by saṃnyāsa-yoga purified — those passion-less ones at the time of dissolution all become released in Brahman","sa":["वेदान्तविज्ञानसुनिश्चितार्थाः संन्यासयोगाद्यतयः शुद्धसत्त्वाः ।","ते ब्रह्मलोकेषु परान्तकाले परामृताः परिमुच्यन्ति सर्वे ॥ ६॥"],"iast":["vedānta-vijñāna-suniścitārthāḥ saṃnyāsa-yogād yatayaḥ śuddha-sattvāḥ |","te brahma-lokeṣu parānta-kāle parāmṛtāḥ parimucyanti sarve || 6 ||"],"words":[["vedānta-vijñāna-suniścita-arthāḥ","m. nom. pl. bv.","those whose aim is well-determined by Vedānta-knowledge"],["saṃnyāsa-yogāt","m. abl. sg.","from the yoga of renunciation"],["yatayaḥ","m. nom. pl.","the strivers"],["śuddha-sattvāḥ","m. nom. pl. bv.","of pure sattva"],["te","m. nom. pl.","they"],["brahma-lokeṣu","m. loc. pl.","in the Brahman-worlds"],["parānta-kāle","m. loc. sg. tp.","at the supreme-end-time (= time of dissolution, mahā-pralaya)"],["para-amṛtāḥ","m. nom. pl. bv.","(having become) supremely-immortal"],["parimucyanti","3 pl. pres. mid. of pari-muc","(they all) become liberated"],["sarve","m. nom. pl.","all"]],"lit":"\"Those whose aim is well-determined by Vedānta-knowledge, strivers purified-of-sattva through the yoga of renunciation — they, supremely-immortal, are all liberated in the Brahman-worlds at the supreme-end-time (the cosmic dissolution).\"","para":"The verse describes the krama-mukti — the gradual / step-by-step liberation — of the second-tier seekers. Those who are not full jñānins (the saṃprāpyāḥ of 3.2.5) but are vedānta-vijñāna-suniścita-arthāḥ (those whose aim is firmly fixed by Vedānta-knowledge), who through saṃnyāsa-yoga (the yoga of renunciation, not just sāṅkhya-jñāna) are śuddha-sattva (of pure sattva) — these reach the brahma-loka and at the time of cosmic dissolution (parānta-kāla — when the whole brahma-loka itself dissolves) become parāmṛta (supremely immortal) and all together (sarve) achieve liberation. This is the canonical Vedāntic doctrine of krama-mukti: the next-best seekers attain liberation not directly but gradually, through residing in brahma-loka until the cosmic dissolution and then merging with the para-Brahman.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> The verse is the textual basis for the doctrine of krama-mukti, a major topic in Vedānta-sūtra 4.3 and 4.4. Two grades of mukti: (a) sadyo-mukti — immediate liberation, attained by the perfect jñānin (the seer of 3.2.5); (b) krama-mukti — gradual liberation, attained by those whose realisation is not yet complete at death. The krama-mukta goes after death to the brahma-loka (the highest cosmic realm), dwells there until the cosmic dissolution (parānta-kāla), and at that dissolution merges with the para-Brahman. Compare Brahma-Sūtra 4.4.22 (kāryaṃ tyādhikāraṇa) which establishes this doctrine. Śaṅkara's reading of the Muṇḍaka-verse: vedānta-vijñāna-suniścita-arthāḥ — those whose aim is fixed by Vedānta-knowledge; they have the knowledge but it is not yet matured to immediate realisation. saṃnyāsa-yoga — they have additionally renounced. śuddha-sattva — their sattva is purified. These three together qualify for krama-mukti."],["Sivananda","The verse is a great comfort to seekers who feel that they have not yet attained full realisation. The Upaniṣad recognises a second grade — krama-mukti — for those who have done the foundational work but have not crossed to direct realisation. They are not lost; their work is preserved; they reach brahma-loka and eventually merge with Brahman. Sivananda's encouragement: even if you do not reach sadyo-mukti, your sustained sādhanā guarantees at least krama-mukti."],["Krishnananda","vedānta-vijñāna-suniścita-arthāḥ — \"those whose aim is well-determined by Vedānta-knowledge\" — describes those who have grasped the doctrine but not yet had the cognitive event of realisation. They are firm in the doctrine (suniścita), they live by it (saṃnyāsa-yoga), they have purified themselves (śuddha-sattva). They lack only the breakthrough. They will receive the breakthrough at the cosmic dissolution."],["Deussen","This is the principal textual basis of the krama-mukti doctrine. Deussen notes that the Vedānta-sūtras dedicate substantial portions of book 4 to elaborating this verse: 4.3 to the path of the karma-mukti, 4.4 to the state of the krama-mukta in brahma-loka and the eventual merging. The verse is therefore one of the most exegetically productive in the Upaniṣadic corpus."],["Aurobindo","The verse establishes that liberation is not a binary state. There are gradations. The perfect jñānin is liberated immediately; the imperfect knower whose work is yet incomplete is liberated gradually. Both reach the same end. The cosmic structure accommodates the seeker's actual condition at death. Aurobindo treats this as evidence that the Vedāntic cosmos is graciously structured: no preparation is wasted; every level of attainment finds its appropriate consummation."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 3.2.7","title":"At dissolution: the fifteen parts gather to their bases, the gods to their deities, the karmas and the knowing-Self all become one in the supreme Imperishable","sa":["गताः कलाः पञ्चदश प्रतिष्ठा देवाश्च सर्वे प्रतिदेवतासु ।","कर्माणि विज्ञानमयश्च आत्मा परेऽव्यये सर्वे एकीभवन्ति ॥ ७॥"],"iast":["gatāḥ kalāḥ pañca-daśa pratiṣṭhā devāś ca sarve prati-devatāsu |","karmāṇi vijñāna-mayaś ca ātmā pare'vyaye sarve ekī-bhavanti || 7 ||"],"words":[["gatāḥ","f. nom. pl. pp. of gam","(have) gone"],["kalāḥ","f. nom. pl.","the parts"],["pañca-daśa","indecl. num.","fifteen"],["pratiṣṭhāḥ","f. nom. pl.","(to their) bases"],["devāḥ ca sarve","m. nom. pl.","and all the (sense-)gods"],["prati-devatāsu","f. loc. pl.","(to) their (corresponding) deities"],["karmāṇi","n. nom. pl.","the karmas"],["vijñāna-mayaḥ","m. nom. sg. adj.","the knowing"],["ca","indecl.","and"],["ātmā","m. nom. sg.","Self"],["pare","m. loc. sg.","in the supreme"],["avyaye","m. loc. sg.","imperishable"],["sarve","m. nom. pl.","all"],["ekī-bhavanti","3 pl. pres. of eko-bhū","become one"]],"lit":"\"The fifteen parts have gone to their bases; all the (sense-)gods to their (corresponding) deities; the karmas and the knowing Self — all become one in the supreme Imperishable.\"","para":"The dissolution-process of the realised one at death. Three things dissolve: (a) the pañca-daśa kalāḥ — the fifteen parts (the same fifteen of Praśna 6.4: the five jñānendriyas, the five karmendriyas, the five prāṇas — or, in another reckoning, the sixteen kalās of Praśna minus the puruṣa) — they return to their pratiṣṭhā (bases — the elements from which they were composed); (b) the sense-gods (the adhidaivika presiding-deities of the senses) return to their respective deities; (c) the karmas (the karmic deposits of the realised one) and his vijñāna-maya-ātmā (knowing-Self) — all become one (ekī-bhavanti) in the supreme Imperishable (pare avyaye). The realised one at death does not transfer to another world; he dissolves component-by-component, with the Self merging into the para-akṣara.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> The verse describes the videha-mukti (post-bodily liberation) of the realised one. Compare Praśna 6.4 (the sixteen kalās of the Puruṣa). The Muṇḍaka counts fifteen here (excluding the Puruṣa, who does not dissolve), or sixteen including the kalā that is the Puruṣa. The dissolution is three-fold: the elemental components return to elements (the body to its mahā-bhūtas), the sensory components return to their cosmic deities (sight to the sun, hearing to the directions, etc.), and the karmic-cum-cognitive component merges with the para-akṣara. The vijñāna-maya-ātmā — \"the knowing Self\" — is the technical term for the jīvātman in its cognitive aspect. At videha-mukti, this jīvātman dissolves into para-Brahman; the dissolution is total. The Muṇḍaka's account is more compressed than the Bṛhad-Āraṇyaka's parallel (BĀU 3.2 — Yājñavalkya's account of death) but doctrinally identical."],["Sivananda","The verse describes what happens at the death of the realised: the components disperse, each returning to its source. This is the precise reverse of the cosmogony of 2.1 (the Puruṣa emits the components); at videha-mukti, the components reverse and merge back. Sivananda's gloss: \"death of the realised is cosmogony reversed.\""],["Krishnananda","ekī-bhavanti — \"they become one\" — is the verse's most precise teaching. At dissolution, all the previously-distinct components become one — not merged into the Self (which would imply they retain identity within the Self) but ekī-bhū (become one with). The grammar is precise: not adjective + bhū (\"become equal\") but the verbal compound ekī-bhū (\"become-into-one\"), which signifies dissolution into identity."],["Deussen","pañca-daśa kalāḥ — the fifteen parts — is the same count as Praśna 6.4 (where the count is sixteen including the Puruṣa). The variation is editorial; both Upaniṣads describe the same set of components. The verse is the Muṇḍaka's most precise version of the kalā-dissolution doctrine."],["Aurobindo","The verse is, for Aurobindo, the textual basis of his teaching that videha-mukti is not annihilation but integration. The components do not disappear; they return to their sources, while the cognitive-karmic Self merges with the para-akṣara. The realised one is therefore not destroyed at death; he is distributed back to his cosmic sources, with the central conscious element ekī-bhū with the para-Brahman."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 3.2.8","title":"As rivers flowing reach the ocean and disappear into it, abandoning name and form, so the knower abandoning name and form reaches the supreme Person","sa":["यथा नद्यः स्यन्दमानाः समुद्रे-ऽस्तं गच्छन्ति नामरूपे विहाय ।","तथा विद्वान् नामरूपाद्विमुक्तः परात्परं पुरुषमुपैति दिव्यम् ॥ ८॥"],"iast":["yathā nadyaḥ syandamānāḥ samudre 'staṃ gacchanti nāma-rūpe vihāya |","tathā vidvān nāma-rūpād vimuktaḥ parāt paraṃ puruṣam upaiti divyam || 8 ||"],"words":[["yathā","indecl.","as"],["nadyaḥ","f. nom. pl.","the rivers"],["syandamānāḥ","f. nom. pl. pres. part. mid.","(while) flowing"],["samudre","m. loc. sg.","in the ocean"],["astam gacchanti","indecl. + 3 pl. pres.","go-to-setting (= are lost, disappear)"],["nāma-rūpe","n. acc. du. dv.","name-and-form"],["vihāya","ger. of vi-hā","having abandoned"],["tathā","indecl.","so"],["vidvān","m. nom. sg.","the knower"],["nāma-rūpāt","n. abl. sg. dv.","from name-and-form"],["vimuktaḥ","m. nom. sg. pp.","(being) released"],["parāt param","m. acc. sg.","(the) higher-than-the-higher"],["puruṣam","m. acc. sg.","Person"],["upaiti","3 sg. pres. of upa-i","reaches / approaches"],["divyam","m. acc. sg.","divine"]],"lit":"\"As rivers, flowing, set into the ocean, abandoning name-and-form, so the knower, released from name-and-form, reaches the divine Person — higher than the higher.\"","para":"One of the most quoted Upaniṣadic similes for liberation. The rivers (nadyaḥ), while still flowing (syandamānāḥ), set (astaṃ gacchanti — the same idiom used for the sun's setting) into the ocean (samudre) — i.e., they cease to exist as rivers; they abandon their nāma (their names — Yamunā, Gaṅgā, Sarasvatī, etc.) and their rūpa (their separate forms — bed, banks, current). In the same way, the vidvān (the knower) released from nāma-rūpa attains the divine Puruṣa, parāt param (higher than the higher). The river-into-ocean simile complements 2.1.1's spark-from-fire: there, beings emerge from the akṣara as sparks from fire; here, the seer dissolves into the Puruṣa as rivers into ocean. Together the two similes describe the complete cycle: emanation and return.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> The simile is the canonical Upaniṣadic image of videha-mukti. The river continues flowing right up until it reaches the ocean (syandamānāḥ — present participle, the action is continuous); only at the ocean's edge does it abandon its name-and-form. Similarly, the realised one continues his life-activity (kriyāvān of 3.1.4) right up to the moment of death; only at death does he abandon his nāma-rūpa and merge with the para-puruṣa. The simile therefore precisely captures the integration of jīvanmukti (continued life-activity) and videha-mukti (post-death dissolution): the same agent does both, in temporal sequence. Note also: the river's flowing all the way to the ocean shows that life is not interrupted by realisation; the realised continues, exactly as before, until natural dissolution. Compare Chāndogya 6.10.1 (the same river-ocean simile but with a different emphasis — there, the rivers do not return after merging) and BĀU 2.4.12 (the lump-of-salt simile, which captures the same dissolution)."],["Sivananda","The river-ocean simile is the standard teaching-image for advanced seekers. The river-life does not have to end early; it flows all the way to the ocean. The seeker does not have to renounce life prematurely; he continues until natural death and dissolution. Sivananda's gloss to lifelong householders: \"your river continues; do not artificially divert it. It will reach the ocean of its own accord when the time comes.\""],["Krishnananda","nāma-rūpāt vimuktaḥ — \"released from name-and-form\" — is the most precise definition of mokṣa in Vedānta. Bondage is precisely the identification with nāma-rūpa (the individuality of one's name and embodied form); release is precisely the freedom from this identification. Krishnananda's standard formulation: \"the river does not lose its waters in the ocean; it loses only its name-and-form. The waters are now ocean-waters. So the realised one does not lose his being; he loses only his name-and-form. His being is now Brahman-being.\""],["Deussen","The river-ocean simile is the most-cited Upaniṣadic image in later Vedānta. It captures perfectly the doctrine of identity-with-difference: the rivers were always water (continuity), but their separateness was nāma-rūpa, now abandoned (release). Compare BG 11.28 (the same simile, used differently — the warriors rush toward Kṛṣṇa's mouth as rivers rush to the ocean)."],["Aurobindo","parāt param puruṣam — \"the Person higher than the higher\" — is the verse's most precise designation of the destination. \"Higher than the higher\" is not just a comparative; it is the technical term for the para-Puruṣa beyond the para-Brahman of less-comprehensive teachings. Aurobindo treats this as the Muṇḍaka's most precise pre-formulation of his own uttama-Puruṣa doctrine: the divine Person who is beyond the kṣara-puruṣa (the perishable, embodied person) and the akṣara-puruṣa (the imperishable, witness person). The river reaches not just the ocean of Brahman but the divine Puruṣa beyond."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 3.2.9","title":"The Brahman-knower becomes Brahman; no non-knower is born in his line; he crosses sorrow, crosses sin, becomes deathless","sa":["स यो ह वै तत्परमं ब्रह्म वेद ब्रह्मैव भवति नास्याब्रह्मवित्कुले भवति ।","तरति शोकं तरति पाप्मानं गुहाग्रन्थिभ्यो विमुक्तोऽमृतो भवति ॥ ९॥"],"iast":["sa yo ha vai tat paramaṃ brahma veda brahmaiva bhavati nāsyābrahma-vit kule bhavati |","tarati śokaṃ tarati pāpmānaṃ guhā-granthibhyo vimukto'mṛto bhavati || 9 ||"],"words":[["saḥ","m. nom. sg.","he"],["yaḥ","m. nom. sg. rel.","who"],["ha vai","indecl.","verily"],["tat paramam","n. acc. sg.","that supreme"],["brahma","n. acc. sg.","Brahman"],["veda","3 sg. pf. of vid","knows"],["brahma eva","n. nom. sg.","Brahman alone"],["bhavati","3 sg. pres. of bhū","becomes"],["na asya","m. gen. sg.","not of his"],["a-brahma-vit","m. nom. sg.","(any) non-Brahman-knower"],["kule","n. loc. sg.","in the family"],["bhavati","3 sg. pres.","is born"],["tarati","3 sg. pres. of tṝ","crosses over"],["śokam","m. acc. sg.","sorrow"],["tarati","3 sg. pres.","crosses"],["pāpmānam","m. acc. sg.","sin"],["guhā-granthibhyaḥ","m. abl. pl. tp.","from the heart-knots"],["vimuktaḥ","m. nom. sg. pp.","(being) released"],["a-mṛtaḥ","m. nom. sg.","deathless"],["bhavati","3 sg. pres.","becomes"]],"lit":"\"He who indeed knows that supreme Brahman becomes Brahman itself; no non-Brahman-knower is (ever again) born in his family. He crosses sorrow, crosses sin; released from the heart-knots, he becomes deathless.\"","para":"The classic mantra of brahma-vid brahma-bhavati. He who knows the supreme Brahman becomes Brahman. From his family no non-Brahman-knower is (henceforth) born — the realisation propagates through the lineage. He crosses over sorrow (śoka), crosses over sin (pāpman), is released from the heart-knots (guhā-granthi, plural — the various inner bondages), and becomes deathless (amṛta). The verse compresses the entire soteriological teaching of the Muṇḍaka into a single śloka.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> The opening line brahma-vid brahma-bhavati is the most-cited identity-statement in Advaita Vedānta. Knowing Brahman is becoming Brahman: the knowing is the becoming, not a route to a separate becoming. The clause nāsyābrahma-vit kule bhavati — \"no non-knower is born in his family\" — admits two readings: (a) literal: realisation propagates by spiritual inheritance through the family; (b) doctrinal: the very category of \"family\" is transformed for the realised — his \"family\" is now the community of brahma-knowers, in which no non-knower can be. Śaṅkara prefers the literal reading: the spiritual force of a realised ancestor influences the family for generations. The three crossings (śoka, pāpman, granthi) are the canonical inventory of release: emotional (sorrow), moral (sin), and structural (the knots) — all three are crossed simultaneously upon realisation. amṛto bhavati — \"becomes deathless\" — is the result: not goes-to-an-immortal-place but becomes-the-immortal-itself."],["Sivananda","This is the Upaniṣad's most-quoted single verse for transmission-purposes. Sivananda recommends reciting it daily as the closing of one's morning sādhanā: brahma-vid brahma-bhavati — \"the knower of Brahman becomes Brahman.\" The verse is itself a kind of self-fulfilling cognition: to recite it with conviction is to begin to become what it names."],["Krishnananda","guhā-granthibhyaḥ vimuktaḥ — \"released from the heart-knots\" — is plural (granthibhyaḥ — abl. pl.). There are multiple heart-knots: the knot of body-identification, the knot of ego-affirmation, the knot of craving, the knot of aversion. All are released simultaneously upon realisation. Compare 2.1.10 (singular avidyā-granthi) and 2.2.8 (singular hṛdaya-granthi); the plural here suggests a more articulated picture of the multiple knots that constitute the single complex."],["Deussen","brahma-vid brahma-bhavati is verbally identical to Taittirīya 2.1.1 (brahma-vid āpnoti param — \"the Brahman-knower attains the supreme\") and BĀU 4.4.6 (brahmaiva san brahmāpyeti — \"being Brahman, he reaches Brahman\"). The Muṇḍaka's formulation is the most compressed and most epigrammatic."],["Aurobindo","brahma-vid brahma-bhavati is for Aurobindo the most concentrated statement of the Upaniṣadic doctrine of identity-realisation. The knowing and the becoming are not two steps; they are one act seen under two aspects. To know Brahman is already to be Brahman; the knowing is the being. Aurobindo's gloss: \"there is no path between knowing Brahman and being Brahman; the path was an illusion of separation, and the knowing dissolves it.\""]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 3.2.10","title":"This is taught by the Ṛc: only to those active, śrotriya, brahma-niṣṭha, who themselves offer to the One Ṛṣi, who have completed the head-vow","sa":["तदेतदृचाऽभ्युक्तम् ।","क्रियावन्तः श्रोत्रिया ब्रह्मनिष्ठाः स्वयं जुह्वत एकर्षिं श्रद्धयन्तः ।","तेषामेवैतां ब्रह्मविद्यां वदेत शिरोव्रतं विधिवद्यैस्तु चीर्णम् ॥ १०॥"],"iast":["tad etad ṛcābhyuktam |","kriyāvantaḥ śrotriyā brahma-niṣṭhāḥ svayaṃ juhvata ekarṣiṃ śraddhayantaḥ |","teṣām evaitāṃ brahma-vidyāṃ vadeta śiro-vrataṃ vidhi-vad yais tu cīrṇam || 10 ||"],"words":[["tat etat","n. nom. sg.","this"],["ṛcā","f. instr. sg.","by an Ṛc"],["abhyuktam","n. nom. sg. pp.","is spoken (about)"],["kriyā-vantaḥ","m. nom. pl.","(those) active (in rites)"],["śrotriyāḥ","m. nom. pl.","Veda-learned"],["brahma-niṣṭhāḥ","m. nom. pl. tp.","established in Brahman"],["svayam","indecl.","themselves"],["juhvate","3 pl. pres. mid. of hu","offer"],["ekarṣim","m. acc. sg.","to the One-Seer (= Ekarṣi, name of a fire)"],["śraddhayantaḥ","m. nom. pl. pres. part. denom. of śraddhā","(while) having faith"],["teṣām eva","m. gen. pl.","to those alone"],["etām","f. acc. sg.","this"],["brahma-vidyām","f. acc. sg.","knowledge of Brahman"],["vadeta","3 sg. pot. of vad","let one tell"],["śiraḥ-vratam","n. acc. sg. tp.","the head-vow"],["vidhi-vat","indecl. adv.","according to rule"],["yaiḥ tu","m. instr. pl.","by whom"],["cīrṇam","n. nom. sg. pp.","(has been) performed"]],"lit":"\"This is what is spoken (about it) by the Ṛc: 'Those active, Veda-learned, established in Brahman, who themselves offer (the homa) to the Ekarṣi-fire with faith — to those alone may one tell this knowledge of Brahman, (those) by whom the head-vow has been performed according to rule.'\"","para":"The penultimate verse sets the conditions for transmission. Citing an Ṛc — i.e., a verse from the Atharva-Veda Saṃhitā here treated as the corroborating authority — the Upaniṣad lists five qualifications for the eligible pupil: (a) kriyāvantaḥ (those active in rites — not pure jñānins to the exclusion of karma); (b) śrotriyāḥ (Veda-learned); (c) brahma-niṣṭhāḥ (established in Brahman); (d) svayam juhvate ekarṣim śraddhayantaḥ (those who themselves perform the homa to the Ekarṣi-fire with faith — the Ekarṣi is the Atharva-Veda's own sacred fire); (e) śiro-vrataṃ vidhi-vat yais tu cīrṇam (those by whom the head-vow has been performed according to rule). To these alone the brahmavidyā may be transmitted. The Upaniṣad is here explicitly Atharvan in restricting its teaching to Atharvan-school initiates who have performed the muṇḍana / śiro-vrata.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> The verse establishes the formal eligibility (adhikāra) for receiving brahmavidyā. The five qualifications are the Muṇḍaka's most precise statement of who may be taught. Note the specifically Atharvan element: the Ekarṣi-fire is the Atharvan school's own ritual fire (the term means \"the one-seer\" or \"the lone seer\"), and the śiro-vrata is the Atharvan-specific head-vow (muṇḍana). The Upaniṣad therefore restricts its teaching to Atharvan-school initiates. Śaṅkara's exegesis: the qualifications are not arbitrary but pedagogically necessary. kriyāvantaḥ ensures the pupil has karmic discipline; śrotriya ensures textual competence; brahma-niṣṭha ensures the pupil already has some inner anchoring in Brahman; svayam juhvata ekarṣim ensures the pupil's daily ritual life is intact; śiro-vrata-cīrṇa is the Atharvan-specific qualification. The verse's restrictive force is the Muṇḍaka's textual basis for the doctrine of adhikāra: not everyone may receive this teaching. Śaṅkara emphasises that the restriction is for the pupil's protection — premature transmission would not be efficacious and could be confusing."],["Sivananda","The verse instructs the teacher, not the pupil. A teacher may only transmit brahmavidyā to those who meet these qualifications. Sivananda emphasises: do not freely give what cannot be received. The five-fold qualification is the teacher's check-list. If the pupil does not have these, the teacher must first help the pupil acquire them; the brahmavidyā transmission itself must wait."],["Krishnananda","The verse is the Muṇḍaka's most precise restriction-clause. It is the textual basis for the doctrine that brahmavidyā is technical knowledge requiring formal preparation, not popular wisdom available to all. The Muṇḍaka is not democratic in transmission; it is gradually exclusive. The seeker must first earn the qualifications before being told the doctrine."],["Deussen","The qualifications are exclusively Atharvan in character. The Ekarṣi-fire and the śiro-vrata are Atharvan-specific. The Upaniṣad is therefore intra-school: it transmits brahmavidyā within the Atharvan tradition, not across schools. Deussen treats this as evidence that the Upaniṣad was originally a closed-school text and only later became widely diffused. The wider Vedāntic tradition has largely ignored these qualifications and treated the Muṇḍaka's brahmavidyā as universally transmissible — but the verse itself is restrictive."],["Aurobindo","kriyāvantaḥ (active in rites) is the first qualification — and the most paradoxical, given that 1.2.7–10 critiqued ritualism. The resolution: the criticised ritualism is the ritualism-treated-as-final; the praised ritualism is the ritualism-as-preparation. The pupil who is to receive brahmavidyā must have done his ritual work (not as final, but as preparation). Aurobindo treats this as the verse establishing that the karma-kāṇḍa is not abolished but is the foundation for the jñāna-kāṇḍa; both are needed in sequence."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 3.2.11","title":"Aṅgiras the ṛṣi spoke this truth of old; one who has not performed the (head-)vow does not study (it). Salutation to the supreme seers, salutation to the supreme seers.","sa":["तदेतत्सत्यमृषिरङ्गिराः पुरोवाच नैतदचीर्णव्रतोऽधीते ।","नमः परमऋषिभ्यो नमः परमऋषिभ्यः ॥ ११॥"],"iast":["tad etat satyam ṛṣir aṅgirāḥ purovāca naitad a-cīrṇa-vrato'dhīte |","namaḥ parama-ṛṣibhyo namaḥ parama-ṛṣibhyaḥ || 11 ||"],"words":[["tat etat","n. nom. sg.","this"],["satyam","n. nom. sg.","(is) the truth"],["ṛṣiḥ aṅgirāḥ","m. nom. sg.","the rṣi Aṅgiras"],["puro-uvāca","3 sg. pf. of pra-vac (with purā)","spoke of old"],["na etat","n. acc. sg.","this (Upaniṣad)"],["a-cīrṇa-vrataḥ","m. nom. sg. bv.","(one whose) vow is not performed"],["adhīte","3 sg. pres. mid. of adhi-i","studies / recites"],["namaḥ","indecl.","salutation"],["parama-ṛṣibhyaḥ","m. dat. pl.","to the supreme seers"],["namaḥ","indecl.","salutation"],["parama-ṛṣibhyaḥ","m. dat. pl.","to the supreme seers"]],"lit":"\"This is the truth — (which) the rṣi Aṅgiras spoke of old. One whose (head-)vow has not been performed does not study (= recite) this. Salutation to the supreme seers! Salutation to the supreme seers!\"","para":"The closing colophon. The Upaniṣad re-attributes its teaching to the original rṣi Aṅgiras (named in 1.1.2 as the receiver of the parāvarā brahmavidyā). It reaffirms the restriction of 3.2.10: an a-cīrṇa-vrata — one whose head-vow has not been performed — does not recite this. The Upaniṣad closes with a doubled salutation to the supreme seers (parama-ṛṣibhyaḥ namaḥ — doubled, as is standard in Atharvan colophons). The doubling marks the verse as the canonical closure.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> The Upaniṣad closes by returning to its opening: 1.1.2 named Aṅgiras as the recipient of the parāvarā brahmavidyā; 3.2.11 names Aṅgiras as the original speaker. The frame closes. The qualifying clause naitad a-cīrṇa-vrato'dhīte — \"one whose vow has not been performed does not study this\" — is the formal restriction-clause that the tradition has, in practice, applied: the Upaniṣad is recited in Atharvan-school contexts only. The doubled salutation namaḥ parama-ṛṣibhyaḥ namaḥ parama-ṛṣibhyaḥ is the standard Atharvan colophon-closure."],["Sivananda","The closing colophon is the Upaniṣad's last word on adhikāra: this text is for those who have done the preparatory work. Sivananda gracefully extends the modern sense of \"head-vow\" to include the broader discipline of inner head-restraint — keeping the head (= mind) under vow (= disciplined). Anyone who has not undertaken inner mental discipline does not really study the Muṇḍaka, even if he reads its words."],["Krishnananda","The closing namaḥ parama-ṛṣibhyaḥ is the Upaniṣad's acknowledgement that the brahmavidyā transmitted in it does not originate with any one author. It is the inheritance of the parama-ṛṣis — the supreme seers — the lineage running back to Brahmā via Atharvan, Aṅgir, Bhāradvāja Satyavāha, and Aṅgiras. The salutation is to all of them. Krishnananda treats this as the verse establishing the cumulative-tradition character of brahmavidyā: no individual realised it alone; the lineage realised it together and transmits it through each member."],["Deussen","The doubled namaḥ closing is standard Atharvan colophon-style. Compare Praśna 6.8 (which also closes with a doubling, in that case namo namaḥ). The Atharvan Upaniṣads share these colophon-conventions."],["Aurobindo","The Upaniṣad closes by re-asserting its essential restrictiveness — and by saluting the lineage of supreme seers. The double salutation is the verse's gesture of gratitude: realisation is not an individual achievement but a lineage-inheritance, and the salutation acknowledges this. Aurobindo's gloss to the modern seeker: \"the supreme seers are not gone; they are still present in the lineage. The salutation is therefore not retrospective but present.\""]],"notes":["End of khaṇḍa 3.2 and end of the Muṇḍaka Upaniṣad."]},{"ref":"Visual Representations","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["Seven ASCII diagrams of the Upaniṣad's distinctive geometries. Each diagram illustrates one of the Muṇḍaka's signature images. Read with the corresponding mantra at hand."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Diagram 1","title":"The parā / aparā two-tier (1.1.4–5)","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["Aparā vidyā = the four Vedas + six Vedāṅgas (all enumerable, all śabda); parā vidyā = that-by-which-the-akṣara-is-reached (not enumerable, defined only relationally).","<pre>\n                         AKṢARA (the Indestructible)\n                                  ▲\n                                  │  ← parā vidyā (&quot;by which it is reached&quot;)\n                                  │     defined only by its result; not enumerable\n                                  │\n        ┌─────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────┐\n        │                                                   │\n        ▼                                                   ▼\n   APARĀ VIDYĀ (1.1.5)                            (Result of parā: 1.1.6)\n   ────────────────                               ────────────────\n   Four Vedas:                                    a-dreśya (invisible)\n     · Ṛgveda                                     a-grāhya (ungraspable)\n     · Yajurveda                                  a-gotra  (familyless)\n     · Sāmaveda                                   a-varṇa  (colourless/casteless)\n     · Atharvaveda                                a-cakṣuḥ-śrotram\n   Six Aṅgas:                                     a-pāṇi-pādam\n     · Śikṣā (phonetics)                          nitya   (eternal)\n     · Kalpa (ritual)                             vibhu   (pervading)\n     · Vyākaraṇa (grammar)                        sarva-gata (everywhere)\n     · Nirukta (etymology)                        su-sūkṣma (subtle)\n     · Chandas (metrics)                          avyaya  (imperishable)\n     · Jyotiṣa (astronomy)                        BHŪTA-YONI (womb of beings)</pre>","Reading: aparā is full to enumerate, the parā only to indicate by negation-and-result. The two-tier is the doctrinal core of the Muṇḍaka. Note that the four Vedas themselves fall under aparā — the most radical claim of the verse."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Diagram 2","title":"Cosmogonic triad: spider, plants, hair (1.1.7) → fire-sparks (2.1.1)","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["Four similes mounting in intimacy. Spider = purposive emanation-and-reabsorption. Plants = spontaneous emergence. Hair = effortless growth. Fire-sparks = same-substance multiplicity.","<pre>\n   (1.1.7)                             (2.1.1)\n   ───────────────                     ───────────────────\n                                                ___\n   ┌──spider──┐                              .-&#x27; ✦ &#x27;-.    sparks\n   │   ⊙      │  weaves out                /  ✦  ✦   \\   sahasraśaḥ\n   │  /│\\     │  → and draws back         |   ✦ ✦ ✦   |  prabhavante\n   │ thread  │     thread                  \\   ✦  ✦  /   sa-rūpāḥ\n   └─────────┘                              &#x27;-._ ✦ _.&#x27;\n                                                 ▼\n                                              [FIRE]\n   ╔════════════╗                            su-dīpta pāvakāt\n   ║   ‹|›|›|›  ║ plants                       (akṣara)\n   ║ ░░ EARTH ░░║ rise spontaneously\n   ╚════════════╝\n                                          AKṢARA emits beings\n   ╭─💆–────╮                             as fire emits sparks:\n   │ )))))) │   hair grows                same-substance,\n   │ (puruṣa)│   without willing           briefly distinct,\n   ╰─────────╯                             returning to source.\n   ─ FROM AKṢARA → THE COSMOS IS EMITTED ────────────────────\n   (purposive · spontaneous · effortless · same-substance)</pre>","The four images together establish abhinna-nimitta-upādāna: the akṣara is both efficient and material cause. Spider models emanation-and-return; plants model spontaneity; hair models effortlessness; sparks model same-substance multiplicity."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Diagram 3","title":"Praṇava-dhanus meditation (2.2.3–4)","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["Oṃ = bow; ātman = arrow; Brahman = target. The arrow becomes the target's substance (śaravat tan-mayo bhavet).","<pre>\n            (bow drawn back; mind in tad-bhāva)\n                       │\n                       │\n        ╔══OṂ══╗◁ ─ ─ ─┼─ ─ ─ ─ ─ ─ ─ ─ ─ ─ ─ ▷ ☉ BRAHMAN\n        ║ \\         ──▶ÁTMAN▶ ──────────────────▶  (lakṣya, akṣara)\n        ║  )praṇava-dhanus    (śara = self)\n        ║ /                    sharpened by\n        ╚══════╝               upāsana\n\n              (1)               (2)              (3)\n         take up bow      fit &amp; sharpen      draw, un-heedless,\n         (gṛhītvā          arrow              loose\n         aupaniṣadam)      (sandhayīta)       (vyadh / viddhi)\n\n   ── After piercing ──────────────────────────────────────────\n   The arrow does not return. It becomes the target&#x27;s substance:\n   śara-vat tan-mayo bhavet — &quot;like an arrow, of-That-substance&quot;.\n   Mokṣa is therefore identification, not arrival.</pre>","Note: the meditation is directional and penetrative, not diffuse — distinguishing the Muṇḍaka from receptivity-traditions. Aurobindo treats this as one of two valid spiritual movements: active (bow-arrow) and receptive (listening)."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Diagram 4","title":"Dvā suparṇā: two birds on one tree (3.1.1)","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["Two birds, friends, on the same tree. One eats sweet pippala; the other watches without eating. The eater = jīva (saṃsāric self); the watcher = paramātman (witness Lord). Same tree (the body / saṃsāra); same substance; two functions.","<pre>\n                          ┌──────────────┐\n                          │  ╱╲╱╲╱╲╱╲    │   ← samānaṃ vṛkṣam\n                          │ ╱  WHOLE  ╲  │     (the body / the world /\n                          │ ╲  TREE   ╱  │      saṃsāra)\n                          │  ╲ pippala ╱ │\n                          │   ╲ (fruit)╱ │       sayujā sakhāyā\n                          │    ╲      ╱  │       (yoked, friends)\n                          │     │ ◓◑ │   │\n   anyo abhicākaśīti ──▶  │     │ ◐◓ │  ◀── tayor anyaḥ\n   (&quot;watches&quot;)            │     │   ●│        pippalaṃ\n   ANA-AŚNAN              │     │   ●│        svādv atti\n   ── watches without     │     │   ●│        (&quot;the one eats\n       eating ────────────│     │   ●│         the sweet fruit&quot;)\n                          │     ╲ ╱  │\n   PARAMĀTMAN             │      └    │            JĪVA\n   (witness, at ease)     │           │            (eater, suffers)\n                          └───────────┘\n   Resolution (3.1.2):\n      The jīva, helpless, sorrowful — sees the watcher as juṣṭa-īśa\n      (the worshipped Lord) and his greatness, and becomes vīta-śoka\n      (free of sorrow). The two were always one in substance;\n      the seeing recognises what was already so.</pre>","This is the most famous Upaniṣadic figure for witness-consciousness. Shared verbatim with Śvetāśvatara 4.6 and Ṛgveda 1.164.20; only the Muṇḍaka resolves the riddle (in 3.1.2). The Atharvan resolution: the eater, recognising the watcher as a worshipped Lord, is freed."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Diagram 5","title":"The river-into-ocean simile of mokṣa (3.2.8)","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["Rivers flow continuously (syandamānāḥ) all the way to the ocean. Only at the ocean's edge do they astaṃ gacchanti (\"set\") — abandoning name-and-form. The realised one continues life-activity (kriyāvān) right up to natural dissolution; only at death are nāma-rūpa abandoned.","<pre>\n   Sources                Flowing                       Sea\n   ───────                ───────                       ───\n                                                ░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░\n   ⛰ Himavat            Gaṅgā ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━╲ ░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░\n       ║                                       ╲░░  SAMUDRA   ░░\n       ║                Yamunā ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━╲ ░░ (the One) ░░\n       ║                                       ╲░░░░░░░░░░░░░░\n       ╠══════▶ Sarasvatī (sub-stream) ─ ─ ─ ─ ╲░░░ astaṃ ░░░░\n       ║                                       ╱░░  gacchanti ░\n       ║                Godāvarī ━━━━━━━━━━━━━╱ ░░░ &quot;go to ░░░░\n       ║                                       ╱░░  setting&quot; ░░\n       ╚════════════════ Kāverī ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━╱ ░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░\n                                                ░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░\n       NĀMA-RŪPA:      each river has               at the ocean:\n     · its own name     name &amp; form                 NĀMA-RŪPA\n     · its own form     (Gaṅgā, Yamunā, ...)        VIHĀYA\n     · its own course   distinct &amp; flowing          (abandoned)\n\n   Doctrinal point:\n   ─ The river loses only its name-and-form; its water is now\n     ocean-water. So the realised one loses only his name-and-form;\n     his being is now Brahman-being.\n   ─ The flowing continues (syandamānāḥ — present participle) all\n     the way to the ocean. Realisation does not interrupt life;\n     it allows life to continue until natural dissolution.</pre>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Diagram 6","title":"The hub of the heart: spokes converging (2.2.6)","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["As spokes converge at the chariot-hub (ratha-nābhi), so all the subtle channels (nāḍyaḥ) converge at the heart-centre. The Self moves within them, born in many ways. Meditate on the Self as Oṃ.","<pre>\n                          ↑ udāna-nāḍī (throat → crown)\n                          │\n                        ◁─┼─▷\n                       ◁  │  ▷\n                      ◁   │   ▷\n                     ◁    │    ▷\n   ◀───────────────◀ ◀────●────▶ ▶───────────────▶\n   (lateral nāḍī)              ← HRDAYA-CAKRA →    (lateral nāḍī)\n                     ▷    │    ◁\n                      ▷   │   ◁\n                       ▷  │  ◁\n                        ▷─┼─◁\n                          │\n                          ↓ apāna-nāḍī (pelvis)\n                          \n   Reading:\n   · The hub (●) = the heart-cavity (hṛdaya-guhā).\n   · The spokes = the bodily channels (nāḍyaḥ).\n   · The Self (ātman) sits at the hub, &quot;moving within, born in\n     many ways&quot; (bahudhā jāyamānaḥ): as seeing in the eye,\n     hearing in the ear, breath in the lungs, etc.\n   · Meditate as Oṃ: gather all the spoke-attention back to\n     the hub. svasti vaḥ pārāya tamasaḥ parastāt — &quot;may you\n     cross beyond the darkness.&quot;</pre>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Diagram 7","title":"The four-fold sādhanā of 3.1.5 leading to the four-fold result of mokṣa","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["The four means (satya, tapas, samyag-jñāna, brahmacarya) operate together (nityam — continuously) → the four-fold result: heart-knot split, doubts severed, karmas exhausted, supreme sāmya attained.","<pre>\n            FOUR-FOLD MEANS                          FOUR-FOLD RESULT\n            (3.1.5: nityam continuously)             (2.2.8 + 3.1.3)\n            ───────────────────────────              ──────────────────────────\n            ┌─────────────────────┐                  ┌────────────────────────┐\n            │  satya              │                  │ bhidyate hṛdaya-       │\n            │  (truth-being)      │  ────────▶       │ granthiḥ               │\n            │                     │                  │ (heart-knot is split)  │\n            └─────────┬───────────┘                  └────────────────────────┘\n                      │                                          ▲\n            ┌─────────┴───────────┐                  ┌───────────┴────────────┐\n            │  tapas              │                  │ chidyante sarva-       │\n            │  (concentrated      │  ────────▶       │ saṃśayāḥ               │\n            │   discipline)       │                  │ (doubts severed)       │\n            └─────────┬───────────┘                  └────────────────────────┘\n                      │                                          ▲\n            ┌─────────┴───────────┐                  ┌───────────┴────────────┐\n            │  samyag-jñāna       │                  │ kṣīyante karmāṇi       │\n            │  (discriminative    │  ────────▶       │ (karmas exhausted —    │\n            │   cognition)        │                  │  saṃcita + āgāmi)      │\n            └─────────┬───────────┘                  └────────────────────────┘\n                      │                                          ▲\n            ┌─────────┴───────────┐                  ┌───────────┴────────────┐\n            │  brahmacarya        │                  │ paramaṃ sāmyam upaiti  │\n            │  (continuous Veda-  │  ────────▶       │ (supreme equality /    │\n            │   discipline)       │                  │  identity attained)    │\n            └─────────────────────┘                  └────────────────────────┘\n                                                          \n            ▶ Catalyst (3.1.8): jñāna-prasāda + viśuddha-sattva → seeing.\n            ▶ Final triggering (3.2.3): yam evaiṣa vṛṇute tena labhyaḥ\n              — &quot;by whom alone (the Self) chooses, by him it is attained&quot;.</pre>","Note: the four means are necessary but not sufficient. The triggering moment is the Self's choosing (3.2.3) — the seeker prepares; the Self does the choosing. The recognition is simultaneous: knowing Brahman = being Brahman (3.2.9)."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Practical Steps and Observations","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["Eight practice-oriented sections extracting the operational teaching of the Upaniṣad. Each section can stand as a daily contemplation; together they form a complete Muṇḍaka sādhanā."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"1. Distinguishing parā from aparā in your study","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["The Muṇḍaka's first practical instruction is epistemological. Every text you read, every teaching you receive, can be approached in one of two modes: as aparā (information to be absorbed) or as parā (access-route to the akṣara). The same text — even the Muṇḍaka itself — can be read in either mode.","Aparā-mode signs: you finish a reading session feeling that you know more than before; you can recall and recite the content; you can argue against opposing positions; the reading produces a sense of intellectual accomplishment.","Parā-mode signs: you finish a reading session feeling less rather than more; you cannot summarise it because what was disclosed is not summary-able; the discriminations you used before reading have been undone; the reading produces a sense of dissolution rather than accomplishment.","Daily practice: when sitting to read any spiritual text, before opening it, set the explicit intention: \"I read this as parā, not aparā.\" Notice during the reading whether the intention holds. When it slips into aparā-mode (collecting points, comparing positions), gently re-establish parā-mode by pausing and re-reading the last passage with attention to what-points-to-the-akṣara rather than what-the-words-mean."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"2. The eighteenfold check: examining your aparā at its strongest","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["Mantras 1.2.1–6 give a precise inventory of correct ritual life: the new-moon offering, the full-moon, the cāturmāsya, the new-crop offering, the guest-offering, the daily āhuti, the vaiśvadeva, all according to procedure. The Upaniṣad does not mock this inventory; it takes it seriously and only then says it is a raft, not a bridge.","Practice: identify your own equivalent of this inventory. What is your aparā at its most disciplined? For some seekers it is daily meditation, journaling, scripture-recitation, dietary discipline. For others it is professional excellence, family service, charitable giving. List your aparā-disciplines.","Then ask: am I treating these as variṣṭha (the highest)? If they are real disciplines well executed, you will have arrived at \"the back of heaven\" — a high cosmic position — and at the end of the cosmic age you will descend. This is the Muṇḍaka's specific diagnosis (1.2.9–10) — not punishment, but exhaustion.","The shift from aparā to parā is not to abandon the disciplines but to recognise their level. Continue them — they are Puruṣa-emanated (2.1.6 explicitly grants this). But do not treat them as the highest."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"3. Nirveda","title":"the dispassion of recognition (1.2.12)","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["The Upaniṣad specifies the precise emotional precondition for approaching a teacher: nirveda. This is not despair, not contempt for the world, not exhausted disillusionment. It is the calm cognitive recognition that nāsty a-kṛtaḥ kṛtena — \"there is no eternal (to be attained) by means of the impermanent.\" The recognition is calm because it is true; the calmness is the mark of the recognition's genuineness.","Test for nirveda: when you contemplate the possibility that your most ambitious life-projects, if fully successful, would still leave the akṣara unreached — does this thought disturb you, or does it merely clarify your priorities? Disturbance indicates that you are still treating worldly success as final; clarity indicates that nirveda has begun.","Nirveda is not produced by external loss or trauma. It is produced by parīkṣya lokān karma-citān — \"examining the karma-built worlds\" — the careful intellectual examination of what is gainable by karma. When the examination is thorough, nirveda follows automatically. It cannot be willed."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"4. The samit-pāṇi gesture: how to approach a teacher","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["Mantra 1.2.12 prescribes the formal mode of teacher-approach: gurum eva abhigacchet samit-pāṇiḥ — \"approach a teacher, with fuel-stick in hand.\" The samit is symbolic: the student brings his own fuel — his own conditioned mind — as offering. The fuel is not a gift to the teacher; it is the student's own self brought as offering.","Modern equivalent: when approaching any teaching situation — a lecture, a study group, a one-on-one with a guru, a serious book — bring your conditioned mind explicitly as offering. Make this explicit by sitting silent for ten breaths before the encounter and inwardly saying: \"This is my mind as it is now. I offer it to be worked on. I will not defend it from what comes.\"","The samit-pāṇi disposition is incompatible with the modern stance of \"let me see what they say and evaluate.\" Evaluation comes later. The samit-pāṇi moment is offering-without-evaluation. Restore this moment in your approach to teaching; without it the brahmavidyā does not transmit."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"5. Praṇava-dhanus meditation: a sitting practice (2.2.3–4)","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["The Muṇḍaka's most concrete meditation instruction. Sit in any stable posture. Close the eyes. Spend two minutes settling the breath into slow, even rhythm.","Phase 1 — taking up the bow: silently invoke the Upaniṣadic teaching as a whole. Recognise that you are not improvising; you are using a precise instrument that the tradition has built. Let yourself feel \"held\" by the tradition's accumulated work. This is the bow in your hand.","Phase 2 — fitting the arrow: gather attention to a single point. Most teachers locate this at the heart-centre (anāhata, in tantric vocabulary), though it can equally be located at the bhrūmadhya or even at no fixed location. The location matters less than the singularity. Withdraw scattered attention to one point. This is the arrow on the string.","Phase 3 — sharpening: the arrow on the string is not yet sharp. Repeated meditation makes it sharp. Stay in single-pointed attention for at least ten minutes — long enough for the attention to deepen past surface-distractibility. This is upāsana-niśita.","Phase 4 — drawing back: the mind already absorbed in That-state (tad-bhāva-gata cetas) draws the bow. This is the inner gathering just before the cognition. You may sense a deepening, a quieting, a sense of being at the edge.","Phase 5 — un-heedless loosing: with apramatta (un-heedless) attention, release. The release is not an act of will — it is the natural completion of the prepared sequence. Do not try to force the cognition; let the sequence complete itself.","After the practice: do not analyse what happened. Sit in silence for a further five minutes. The arrow does not return — śaravat tan-mayo bhavet — \"like an arrow, (you) become of-That-substance.\" The seeker who insists on returning to analyse has interrupted the becoming."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"6. The two-birds discipline: living from the watcher","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["Mantra 3.1.1–2 establishes the structural truth that there are two functions of consciousness in every life: the eating one (jīva, agent and experiencer) and the watching one (paramātman, witness). They are not two selves but two functions of one consciousness.","Practice through the day: whenever you notice yourself in distress, identify the function you are operating from. Are you operating as the eater (immersed in the experience, identified with the agent who suffers) or as the watcher (witnessing the experience, not identified with it)? Most distress is from over-identification with the eater.","The corrective is not to suppress the eater — the bird continues to eat the pippala — but to shift identity-centre to the watcher. Both birds remain on the tree; you simply locate yourself in the second one.","Aurobindo's specification: the shift is identification, not abandonment. The eater continues eating (the body continues to act and experience); but \"you\" are now the watcher. The body's experience is observed without being your experience. Practice this shift in moments of small distress until it can be applied in moments of large distress.","Test of competence: in your next moment of acute disappointment, can you locate the watcher within ten seconds? If not, the discipline has not yet matured. Continue practising in lower-stakes situations."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"7. Satyam eva jayate as daily principle","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["Mantra 3.1.6 — \"truth alone triumphs, not untruth\" — works as both moral injunction and cosmic principle. The moral injunction: live by truth in every transaction. The cosmic principle: only the True is real; what falsifies eventually exhausts itself; only the True endures.","Daily practice: at every choice-point where truth and convenience pull in different directions, choose truth. Note that this is not a maximalist position — sometimes diplomatic silence is required; sometimes a question does not warrant an answer; sometimes the truth is more than the asker can receive. But within these qualifications: choose truth over falsity.","Observation over years: those who consistently choose truth find their lives gradually simplifying. The complexity of maintaining multiple positions falls away. Energy formerly spent in narrative-management becomes available for other work. The cosmic claim of the verse is also a psychological one: untruth costs more than truth.","Cosmic principle as anchor: in situations where speaking the truth seems risky, recall the cosmic principle: nānṛtam — \"not untruth\" — does not triumph. Whatever advantage falsity seems to gain is temporary. The verse is a guarantee, not just an injunction."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"8. nāma-rūpa-vimukti","title":"the river-mind practice (3.2.8)","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["The simile of rivers flowing into the ocean is also a contemplation. The rivers flow continuously (syandamānāḥ) all the way to the ocean — they do not stop flowing in anticipation of arriving. Only at the ocean's edge do they astaṃ gacchanti — abandon name-and-form.","The teaching for daily life: do not artificially abandon your nāma-rūpa (your role, your name, your form, your individual life-shape) before natural dissolution. Continue flowing. Continue performing your specific role. The river that tries to abandon its riverhood before reaching the ocean does not become the ocean; it just becomes a dried-up channel.","Practice: when tempted to spiritual escape — quitting jobs, abandoning families, withdrawing from responsibilities in the name of liberation — apply the river-test. Is this nāma-rūpa being abandoned because it has reached the ocean, or in advance of the ocean? If the latter, return to the river-life. The ocean meets the river that has flowed all the way to it.","The complementary teaching: when natural dissolution begins (the body declining, the social roles falling away in old age), do not cling to nāma-rūpa. Let it dissolve in its proper time. The seeker who clings to identity in the moment of its natural dissolution prolongs the river-life past its natural end.","The Muṇḍaka's life-arc: full participation in life (kriyāvān of 3.1.4) until natural dissolution (river-into-ocean of 3.2.8). Neither premature escape nor terminal clinging."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Cross-references in the Upaniṣadic Corpus","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["The Muṇḍaka draws on and is drawn from a wide range of Vedic and Upaniṣadic sources. The table below tracks the principal intertextual relations: verses the Muṇḍaka quotes, verses that quote the Muṇḍaka, and verses that are clearly parallel-developments of a shared source.","<table><tr><td>Locus</td><td>Parallel / quoted text</td><td>Relation</td></tr><tr><td>1.1.1 (śānti-pāṭha)</td><td>Praśna 1 (same śānti) · Māṇḍūkya 1 (same śānti) · AV 19.71.1</td><td>Shared Atharva-Veda peace-mantra; identical wording in all three Upaniṣads.</td></tr><tr><td>1.1.4 (parā / aparā)</td><td>Chāndogya 6.1.3 (kena tu khalu) · BĀU 4.5.7 (Maitreyī catechism) · BG 13.7–11 (jñāna / ajñāna)</td><td>Doctrinal parallel; MuU sharpens the distinction into a formal classification of two vidyās.</td></tr><tr><td>1.1.7 (spider-thread)</td><td>BĀU 2.1.20 (Ajātaśatru: \"as a spider issues forth its thread\") · ŚU 6.10 (the same image)</td><td>Shared cosmogonic simile. BĀU is the apparent source; MuU and ŚU borrow.</td></tr><tr><td>1.2.7 (plavāḥ adṛḍhāḥ — \"unstable rafts\")</td><td>BG 9.21 (\"having enjoyed the heaven-world, they return to the mortal world\") · KaU 1.2.6</td><td>Anti-ritualist polemic. MuU's image is sharpest; BG's softens it slightly.</td></tr><tr><td>1.2.8 (\"deluded, deeming themselves wise; blind led by blind\")</td><td>Kaṭha 1.2.5 (verbally close: avidyāyām antare vartamānāḥ…)</td><td>Almost verbatim. MuU is the apparent source; KaU borrows.</td></tr><tr><td>1.2.10 (iṣṭā-pūrta)</td><td>Chāndogya 5.10 (the path of fathers) · BĀU 6.2.15 (the two paths)</td><td>Shared technical category from Brāhmaṇa-period mīmāṃsā.</td></tr><tr><td>1.2.12 (samit-pāṇiḥ śrotriyaṃ brahma-niṣṭham)</td><td>Praśna 1 (the six approach Pippalāda) · Chāndogya 4.4 (Satyakāma to Hāridrumata Gautama) · Brahma-Sūtra 1.1.1 (athāto brahma-jijñāsā: cited as proof-text by Śaṅkara)</td><td>The canonical Vedāntic guru-approach. MuU is the locus classicus, cited by every major ācārya.</td></tr><tr><td>2.1.1 (sparks from fire)</td><td>BĀU 2.1.20 (\"as from a fire well-kindled, sparks issue forth in their thousands\")</td><td>MuU compresses the BĀU image into a single śloka.</td></tr><tr><td>2.1.4 (cosmic-body Puruṣa)</td><td>Ṛgveda 10.90 (Puruṣa-sūkta) · ŚU 3.16 (\"hands and feet everywhere\")</td><td>MuU is the Atharvan compression of the ṚV Puruṣa-sūkta. Notably omits the four-varṇa derivation of ṚV 10.90.12.</td></tr><tr><td>2.1.5 (pañcāgni-vidyā compressed)</td><td>Chāndogya 5.4–10 (the full pañcāgni-vidyā) · BĀU 6.2 (the same)</td><td>MuU compresses the seven-khaṇḍa CU treatment into four lines.</td></tr><tr><td>2.2.2 (\"smaller than the small … luminous\")</td><td>Kaṭha 1.2.20 (aṇor aṇīyān mahato mahīyān) · ŚU 3.20 (the same image)</td><td>Doctrinal parallel; the formula is common across schools.</td></tr><tr><td>2.2.4 (praṇava-dhanus)</td><td>Praśna 5.5 (Oṃ as means of reaching Brahman) · Māṇḍūkya 1 (Oṃ as everything)</td><td>MuU contributes the specific bow-arrow architecture; other Upaniṣads use Oṃ differently.</td></tr><tr><td>2.2.6 (chariot-hub of channels)</td><td>Praśna 6.6 (sixteen kalās converging at the puruṣa) · BĀU 2.5.15 · KaU 6.16–17 (the 101 channels of the heart)</td><td>Shared subtle-anatomy image. MuU's version is the most general; KaU's the most anatomically precise.</td></tr><tr><td>2.2.10 (\"there the sun shines not\")</td><td>Kaṭha 2.2.15 (verbatim) · ŚU 6.14 (verbatim) · BG 15.6 (paraphrased)</td><td>Verse appears in three Upaniṣads with identical wording. MuU is the probable source; KaU and ŚU borrow.</td></tr><tr><td>2.2.11 (\"Brahman alone is before, behind…\")</td><td>Chāndogya 7.25.2 (Sanatkumāra: \"this Self is below, above, in front…\") · Bhagavad-Gītā 13.13</td><td>Doctrinal parallel; MuU compresses the CU six-directional formula.</td></tr><tr><td>3.1.1 (dvā suparṇā)</td><td>Śvetāśvatara 4.6 (verbatim) · Ṛgveda 1.164.20 (the original)</td><td>The Vedic original is ṚV 1.164.20 (Dīrghatamas hymn, riddle-form, no resolution). MuU and ŚU borrow with differing resolutions. See parallel-column table at 3.1.1 above.</td></tr><tr><td>3.1.4 (ātma-krīḍa ātma-rati kriyāvān)</td><td>Chāndogya 7.25.2 (identical triplet)</td><td>Identical phrase; one borrows from the other or both quote a common source.</td></tr><tr><td>3.1.6 (satyam eva jayate)</td><td>(no direct parallel; widely cited later: Indian Republic national emblem, 1950)</td><td>Unique to the Muṇḍaka. The most-quoted half-mantra in modern India.</td></tr><tr><td>3.1.8 (\"not by eye, not by speech…\")</td><td>Kena 1.3–4 (\"there the eye goes not, neither speech nor mind\") · KaU 2.3.12</td><td>Doctrinal parallel; the apophatic formula is common across schools.</td></tr><tr><td>3.2.3 (nāyam ātmā pravacanena labhyo)</td><td>Kaṭha 1.2.23 (verbatim, including the closing tasyaiṣa ātmā vivṛṇute tanūṃ svām)</td><td>Either MuU borrows from KaU or both quote a common source. Verbally identical.</td></tr><tr><td>3.2.6 (krama-mukti)</td><td>Brahma-Sūtra 4.3 + 4.4 (extended elaboration of krama-mukti) · BĀU 4.4.5–6</td><td>MuU is the principal textual basis of the krama-mukti doctrine in later Vedānta.</td></tr><tr><td>3.2.7 (fifteen kalās at dissolution)</td><td>Praśna 6.4 (sixteen kalās, including the Puruṣa)</td><td>MuU counts 15 (excluding the Puruṣa); Praśna counts 16 (including). Same doctrine, different reckonings.</td></tr><tr><td>3.2.8 (rivers into ocean)</td><td>Chāndogya 6.10.1 (\"as these rivers, flowing east and west, going to the ocean, become merely ocean…\") · BG 11.28 (warriors rushing as rivers into Kṛṣṇa) · BĀU 2.4.12 (the lump-of-salt simile, structurally parallel)</td><td>MuU's version is the most often cited in later Advaita literature; CU is the apparent source.</td></tr><tr><td>3.2.9 (brahma-vid brahma-bhavati)</td><td>Taittirīya 2.1.1 (brahma-vid āpnoti param) · BĀU 4.4.6 (brahmaiva san brahmāpyeti)</td><td>Identity-formula attested in three Upaniṣads; MuU's wording is the most epigrammatic.</td></tr><tr><td>3.2.10 (śiro-vrata)</td><td>(no Upaniṣadic parallel; the head-vow is Atharvan-school-specific)</td><td>Unique to the Muṇḍaka and its Atharvan context.</td></tr></table>","Note on direction of borrowing: where the Muṇḍaka shares verbal material with the Kaṭha (1.2.8 / KaU 1.2.5; 2.2.10 / KaU 2.2.15; 3.2.3 / KaU 1.2.23), the direction is disputed. Deussen leans toward MuU as the source; some modern scholars (e.g., Olivelle 1998) reverse this. The verbatim agreements may also reflect a common Atharvan-Kāṭhaka oral tradition older than either Upaniṣad. Where MuU shares material with the Bṛhad-Āraṇyaka (1.1.7 spider-thread, 2.1.1 sparks-from-fire), the BĀU is consistently the earlier source."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Bibliography","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["The selections below are those actually drawn upon in the commentary digests above, organised by category. Sanskrit editions first, then English translations and commentaries, then secondary scholarship."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Sanskrit text","title":"primary editions","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["Hattangadi, Sunder. Muṇḍakopaniṣat. sanskritdocuments.org ITRANS file, doc_upanishhat/mundaka.itx. The convenient digital source; based on the Adyar critical edition.","Limaye, V. P., and R. D. Vadekar. Eighteen Principal Upaniṣads, Volume 1. Poona: Vaidika Saṃśodhana Maṇḍala, 1958. Critical Sanskrit edition with Śaṅkara-bhāṣya variants.","Olivelle, Patrick. The Early Upaniṣads: Annotated Text and Translation. New York: Oxford University Press, 1998. Includes a critical Sanskrit text of the Muṇḍaka with apparatus.","Bhattacharyya, Dipak. The Paippalāda-Saṃhitā of the Atharvaveda. Calcutta: Asiatic Society, 1997. The principal published witness to the Paippalāda recension, against which the Śaunaka readings used in this volume may be compared."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Translations and commentaries (the five drawn upon)","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[["Sivananda, Swami. The Principal Upanishads. Rishikesh","Divine Life Society, 1937; many reprints. Includes Sivananda's gloss on the Muṇḍaka; supplemented by his lecture-series The Mundakopanishad (Rishikesh, mimeographed, dates uncertain)."],["Krishnananda, Swami. The Mundaka Upanishad. Rishikesh","Divine Life Society, 1980s lecture series, published posthumously; available at swami-krishnananda.org."],["Deussen, Paul. Sechzig Upaniṣad's des Veda. Leipzig","F. A. Brockhaus, 1897. English translation: Sixty Upaniṣads of the Veda, tr. V. M. Bedekar and G. B. Palsule, 2 vols., Delhi: Motilal Banarsidass, 1980."],["Aurobindo, Sri. The Upaniṣads I","Īśā, Kena, Muṇḍaka with Commentaries and Translations. Pondicherry: Sri Aurobindo Ashram Trust, 1972 (originally serialised in Ārya, 1916–20); reprinted as Collected Works of Sri Aurobindo (CWSA) Volume 17, 2001."]],"notes":["Gambhirananda, Swami. Eight Upaniṣads, with the Commentary of Śaṅkarācārya, Volume 2 (Aitareya, Muṇḍaka, Māṇḍūkya, Praśna). Kolkata: Advaita Ashrama, 1958; many reprints. The carrier of Śaṅkara's Muṇḍaka-bhāṣya in English. Cited herein for every Śaṅkara digest."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Other consulted translations","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["Hume, Robert Ernest. The Thirteen Principal Upanishads. Oxford University Press, 1921; 2nd edition revised 1931. The first scholarly English translation of the Muṇḍaka. Useful for comparing pre-Aurobindo Anglophone interpretation.","Radhakrishnan, S. The Principal Upaniṣads. London: George Allen &amp; Unwin, 1953. Includes the Muṇḍaka with extensive cross-referencing.","Olivelle, Patrick. Upaniṣads. Oxford World's Classics, 1996; New York: OUP, 1998 (critical edition). Modern philological translation with substantial notes.","Sharma, Arvind. The Experiential Dimension of Advaita Vedanta. Delhi: Motilal Banarsidass, 1993. Includes detailed analysis of the Muṇḍaka's epistemology of brahmavidyā."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Secondary scholarship","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[["Deussen, Paul. Die Philosophie der Upanishads. Leipzig","F. A. Brockhaus, 1899. English translation The Philosophy of the Upanishads, tr. A. S. Geden, Edinburgh: T. &amp; T. Clark, 1906. The classical scholarly synthesis of Upaniṣadic doctrine."]],"notes":["Belvalkar, S. K. and R. D. Ranade. History of Indian Philosophy, Volume 2: The Creative Period. Poona: Bilvakuñja Publishing House, 1927. Detailed treatment of the Muṇḍaka within the principal-Upaniṣad corpus.","Ranade, R. D. A Constructive Survey of Upanishadic Philosophy. Poona: Oriental Book Agency, 1926. Especially valuable for the Muṇḍaka's epistemology.","Mahadevan, T. M. P. The Philosophy of Advaita. London: Luzac, 1938; 4th edition Delhi: Munshiram Manoharlal, 1976. Treats the Muṇḍaka as central to the Advaita position on knowledge.","Pereira, José. Hindu Theology: A Reader. Garden City: Image Books, 1976. Includes annotated extracts from the Muṇḍaka in the context of comparative Vedānta.","Cohen, Signe (ed.). The Upaniṣads: A Complete Guide. London: Routledge, 2018. Recent collaborative scholarly volume with chapters on the Muṇḍaka.","Olivelle, Patrick. \"Dharmaskandhāḥ and Brahmasaṃsthaḥ: A Study of Chāndogya Upaniṣad 2.23.1.\" Journal of the American Oriental Society 116 (1996): 205–219. Useful for adjacent doctrinal background."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"On the śiro-vrata and Atharvan-school context","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["Bloomfield, Maurice. The Atharvaveda. Strassburg: Karl J. Trübner, 1899. Standard handbook on the Atharva-Veda including its Upaniṣads.","Whitney, William Dwight. Atharva-Veda Saṃhitā, 2 vols. Cambridge MA: Harvard Oriental Series, 1905. Critical text and commentary on the Atharva-Veda Saṃhitā.","Gonda, Jan. The Ritual Sūtras. Wiesbaden: Otto Harrassowitz, 1977. Includes treatment of the Atharvan ritual context that informs Muṇḍaka 3.2.10.","Kuiper, F. B. J. \"The Bliss of Aśa.\" Indo-Iranian Journal 8 (1964): 96–129. Indispensable on the satya-concept of 3.1.6 in its Vedic-Avestan background."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Cross-Upaniṣadic studies","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["Frauwallner, Erich. History of Indian Philosophy, Volume 1: The Philosophy of the Veda and the Epic; The Buddha and the Jina; The Sāṃkhya and the Classical Yoga System. Tr. V. M. Bedekar. Delhi: Motilal Banarsidass, 1973 (German original 1953). Important for the Muṇḍaka's place in the development of pre-classical Indian philosophy.","Cohen, Signe. Text and Authority in the Older Upaniṣads. Leiden: Brill, 2008. Treats the Muṇḍaka's authority-claims (the five-generation paraṃparā, the śiro-vrata restriction) in comparison with other Upaniṣads.","Black, Brian. The Character of the Self in Ancient India: Priests, Kings, and Women in the Early Upaniṣads. Albany: SUNY Press, 2007. Useful for the social context of Muṇḍaka 1.1.3 (Śaunaka the mahāśāla).","--- end of Mundaka_Upanishad.docx ---"],"kind":"note"}]}]};
